evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:344:{"type":"assistant/chunk","seq":8019,"time":1789026759244,"data":{"turn":1,"step":5,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":0,"block":{"type":"reasoning","text":"Now I have a solid understanding. Let me also quickly check the web-client.js workmate UI section (lines 120-380) and the sidebar-tab test + team-member binding (memberPersona/`memberPersona` uses member.name == workmate key).\n\nLet me check the GUI section and the test setup to size the work precisely.\n\nActually, I should be efficient. I have enough for the DAG design. But I want to be sure about:\n1. How the GUI wires (fetch-based, POST routes) — need rename/delete routes + UI.\n2. Whether there's an existing `memberPersona` binding that needs the workmate name — yes, member name == workmate dir key. Rename means existing team members bound to the old name lose backing. Need documentation/semantics decision. Also possibly update team member names? Probably: rename only affects the library; existing team members keep old name (documented), OR we could offer an optional cascade. That's a requirements question.\n3. Delete semantics: hard delete vs archive. Since workmates hold evolved memory, a hard delete should require explicit confirm; maybe a recycle/trash dir. That's a requirements question for the user.\n\nThis is exactly what the requirements conference is for. I need to hold a joint requirements session with the user AND the agents. However — practical constraint: members only spawn after approval. So the requirements conference with AGENTS present can't literally happen with spawned teammates during staging. The protocol says: \"before the team writes any task DAG, hold a joint requirements session with the user AND the AGENTS present\". Members can be staged and I can ask them questions only after they run. Hmm. But the instructions say the deliverable is a requirements summary agreed with the user, which becomes the input to the staged plan.\n\nPractical approach: I do the reconnaissance myself (done), then ask the user key requirements questions now (before finalizing the DAG), so the staged plan encodes agreed requirements. I can also include a requirements task in the DAG (kind=requirements) as the first node, and shape the plan so implementation tasks depend on it. The conference with \"AGENTS present\" — while staged, members can't run. So the pragmatic reading: I collect the questions (including ones a specialist would raise — I can consult the read-only specialists via mpd_role_spawn one-shot BEFORE staging a DAG, since those are separate from the team). Actually that's a good move: use mpd_role_spawn (Architect/Plan Reviewer) to get requirement questions, then batch them to the user with ask_user_question.\n\nBut careful: the protocol says don't spawn members during staging (agent_teams), but mpd_role_spawn is a one-shot specialist consult, a different mechanism. The instruction says \"For multi-member team work prefer the adopted dsh-agent-teams protocol, not repeated one-shot spawns\" — but a single consultation for the requirements conference is reasonable and explicitly sanctioned by the REQUIREMENTS CONFERENCE rule (Architect/Planner/Plan Reviewer). Hmm, it costs time. Let me weigh: I already have deep grounding. I think a lightweight approach is better: do the recon myself (done), ask the user the 3-4 genuinely open product questions, then build the DAG. I'll include a requirements task in the DAG assigned to a read-only specialist (Plan Reviewer or Planner) that verifies requirements agreement before implementation — actually the reviewers in mpd profile: Architect, Researcher, Planner, Explorer, Plan Reviewer, Vision Analyst are read-only.\n\nWait — but there's a nuance: is a requirements task necessary? The user's request is crisp: 1) rename workmate, 2) delete workmate. Scope: also traverse the project to understand details (that's recon — I'm doing it, and could delegate a deeper \"full project traversal\" research task so the team genuinely covers it).\n\nGiven the goal includes \"遍历工程，了解工程详细信息\" (traverse the project, understand detailed info) — that's a research/requirements task: produce a grounded map of the workmate system + affected surfaces. That's a real task for the Researcher/Explorer.\n\nLet me decide the DAG (quality-mode-ish):\n\n1. **T1 requirements** (Researcher or Planner, read-only, kind=requirements): Full grounded traversal — workmate system end-to-end: plugin source, service surface, web routes, GUI tab, locale dictionaries, agent-teams member binding, tests, docs (AGENTS.md/README bilingual), evidence conventions. Plus verdict=pass requirements contract for rename+delete: exact acceptance criteria, invariants (path safety, sanitize, index consistency, home isolation in QA), edge cases (rename to existing name, rename to same name, delete non-existent, delete with active team member binding, name collisions case-insensitivity, path traversal via `..`). Deliverable: `docs/` note? Careful — docs are bilingual human-facing. Maybe put the requirements summary in the task output + a workspace file under a QA/process path... Hmm, minimal diffs principle. Perhaps put it into `evidence/workmate/<slug>/<timestamp>/` as evidence. Actually a requirements doc is process artifact — fine in evidence dir.\n\nBetter: keep it as task output (structured), no extra file sprawl. But acceptance requires on-disk evidence for gates.\n\n2. **T2 implementation — core plugin** (Senior Engineer): implement rename + delete in `packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/src/index.ts`: new tools `mpd_workmate_rename`, `mpd_workmate_delete`; service methods; web routes `/plugins/mpd-workmate/rename`, `/plugins/mpd-workmate/delete`; index.json consistency; path safety; rebuild dist.\n   inScope: packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/**\n   verify: `bun test packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/test/workmate.test.ts`, `bun run typecheck`, rebuild dist.\n\n3. **T3 implementation — GUI** (Junior Engineer or Senior Engineer): workmate sidebar tab rename/delete affordances + locale dictionaries zh/en + rebuild client.js (`node scripts/build-mpd-client.mjs`), keep the sidebar-only design (no fallback) and seam policy.\n   inScope: packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/src/web-client.js, packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/client.js, test files.\n   Depends on T2 (needs route contract) — genuine prerequisite (route names/response shapes).\n\nHmm — could they run in parallel? T3 needs the route contract. But if the contract is FIXED by T1 (requirements defines exact route names + payloads), T2 and T3 could go parallel. Yet with the GUI depending on routes' exact JSON, and risk of divergence, a dependency T3←T2 is a genuine prerequisite-ish. To maximize parallelism while keeping correctness, I'll define exact route/response contracts in T1 (requirements), then let T2 and T3 both depend on T1 and run in PARALLEL. That's the \"independent work should be parallel\" instruction. But risk: if T2's implementation deviates, the GUI breaks. Mitigation: T1 fixes the contract as binding; verification covers both.\n\nHmm, but a serial dependency is safer and gives a real prerequisite. The instruction says \"dependencies are only genuine prerequisites\". The GUI literally needs routes to exist to be tested end-to-end. But unit test of the client can be done against a stub. I think requiring T1 contract + parallel is defensible, but there's real integration risk in a repo with this much GUI fragility (see the sidebar troubleshooting table — the GUI is brittle). I'll make T3 depend on T2. Actually wait — for parallelism/scheduling efficiency... The user cares about a correct result. T2 and T3 are small; the critical path matter less. But the protocol says independent work should be parallel and dependencies only genuine prerequisites.\n\nDecision: T2 (core plugin tools/routes) and T3 (client UI) both depend on T1. Rationale: T1 pins the exact wire contract, making them genuinely independent. Hmm, but if T2 and T3 both edit... T2 edits mpd-workmate-plugin, T3 edits mpd-bundle-plugin + client.js. No file overlap → safe parallel.\n\nHmm, but there's a catch: T3's build (`node scripts/build-mpd-client.mjs`) reads web-client.js and writes client.js. Fine.\n\n4. **T4 tests** — who owns? The implementation tasks should include their own tests (TDD). But a dedicated verification task (kind=verification) is needed: an independent member runs the gates and real-case QA. That's T4: verification: run `bun test packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/test/workmate.test.ts`, `bun run typecheck`, `bun run test:qa`, and a real end-to-end check via the actual dsh web profile? Live GUI check may be heavy. At minimum: plugin-level real test with sandbox HOME (test already has a harness), plus dsh QA case with overlay boot (`skills/dsh-qa`).\n\nActually — the dsh-qa skill exists. A real case could boot dsh headless with HOME=sandbox and call the workmate tools... but tools need an LLM session. The existing test file already does a sandbox-HOME lifecycle test with fake ctx/tools. So verification: extend the test to cover rename/delete + run gates + boot check `dsh --profile headless --dump-config` in isolated DSH_HOME (per AGENTS.md §4 gate \"Boot check\").\n\n5. **T5 review** (Reviewer, kind=review, round=1, reviewedTaskId=T2/T3...). A review task can only reference ONE reviewedTaskId. Hmm — the schema says `reviewedTaskId` required for kind=review, singular. So two reviews: one for T2, one for T3? That inflates. Alternatively one review referencing T2 (the core) and T3 covered by... the review task's own description. The system's contract is per-task. I'll create two review tasks? \"the smallest useful task graph\". Two reviews = 2 more members busy. Hmm.\n\nGiven the bundled integrity: rename/delete must work through BOTH surfaces (tools + GUI). I'd rather have ONE review task on the core implementation (T2) whose description explicitly also inspects the GUI delta, plus the verification task T4 covering both. But quality-gate semantics: a review completes only with verdict=pass; needs_revision → auto repair + next review. If the review's reviewedTaskId is T2 and it flags GUI issues, the repair loop is anchored at T2... The repair generated depends on the failed review; source finding ids. It'd work but the repair task's inScope would be T2's scope.\n\nCleaner: make the GUI task part of the reviewed implementation chain: T3 depends on T2 but review T3 as well... Let me just do 2 review tasks — no. Let me reconsider: maybe fold GUI into the SAME implementation task (one implementer does core + GUI), making one review clean. But that reduces parallelism and the GUI is a distinct skill (React-ish client bundle, locale, build script).\n\nAlternative: TWO implementation tasks, and ONE review task per, but assign reviews to the same Reviewer member sequentially (a member cannot own 2 unfinished tasks, so second review waits — fine).\n\nLet me think about what best serves quality:\n- T2 core (tools + routes + plugin tests) — needs review.\n- T3 GUI (client tab + locale + build + client tests) — needs review.\nTwo reviews is honest and the reviewers (Reviewer + Plan Reviewer) are distinct members, so they can run in parallel. That fits the roster: Reviewer (metis) and Plan Reviewer (momus) are both read-only quality roles. Plan Reviewer is described as a plan-review role though; mpd profile says \"Plan Reviewer ... take requirements/review/analysis tasks only\". So Plan Reviewer can review. Good: Reviewer reviews T2, Plan Reviewer reviews T3. Hmm, but is it appropriate for Plan Reviewer to do an implementation review? Profile text says they take requirements/review/analysis tasks. OK.\n\nActually for cleanliness: Reviewer → T2 review (core semantics, safety, index consistency); Plan Reviewer → T3 review (GUI/locale/build contract + seam policy + acceptance vs requirements). Good.\n\n6. **T6 integration** (Lead, kind=integration): after reviews pass — integrate: docs updates (AGENTS.md workmate glossary/troubleshooting rows? README bilingual if user-facing), ensure the bundle row/dist rebuilt, run the full gate sweep, pack check? Integration task depends on both reviews.\n\nAlso docs: `packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/README.md` + `README.zh-CN.md` (bilingual requirement!) must document the new tools, plus AGENTS.md references (glossary/tools list, §6/§13 maybe), and the root README if it lists workmate tools. That's a doc task — put it as part of T2/T3? Bilingual doc rule: \"A change to a human-facing doc updates BOTH versions in the same commit.\" So README.md + README.zh-CN.md for the workmate plugin must be updated. I'll fold that into a dedicated task (T5 docs, depends on T1 contract) or into integration. Better: make it its own task assigned to... a worker. Hmm, \"smallest useful DAG\".\n\nLet me count members needed: read-only (Researcher/Planner for requirements T1; Reviewer for T2-review; Plan Reviewer for T3-review) + workers (Senior Engineer T2, Junior Engineer T3, Deep Worker or Senior Engineer for docs, Lead for integration, Explorer maybe for GUI recon/verification).\n\nLet me now design the final DAG concretely (I'll also add exact route/contract definitions as part of T1's deliverable).\n\nProposed tasks:\n- **T1 [requirements]** — Grounded traversal + requirements contract for workmate rename/delete. Assignee: Planner (prometheus) or Researcher. The goal explicitly asks to traverse the project and understand it; Research is the fit ⇒ **Researcher**. But the requirements contract (acceptance criteria, wire contract) is planning ⇒ **Planner**. Since it's kind=requirements with verdict gate, assign to **Planner** and have it also do the traversal (its persona is an explore-first planning consultant — perfect: \"Explore-first planning consultant (Prometheus) that grounds in the co...\").\n  Deliverable: concise requirement contract + wire protocol + edge cases, verdict=pass.\n  Hmm — do I want a separate traversal deliverable? The user asked \"遍历工程，了解工程详细信息\" — the team should genuinely traverse. Planner's grounded traversal of the workmate system (plugin, routes, GUI, agent-teams binding, tests, docs, evidence conventions) + the affected-surface map = the answer. One task. Plus maybe an **Explorer** task for the broader project map (whole-repo traversal: 24 packages, gates, QA, docs policy) — the user asked to traverse the WHOLE project, not just workmate. That's genuinely independent and parallelizable ⇒ good separate ready task, running in parallel from the start, assigned to **Explorer**, and its output feeds the docs/integration task.\n\nSo:\n- T1 [requirements, Planner]: requirements + wire contract for rename/delete (workmate system deep-dive). Gate: verdict=pass.\n- T2 [work, Explorer]: whole-project traversal dossier (packages, plugins, gates, install/pack flow, QA discipline, docs policy, where workmate sits) → deliverable: structured report. Independent, ready immediately. Might feed T7 docs/integration. Make T7 depend on T2? Not strictly required... but \"later synthesis\" deps are allowed. I'd make the docs task depend on T2 (needs the project map for accurate cross-references) — hmm, docs are about the workmate package README + AGENTS.md rows. T2's map helps. I'll make it depend on T2 (genuine input) — no, careful: adding a dep serializes. AGENTS.md is already known to me. I'll make docs depend on T1 only (needs the tool contract) and let T2 feed the captain's synthesis + integration. Actually integration (T8) can depend on T2 (dossier) as a genuine input. Hmm, that would serialize integration behind the explorer — integration is at the end anyway. Fine, low cost, real input.\n\nLet me simplify to keep it small but complete:\n\n1. **R1 [requirements, Planner]** — Workmate rename/delete: grounded requirements + binding wire contract. verdict gate.\n2. **E1 [work, Explorer]** — Whole-project traversal dossier. (parallel, ready now)\n3. **I1 [implementation, Senior Engineer]** — core plugin: rename + delete tools/service/routes + unit tests + rebuild dist. depends R1. inScope packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/**.\n4. **I2 [implementation, Junior Engineer]** — GUI: rename/delete affordances in workmate sidebar tab + zh/en dictionaries + client rebuild + client test updates. depends R1. inScope packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/** (src/web-client.js, client.js, test/**).\n   (Runs parallel with I1 thanks to the pinned wire contract in R1.)\n5. **V1 [verification, Deep Worker?]** — Independent verification: run plugin tests, client tests, typecheck, test:qa, isolated boot `dsh --profile headless --dump-config` + real sandbox-HOME end-to-end rename/delete through the service/routes; evidence on disk. depends I1, I2.\n   Who? Deep Worker (hephaestus) is a strong executor — verification is a quality kind; assignee could be Senior Engineer, but I1 is Senior Engineer and a member can't own 2 unfinished tasks — fine, sequenced. Better: assign V1 to **Deep Worker** (execution-heavy verification) or **Reviewer**? Reviewer is doing review. Let me assign V1 to Deep Worker.\n6. **RV1 [review, Reviewer]** — review of I1 (core). depends I1.\n7. **RV2 [review, Plan Reviewer]** — review of I2 (GUI + contract conformance). depends I2.\n   RV1/RV2 could depend on V1 (evidence available for the review)? Reviews judge the latest implementation. Giving reviewers the verification evidence helps. But dependency RV1←V1 means review waits for verification which waits for I2. That serializes heavily. Reviewers can run their own checks. Keep RV1 depends I1; RV2 depends I2. V1 independent verification runs in parallel.\n8. **D1 [work, docs]** — bilingual docs: mpd-workmate-plugin README(.zh-CN) + AGENTS.md rows (tools list §1 glossary §13, troubleshooting entries) + any root README tool list. depends R1, and maybe I1/I2 (docs should describe the shipped behavior; describing after implementation avoids doc/impl drift). depends I1 + I2? That serializes docs to the end — which is right for accuracy. But if reviews return needs_revision, docs may need repair too. I'll make D1 depend on I1 and I2 (genuine: doc text must match the shipped tool surface).\n9. **G1 [integration, Lead]** — final integration: gates sweep, bundle boot check, evidence commit-ready summary, verify docs+impl consistency, report. depends V1, RV1, RV2, D1, E1.\n\nMembers: Planner, Explorer, Senior Engineer, Junior Engineer, Deep Worker, Reviewer, Plan Reviewer, Lead, + captain. That's 8 members — mpd profile has 11 (Architect, Researcher, Planner, Explorer, Plan Reviewer, Vision Analyst, Senior Engineer, Junior Engineer, Deep Worker, Lead, Reviewer). With profile create, all 11 are staged automatically. I should NOT recreate members. The profile supplies the roster. So I just design tasks and assign to those member names.\n\nHmm wait — does `agent_teams_create(profile=\"mpd\")` create all 11 members staged? \"Optional profiles expand their configured roster; seed profiles also expand template tasks, while captain profiles leave the graph for the Captain to design.\" mpd is captain-planning → roster only, DAG by me. But the session-start note said a default team \"MPD Default\" (mpd-default-38e78dd5, profile mpd) is ALREADY staged for me, and I may not create a second team while leading this one. So: use the existing staged team! I should inspect its staged roster via agent_teams_status and then add tasks.\n\nThat's important: the plugin already provisioned \"MPD Default\" staged with profile mpd. The slash command says \"Call agent_teams_create with approval=required... Use configured AgentTeams profile mpd\". But the system reminder says a team is already staged and I may not create a second one. Let me check status first — if \"MPD Default\" exists and staged with the roster, I'll use it (rename? no rename tool). The team name \"MPD Default\" is generic. I could keep it; or archive it and create a properly named one (\"mpd\" profile) which aligns with the slash-command instruction. The instruction explicitly says: \"If the default team does not fit this session, archive it with agent_teams_delete first, then create a fresh one with agent_teams_create(approval='required', profile='mpd')\".\n\nDoes \"MPD Default\" fit? It's profile mpd, which is what I need. Its name is generic but names don't matter much... A purpose-named team is nicer: \"Workmate Rename & Delete\". Since the profile matches exactly what I'd create anyway, the cleanest is to reuse the existing staged team (no archive/create churn). The slash-command harness instruction says to call agent_teams_create with profile mpd — but if a staged team already exists, calling create would error (\"may not create a second team\"). I'll check status and decide: I think reusing is right and I'll note it. Hmm, but the explicit slash-command instruction says to call agent_teams_create... The session-start rule takes precedence in practice because creating a second team is disallowed. Let me verify with status.\n\nAlso, the roster: profile mpd already provides all 11 members staged. Then I only add tasks. Let me check status now.\n\nThen: REQUIREMENTS CONFERENCE. I must hold a joint requirements session with the user + agents, using Socratic questioning, and put consolidated questions to the user via ask_user_question. Since members are staged (not running), I can't get their questions by message; but I can consult one read-only specialist one-shot via mpd_role_spawn (Architect or Plan Reviewer) to generate the hard questions — that's the \"agents present\" part, done outside the team's staging. Cost: one subagent call. Value: genuine requirement questions that I might not think of. I think it's worth it — the user's instruction demands the conference, and a Plan Reviewer (momus) persona is exactly the \"adversarial requirements\" role.\n\nLet me do it: spawn Plan Reviewer (momus) one-shot, read-only, to produce a list of requirement questions and edge cases for the workmate rename/delete feature, given a context block with my findings. Run in background while I ask the user my own questions? Careful: I should consolidate questions into ONE batch for the user. So: first get momus's questions (foreground/background), then ask the user the merged batch.\n\nLet me first check team status, then fire the specialist consult in background, then... Actually simplest: run momus consult (background), meanwhile check status and read the GUI code; then batch questions to the user; then build the DAG.\n\nWait — but ask_user_question ends my turn awaiting the answer? Typically the user answers and I continue in the next turn. Then the plan is staged but the user has answered. The protocol wants the Web plan ready at end of turn. Combining: I can ask questions AND stage the plan in the same turn (staging first, then ask). But if the user answers questions in a new turn, I then revise the plan via agent_teams_edit_plan. That's the sanctioned flow (\"the user may return to chat to request changes ... call agent_teams_edit_plan\").\n\nOrder in this turn:\n1. status check (see staged roster)\n2. momus consult (get sharp questions) — must be done before asking the user, so make it foreground-ish (background then collect). Let me run it in background and read GUI code meanwhile, then collect.\n3. Stage the DAG (tasks) — after I know the contract shape. Hmm, if the user's answers change the contract (e.g. delete = archive vs hard delete), tasks change. But I can encode the open decisions in R1 (the requirements task must resolve them with the user's answer) — actually better: I ask the user NOW, then build the DAG from their answers in the SAME turn. Then the plan is directly aligned. And I don't need a clarification round-trip.\n\nBut careful: ask_user_question in the same turn as staging the plan — the answer arrives... Does ask_user_question block within the turn (returning the answer as a tool result) or end the turn? Typically it returns the answer as a tool result so I can continue in the same turn. Let me just try it: ask early, get answers, then stage DAG, then finish turn with \"plan ready\".\n\nHmm, risk: if it ends the turn, then the user answers in the next turn and I stage then. Either way it works.\n\nLet me minimize risk: Ask the user the questions FIRST (as early as possible), then stage. So sequence:\n- status + read GUI (parallel, now)\n- momus consult (need it before asking) — foreground? It could take a while. Alternatively skip momus and derive questions myself... The instructions mandate the requirements conference with agents participating. I'll do one momus consult — it's the strongest fit for \"challenge assumptions, surface edge cases\". Run it with run_in_background default true; then collect with job... no, mpd_role_spawn is a subagent-like tool; its result returns as a notice. Hmm, mpd_role_spawn — does it support background? It has no run_in_background param; it likely returns the result synchronously (one-shot). Let me just call it and see; it should return the specialist's text.\n\nLet me do: [status, read GUI section] in parallel, then momus consult, then ask_user_question, then create tasks.\n\nActually to save time: run status + momus consult in parallel now (independent). Then read GUI while waiting? The consult may return synchronously and consume the turn's time. Fine.\n\nLet me go.\n\nFor the consult context I need the GUI details (locale keys, routes). I'll pass what I know: routes list, tools list, sanitizeName, index.json, agent-teams member binding (member name == workmate key), caps, tests, docs policy.\n\nLet's execute."}}}}
evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:347:{"type":"assistant/chunk","seq":8022,"time":1789026759247,"data":{"turn":1,"step":5,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":3,"block":{"type":"tool-call","id":"call_01_Y7VfKS5e06pIOamVG3ir4466","name":"mpd_role_spawn","arguments":"{\"role\": \"momus\", \"task\": \"You are the Plan Reviewer in a REQUIREMENTS CONFERENCE for a small feature in the my-power-dsh repo (/root/dshProj/my-power-dsh). The user's request (Chinese, verbatim): \\\"遍历工程，了解工程详细信息，并对当前workmate系统做优化：1.可以给workmate改名 2.可以删除workmate\\\" — i.e. (1) allow renaming a workmate, (2) allow deleting a workmate.\\n\\nDo NOT propose the final plan. Your ONE deliverable is an adversarial requirements interrogation: at most 8 SHARP, DECISION-FORCING questions that the captain must put to the USER (product/semantics questions only a human can decide), plus a list of edge cases the implementation MUST handle, plus 2-4 invariants that acceptance criteria must pin. Challenge assumptions, hunt for the real goal, and surface the failure modes a naive \\\"mv dir + rm -rf dir\\\" implementation would create. Be concrete and terse (bullets). No file edits.\\n\\nGROUNDED CONTEXT (measured by the captain — treat as fact, do not re-verify):\\n- Plugin: packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/src/index.ts (439 lines). Library root = $HOME/.mpd/workmate (deliberate cross-project, user-approved HOME exception to the workspace-scoped state rule). One dir per workmate: meta.json (name/baseId/baseName/description/provider/model/readonly/createdAt/updatedAt/uses/lastTask), persona.md (cap 8KiB), memory.md (cap 8KiB, oldest \\\"## \\\" blocks evicted), note.md (cap 1536B, auto-generated short matchable note). Plus a root index.json: { name -> {name, baseId, baseName, uses, updatedAt} }.\\n- sanitizeName(): lowercase, non [a-z0-9_-] -> \\\"-\\\", collapse runs, trim dashes. wmDir(name) = root + sanitizeName(name). Init refuses when the dir exists: `\\\"${name}\\\" already exists`.\\n- Existing tools: mpd_workmate_list / mpd_workmate_init / mpd_workmate_spawn (spawns a one-shot subagent with the workmate persona+memory+note, own provider/model route, readonly bases get toolFilter deny write/edit/apply_patch/mpd_hashline_edit) / mpd_workmate_reflect (append memory, optional persona_delta, regenerate note, bump uses) / mpd_workmate_match (deterministic keyword-overlap scoring, MATCH_THRESHOLD 0.35, weak match => matched=false, caller must init a NEW workmate instead).\\n- A cordis service `mpdWorkmate` is provided: { list, get, read }.\\n- Web routes registered by the plugin (exact paths): GET /plugins/mpd-workmate/list, GET /plugins/mpd-workmate/roster, GET /plugins/mpd-workmate/get?name=, POST /plugins/mpd-workmate/init. GUI = a DSH-better-sidebar tab contributed at client-apply time by packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/src/web-client.js (SIDEBAR_TAB_ID=\\\"mpd-workmate\\\", sidebar-only by design, no floating fallback), with zh/en locale dictionaries (zh is the key-set source of truth) and the built artifact packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/client.js (built by `node scripts/build-mpd-client.mjs`).\\n- CRITICAL CROSS-MODULE BINDING: the adopted agent-teams plugin binds a team member to a workmate by NAME — packages/mpd-agent-teams-plugin/lib/members.js#workmateKey(memberName) sanitizes the MEMBER NAME and, if $HOME/.mpd/workmate/<key> exists, injects that workmate's persona+memory+note into the member persona and instructs the member to call mpd_workmate_reflect after each task. So a member is bound to a workmate purely by name equality; renaming a workmate silently unbinds/rebinds existing team members, and deleting one breaks the binding.\\n- Tests: packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/test/workmate.test.ts (bun test; sandbox HOME; lifecycle init->list->reflect->match, spawn assertions, root-under-HOME assertion). QA convention: evidence/<domain>/<slug>/<timestamp>/{result.json,output.log}; gates: bun test, bun run typecheck, bun run test:qa, node scripts/verify-vendor.mjs, `dsh --profile headless --dump-config` in an isolated DSH_HOME. Docs are BILINGUAL: packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/README.md AND README.zh-CN.md must both be updated in the same change; AGENTS.md is the binding English-only agent manual (it lists workmate tools, glossary entry \\\"workmate\\\", troubleshooting rows).\\n\\nOutput format: (1) USER QUESTIONS — numbered, each with why it matters and the candidate answers; (2) EDGE CASES the implementation must handle; (3) INVARIANTS for acceptance criteria.\", \"context\": \"Repo: /root/dshProj/my-power-dsh (my-power-dsh DeepSeek Harness bundle, AGENTS.md is the binding manual). Feature: workmate rename + delete. Read-only consultation — you are mechanically denied write tools. Return text only.\"}"}}}}
