becomes the input to the staged plan.\n\nPractical approach: I do the reconnaissance myself (done), then ask the user key requirements questions now (before finalizing the DAG), so the staged plan encodes agreed requirements. I can also include a requirements task in the DAG (kind=requirements) as the first node, and shape the plan so implementation tasks depend on it. The conference with \"AGENTS present\" — while staged, members can't run. So the pragmatic reading: I collect the questions (including ones a specialist would raise — I can consult the read-only specialists via mpd_role_spawn one-shot BEFORE staging a DAG, since those are separate from the team). Actually that's a good move: use mpd_role_spawn (Architect/Plan Reviewer) to get requirement questions, then batch them to the user with ask_user_question.\n\nBut careful: the protocol says don't spawn members during staging (agent_teams), but mpd_role_spawn is a one-shot specialist consult, a different mechanism. The instruction says \"For multi-member team work prefer the adopted dsh-agent-teams protocol, not repeated one-shot spawns\" — but a single consultation for the requirements conference is reasonable and explicitly sanctioned by the REQUIREMENTS CONFERENCE rule (Architect/Planner/Plan Reviewer). Hmm, it costs time. Let me weigh: I already have deep grounding. I think a lightweight approach is better: do the recon myself (done), ask the user the 3-4 genuinely open product questions, then build the DAG. I'll include a requirements task in the DAG assigned to a read-only specialist (Plan Reviewer or Planner) that verifies requirements agreement before implementation — actually the reviewers in mpd profile: Architect, Researcher, Planner, Explorer, Plan Reviewer, Vision Analyst are read-only.\n\nWait — but there's a nuance: is a requirements task necessary? The user's request is crisp: 1) rename workmate, 2) delete workmate. Scope: also traverse the project to understand details (that's recon — I'm doing it, and could delegate a deeper \"full project traversal\" research task so the team genuinely covers it).\n\nGiven the goal includes \"遍历工程，了解工程详细信息\" (traverse the project, understand detailed info) — that's a research/requirements task: produce a grounded map of the workmate system + affected surfaces. That's a real task for the Researcher/Explorer.\n\nLet me decide the DAG (quality-mode-ish):\n\n1. **T1 requirements** (Researcher or Planner, read-only, kind=requirements): Full grounded traversal — workmate system end-to-end: plugin source, service surface, web routes, GUI tab, locale dictionaries, agent-teams member binding, tests, docs (AGENTS.md/README bilingual), evidence conventions. Plus verdict=pass requirements contract for rename+delete: exact acceptance criteria, invariants (path safety, sanitize, index consistency, home isolation in QA), edge cases (rename to existing name, rename to same name, delete non-existent, delete with active team member binding, name collisions case-insensitivity, path traversal via `..`). Deliverable: `docs/` note? Careful — docs are bilingual human-facing. Maybe put the requirements summary in the task output + a workspace file under a QA/process path... Hmm, minimal diffs principle. Perhaps put it into `evidence/workmate/<slug>/<timestamp>/` as evidence. Actually a requirements doc is process artifact — fine in evidence dir.\n\nBetter: keep it as task output (structured), no extra file sprawl. But acceptance requires on-disk evidence for gates.\n\n2. **T2 implementation — core plugin** (Senior Engineer): implement rename + delete in `packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/src/index.ts`: new tools `mpd_workmate_rename`, `mpd_workmate_delete`; service methods; web routes `/plugins/mpd-workmate/rename`, `/plugins/mpd-workmate/delete`; index.json consistency; path safety; rebuild dist.\n   inScope: packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/**\n   verify: `bun test packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/test/workmate.test.ts`, `bun run typecheck`, rebuild dist.\n\n3. **T3 implementation — GUI** (Junior Engineer or Senior Engineer): workmate sidebar tab rename/delete affordances + locale dictionaries zh/en + rebuild client.js (`node scripts/build-mpd-client.mjs`), keep the sidebar-only design (no fallback) and seam policy.\n   inScope: packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/src/web-client.js, packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/client.js, test files.\n   Depends on T2 (needs route contract) — genuine prerequisite (route names/response shapes).\n\nHmm — could they run in parallel? T3 needs the route contract. But if the contract is FIXED by T1 (requirements defines exact route names + payloads), T2 and T3 could go parallel. Yet with the GUI depending on routes' exact JSON, and risk of divergence, a dependency T3←T2 is a genuine prerequisite-ish. To maximize parallelism while keeping correctness, I'll define exact route/response contracts in T1 (requirements), then let T2 and T3 both depend on T1 and run in PARALLEL. That's the \"independent work should be parallel\" instruction. But risk: if T2's implementation deviates, the GUI breaks. Mitigation: T1 fixes the contract as binding; verification covers both.\n\nHmm, but a serial dependency is safer and gives a real prerequisite. The instruction says \"dependencies are only genuine prerequisites\". The GUI literally needs routes to exist to be tested end-to-end. But unit test of the client can be done against a stub. I think requiring T1 contract + parallel is defensible, but there's real integration risk in a repo with this much GUI fragility (see the sidebar troubleshooting table — the GUI is brittle). I'll make T3 depend on T2. Actually wait — for parallelism/scheduling efficiency... The user cares about a correct result. T2 and T3 are small; the critical path matter less. But the protocol says independent work should be parallel and dependencies only genuine prerequisites.\n\nDecision: T2 (core plugin tools/routes) and T3 (client UI) both depend on T1. Rationale: T1 pins the exact wire contract, making them genuinely independent. Hmm, but if T2 and T3 both edit... T2 edits mpd-workmate-plugin, T3 edits mpd-bundle-plugin + client.js. No file overlap → safe parallel.\n\nHmm, but there's a catch: T3's build (`node scripts/build-mpd-client.mjs`) reads web-client.js and writes client.js. Fine.\n\n4. **T4 tests** — who owns? The implementation tasks should include their own tests (TDD). But a dedicated verification task (kind=verification) is needed: an independent member runs the gates and real-case QA. That's T4: verification: run `bun test packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/test/workmate.test.ts`, `bun run typecheck`, `bun run test:qa`, and a real end-to-end check via the actual dsh web profile? Live GUI check may be heavy. At minimum: plugin-level real test with sandbox HOME (test already has a harness), plus dsh QA case with overlay boot (`skills/dsh-qa`).\n\nActually — the dsh-qa skill exists. A real case could boot dsh headless with HOME=sandbox and call the workmate tools... but tools need an LLM session. The existing test file already does a sandbox-HOME lifecycle test with fake ctx/tools. So verification: extend the test to cover rename/delete + run gates + boot check `dsh --profile headless --dump-config` in isolated DSH_HOME (per AGENTS.md §4 gate \"Boot check\").\n\n5. **T5 review** (Reviewer, kind=review, round=1, reviewedTaskId=T2/T3...). A review task can only reference ONE reviewedTaskId. Hmm — the schema says `reviewedTaskId` required for kind=review, singular. So two reviews: one for T2, one for T3? That inflates. Alternatively one review referencing T2 (the core) and T3 covered by... the review task's own description. The system's contract is per-task. I'll create two review tasks? \"the smallest useful task graph\". Two reviews = 2 more members busy. Hmm.\n\nGiven the bundled integrity: rename/delete must work through BOTH surfaces (tools + GUI). I'd rather have ONE review task on the core implementation (T2) whose description explicitly also