rdict=pass.\n  Hmm — do I want a separate traversal deliverable? The user asked \"遍历工程，了解工程详细信息\" — the team should genuinely traverse. Planner's grounded traversal of the workmate system (plugin, routes, GUI, agent-teams binding, tests, docs, evidence conventions) + the affected-surface map = the answer. One task. Plus maybe an **Explorer** task for the broader project map (whole-repo traversal: 24 packages, gates, QA, docs policy) — the user asked to traverse the WHOLE project, not just workmate. That's genuinely independent and parallelizable ⇒ good separate ready task, running in parallel from the start, assigned to **Explorer**, and its output feeds the docs/integration task.\n\nSo:\n- T1 [requirements, Planner]: requirements + wire contract for rename/delete (workmate system deep-dive). Gate: verdict=pass.\n- T2 [work, Explorer]: whole-project traversal dossier (packages, plugins, gates, install/pack flow, QA discipline, docs policy, where workmate sits) → deliverable: structured report. Independent, ready immediately. Might feed T7 docs/integration. Make T7 depend on T2? Not strictly required... but \"later synthesis\" deps are allowed. I'd make the docs task depend on T2 (needs the project map for accurate cross-references) — hmm, docs are about the workmate package README + AGENTS.md rows. T2's map helps. I'll make it depend on T2 (genuine input) — no, careful: adding a dep serializes. AGENTS.md is already known to me. I'll make docs depend on T1 only (needs the tool contract) and let T2 feed the captain's synthesis + integration. Actually integration (T8) can depend on T2 (dossier) as a genuine input. Hmm, that would serialize integration behind the explorer — integration is at the end anyway. Fine, low cost, real input.\n\nLet me simplify to keep it small but complete:\n\n1. **R1 [requirements, Planner]** — Workmate rename/delete: grounded requirements + binding wire contract. verdict gate.\n2. **E1 [work, Explorer]** — Whole-project traversal dossier. (parallel, ready now)\n3. **I1 [implementation, Senior Engineer]** — core plugin: rename + delete tools/service/routes + unit tests + rebuild dist. depends R1. inScope packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/**.\n4. **I2 [implementation, Junior Engineer]** — GUI: rename/delete affordances in workmate sidebar tab + zh/en dictionaries + client rebuild + client test updates. depends R1. inScope packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/** (src/web-client.js, client.js, test/**).\n   (Runs parallel with I1 thanks to the pinned wire contract in R1.)\n5. **V1 [verification, Deep Worker?]** — Independent verification: run plugin tests, client tests, typecheck, test:qa, isolated boot `dsh --profile headless --dump-config` + real sandbox-HOME end-to-end rename/delete through the service/routes; evidence on disk. depends I1, I2.\n   Who? Deep Worker (hephaestus) is a strong executor — verification is a quality kind; assignee could be Senior Engineer, but I1 is Senior Engineer and a member can't own 2 unfinished tasks — fine, sequenced. Better: assign V1 to **Deep Worker** (execution-heavy verification) or **Reviewer**? Reviewer is doing review. Let me assign V1 to Deep Worker.\n6. **RV1 [review, Reviewer]** — review of I1 (core). depends I1.\n7. **RV2 [review, Plan Reviewer]** — review of I2 (GUI + contract conformance). depends I2.\n   RV1/RV2 could depend on V1 (evidence available for the review)? Reviews judge the latest implementation. Giving reviewers the verification evidence helps. But dependency RV1←V1 means review waits for verification which waits for I2. That serializes heavily. Reviewers can run their own checks. Keep RV1 depends I1; RV2 depends I2. V1 independent verification runs in parallel.\n8. **D1 [work, docs]** — bilingual docs: mpd-workmate-plugin README(.zh-CN) + AGENTS.md rows (tools list §1 glossary §13, troubleshooting entries) + any root README tool list. depends R1, and maybe I1/I2 (docs should describe the shipped behavior; describing after implementation avoids doc/impl drift). depends I1 + I2? That serializes docs to the end — which is right for accuracy. But if reviews return needs_revision, docs may need repair too. I'll make D1 depend on I1 and I2 (genuine: doc text must match the shipped tool surface).\n9. **G1 [integration, Lead]** — final integration: gates sweep, bundle boot check, evidence commit-ready summary, verify docs+impl consistency, report. depends V1, RV1, RV2, D1, E1.\n\nMembers: Planner, Explorer, Senior Engineer, Junior Engineer, Deep Worker, Reviewer, Plan Reviewer, Lead, + captain. That's 8 members — mpd profile has 11 (Architect, Researcher, Planner, Explorer, Plan Reviewer, Vision Analyst, Senior Engineer, Junior Engineer, Deep Worker, Lead, Reviewer). With profile create, all 11 are staged automatically. I should NOT recreate members. The profile supplies the roster. So I just design tasks and assign to those member names.\n\nHmm wait — does `agent_teams_create(profile=\"mpd\")` create all 11 members staged? \"Optional profiles expand their configured roster; seed profiles also expand template tasks, while captain profiles leave the graph for the Captain to design.\" mpd is captain-planning → roster only, DAG by me. But the session-start note said a default team \"MPD Default\" (mpd-default-38e78dd5, profile mpd) is ALREADY staged for me, and I may not create a second team while leading this one. So: use the existing staged team! I should inspect its staged roster via agent_teams_status and then add tasks.\n\nThat's important: the plugin already provisioned \"MPD Default\" staged with profile mpd. The slash command says \"Call agent_teams_create with approval=required... Use configured AgentTeams profile mpd\". But the system reminder says a team is already staged and I may not create a second one. Let me check status first — if \"MPD Default\" exists and staged with the roster, I'll use it (rename? no rename tool). The team name \"MPD Default\" is generic. I could keep it; or archive it and create a properly named one (\"mpd\" profile) which aligns with the slash-command instruction. The instruction explicitly says: \"If the default team does not fit this session, archive it with agent_teams_delete first, then create a fresh one with agent_teams_create(approval='required', profile='mpd')\".\n\nDoes \"MPD Default\" fit? It's profile mpd, which is what I need. Its name is generic but names don't matter much... A purpose-named team is nicer: \"Workmate Rename & Delete\". Since the profile matches exactly what I'd create anyway, the cleanest is to reuse the existing staged team (no archive/create churn). The slash-command harness instruction says to call agent_teams_create with profile mpd — but if a staged team already exists, calling create would error (\"may not create a second team\"). I'll check status and decide: I think reusing is right and I'll note it. Hmm, but the explicit slash-command instruction says to call agent_teams_create... The session-start rule takes precedence in practice because creating a second team is disallowed. Let me verify with status.\n\nAlso, the roster: profile mpd already provides all 11 members staged. Then I only add tasks. Let me check status now.\n\nThen: REQUIREMENTS CONFERENCE. I must hold a joint requirements session with the user + agents, using Socratic questioning, and put consolidated questions to the user via ask_user_question. Since members are staged (not running), I can't get their questions by message; but I can consult one read-only specialist one-shot via mpd_role_spawn (Architect or Plan Reviewer) to generate the hard questions — that's the \"agents present\" part, done outside the team's staging. Cost: one subagent call. Value: genuine requirement questions that I might not think of. I think it's worth it — the user's instruction demands the conference, and a Plan Reviewer (momus) persona is exactly the \"adversarial requirements\" role.\n\nLet me do it: spawn Plan Reviewer (momus) one-shot, read-only, to produce a list of requirement questions and edge cases for the workmate rename/delete feature, given a context block with my findings. Run in background while I ask the user my own questions? Careful: I should consolidate questions into ONE batch for the user. So: first get momus's questions (foreground/background), then ask the user the merged batch.\n\nLet me first check team status, then fire the specialist consult in background, then... Actually simplest: run momus consult (background), meanwhile check status and read the GUI code; then batch questions to the user; then build the DAG.\n\nWait — but ask_user_question ends my turn awaiting the answer? Typically the user answers and I continue in the next turn. Then the plan is staged but the user has answered. The protocol wants the Web plan ready at end of turn. Combining: I can ask questions AND stage the plan in the same turn (staging first, then ask). But if the user answers questions in a new turn, I then revise the plan via agent_teams_edit_plan. That's the sanctioned flow (\"the user may return to chat to request changes ... call agent_teams_edit_plan\").\n\nOrder in this turn:\n1. status check (see staged roster)\n2. momus consult (get sharp questions) — must be done before asking the user, so make it foreground-ish (background then collect). Let me run it in background and read GUI code meanwhile, then collect.\n3. Stage the DAG (tasks) — after I know the contract shape. Hmm, if the user's answers change the contract (e.g. delete = archive vs hard delete), tasks change. But I can encode the open decisions in R1 (the requirements task must resolve them with the user's answer) — actually better: I ask the user NOW, then build the DAG from their answers in the SAME turn. Then the plan is directly aligned. And I don't need a clarification round-trip.\n\nBut careful: ask_user_question in the same turn as staging the plan — the answer arrives... Does ask_user_question block within the turn (returning the answer as a tool result) or end the turn? Typically it returns the answer as a tool result so I can continue in the same turn. Let me just try it: ask early, get answers, then stage DAG, then finish turn with \"plan ready\".\n\nHmm, risk: if it ends the turn, then the user answers in the next turn and I stage then. Either way it works.\n\nLet me minimize risk: Ask the user the questions FIRST (as early as possible), then stage. So sequence:\n- status + read GUI (parallel, now)\n- momus consult (need it before asking) — foreground? It could take a while. Alternatively skip momus and derive questions myself... The instructions mandate the requirements conference with agents participating. I'll do one momus consult — it's the strongest fit for \"challenge assumptions, surface edge cases\". Run it with run_in_background default true; then collect with job... no, mpd_role_spawn is a subagent-like tool; its result returns as a notice. Hmm, mpd_role_spawn — does it support background? It has no run_in_background param; it likely returns the result synchronously (one-shot). Let me just call it and see; it should return the specialist's text.\n\nLet me do: [status, read GUI section] in parallel, then momus consult, then ask_user_question, then create tasks.\n\nActually to save time: run status + momus consult in parallel now (independent). Then read GUI while waiting? The consult may return synchronously and consume the turn's time. Fine.\n\nLet me go.\n\nFor the consult context I need the GUI details (locale keys, routes). I'll pass what I know: routes list, tools list, sanitizeName, index.json, agent-teams member binding (member name == workmate key), caps, tests, docs policy.\n\nLet's execute."},{"type":"text","text":"Reconnaissance is grounded. Now let me check the staged team and run a specialist consult for the requirements conference in parallel."},{"type":"tool-call","id":"call_00_yA1RrRLyqrnrtd1QuZG76332","name":"agent_teams_status","arguments":"{}"},{"type":"tool-call","id":"call_01_Y7VfKS5e06pIOamVG3ir4466","name":"mpd_role_spawn","arguments":"{\"role\": \"momus\", \"task\": \"You are the Plan Reviewer in a REQUIREMENTS CONFERENCE for a small feature in the my-power-dsh repo (/root/dshProj/my-power-dsh). The user's request (Chinese, verbatim): \\\"遍历工程，了解工程详细信息，并对当前workmate系统做优化：1.可以给workmate改名 2.可以删除workmate\\\" — i.e. (1) allow renaming a workmate, (2) allow deleting a workmate.\\n\\nDo NOT propose the final plan. Your ONE deliverable is an adversarial requirements interrogation: at most 8 SHARP, DECISION-FORCING questions that the captain must put to the USER (product/semantics questions only a human can decide), plus a list of edge cases the implementation MUST handle, plus 2-4 invariants that acceptance criteria must pin. Challenge assumptions, hunt for the real goal, and surface the failure modes a naive \\\"mv dir + rm -rf dir\\\" implementation would create. Be concrete and terse (bullets). No file edits.\\n\\nGROUNDED CONTEXT (measured by the captain — treat as fact, do not re-verify):\\n- Plugin: packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/src/index.ts (439 lines). Library root = $HOME/.mpd/workmate (deliberate cross-project, user-approved HOME exception to the workspace-scoped state rule). One dir per workmate: meta.json (name/baseId/baseName/description/provider/model/readonly/createdAt/updatedAt/uses/lastTask), persona.md (cap 8KiB), memory.md (cap 8KiB, oldest \\\"## \\\" blocks evicted), note.md (cap 1536B, auto-generated short matchable note). Plus a root index.json: { name -> {name, baseId, baseName, uses, updatedAt} }.\\n- sanitizeName(): lowercase, non [a-z0-9_-] -> \\\"-\\\", collapse runs, trim dashes. wmDir(name) = root + sanitizeName(name). Init refuses when the dir exists: `\\\"${name}\\\" already exists`.\\n- Existing tools: mpd_workmate_list / mpd_workmate_init / mpd_workmate_spawn (spawns a one-shot subagent with the workmate persona+memory+note, own provider/model route, readonly bases get toolFilter deny write/edit/apply_patch/mpd_hashline_edit) / mpd_workmate_reflect (append memory, optional persona_delta, regenerate note, bump uses) / mpd_workmate_match (deterministic keyword-overlap scoring, MATCH_THRESHOLD 0.35, weak match => matched=false, caller must init a NEW workmate instead).\\n- A cordis service `mpdWorkmate` is provided: { list, get, read }.\\n- Web routes registered by the plugin (exact paths): GET /plugins/mpd-workmate/list, GET /plugins/mpd-workmate/roster, GET /plugins/mpd-workmate/get?name=, POST /plugins/mpd-workmate/init. GUI = a DSH-better-sidebar tab contributed at client-apply time by packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/src/web-client.js (SIDEBAR_TAB_ID=\\\"mpd-workmate\\\", sidebar-only by design, no floating fallback), with zh/en locale dictionaries (zh is the key-set source of truth) and the built artifact packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/client.js (built by `node scripts/build-mpd-client.mjs`).\\n- CRITICAL CROSS-MODULE BINDING: the adopted agent-teams plugin binds a team member to a workmate by NAME — packages/mpd-agent-teams-plugin/lib/members.js#workmateKey(memberName) sanitizes the MEMBER NAME and, if $HOME/.mpd/workmate/<key> exists, injects that workmate's persona+memory+note into the member persona and instructs the member to call mpd_workmate_reflect after each task. So a member is bound to a workmate purely by name equality; renaming a workmate silently unbinds/rebinds existing team members, and deleting one breaks the binding.\\n- Tests: packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/test/workmate.test.ts (bun test; sandbox HOME; lifecycle init->list->reflect->match, spawn assertions, root-under-HOME assertion). QA convention: evidence/<domain>/<slug>/<timestamp>/{result.json,output.log}; gates: bun test, bun run typecheck, bun run test:qa, node scripts/verify-vendor.mjs, `dsh --profile headless --dump-config` in an isolated DSH_HOME. Docs are BILINGUAL: packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/README.md AND README.zh-CN.md must both be updated in the same change; AGENTS.md is the binding English-only agent manual (it lists workmate tools, glossary entry \\\"workmate\\\", troubleshooting rows).\\n\\nOutput format: (1) USER QUESTIONS — numbered, each with why it matters and the candidate answers; (2) EDGE CASES the implementation must handle; (3) INVARIANTS for acceptance criteria.\", \"context\": \"Repo: /root/dshProj/my-power-dsh (my-power-dsh DeepSeek Harness bundle, AGENTS.md is the binding manual). Feature: workmate rename + delete. Read-only consultation — you are mechanically denied write tools. Return text only.\"}"}],"source":{"kind":"model","provider":"deepseek-official","model":"deepseek-v4-flash"},"id":"3c605a2a-6578-45c3-b4ca-34e3bd5d425f"},"usage":{"inputTokens":3212,"outputTokens":7060,"totalTokens":64672,"cacheReadTokens":54400,"reasoningTokens":5815}},"sourceEventSeqs":[[1012,8024]],"surfaceOp":"append"}
