nent in the plan (and in my message) that CJK names are rejected by default and they can request Unicode support at approval time.\n\nHmm. Let me weigh once more: The user's chat is Chinese. \"可以给workmate改名\" — they'd likely rename to something meaningful. If they try 中文 and get an error, that's a friction point. But the error message would be clear. And the fix is a real cross-plugin key-scheme change. I'll surface it as a REQUIRED decision point in the plan (and offer it in my message as an optional add-on), not silently decide. Good compromise; keeps momentum.\n\nSo: I should probably ask this one question now via ask_user_question (single question, cheap) because it changes the DAG (unicode support = extra task + cross-plugin change). Two batches is acceptable when the second batch is one focused question arising from the specialist consult. Yes — do it: it's the requirements conference doing its job, and it avoids a plan revision round-trip later.\n\nAlso worth confirming the authority question (Q1: should the agent be able to delete via tool?). The user chose \"工具 + GUI\" for the surface, which implies agent tools exist. And delete is archivable-by-default, so agent deletion is recoverable → risk mitigated. I'll add `confirm` semantics: delete requires confirm (typed name) in GUI; agent tool requires `confirm: true`. I'll just design that in; no need to ask.\n\nLet me ask ONE consolidated second question about Unicode/CJK naming. Actually, I can ask two: (1) CJK name support; (2) whether archived team member names should be rewritten (I'll state my recommendation: no, historical records). Hmm #2 I can decide (record-keeping).\n\nActually let me reconsider #2 more carefully — the user said \"改了以后全改\". If I don't cascade into archived teams, is that violating their instruction? Their instruction's intent is likely \"don't leave things half-updated / keep consistency\". Historical snapshots of what happened shouldn't be rewritten. But a *live* team must not silently lose backing → hence refuse. I'll explain this in the plan and in my message. If they disagree at approval, they can say so.\n\nLet me ask the CJK question only. Then build the DAG.\n\nDAG design (final):\n\nTeam: MPD Default (staged, profile mpd, 11 members).\n\n**T1 [requirements] assignee: Planner** — \"Workmate rename/delete: grounded requirements contract + pinned wire protocol\"\n- objective: turn the agreed decisions into a binding contract: exact tool names/args/returns, route names/verbs/payloads/status codes, refusal semantics for the liveness gate, cascade list, archive layout, error taxonomy; plus the edge-case matrix from the interrogation.\n- acceptance: contract covers every edge case; wire contract exact (route path/method/request/response fields/status codes); decisions cite user answers; verdict=pass; deliverable text in the task output + a contract file under evidence/.\n- kind requirements, verdict gate. depends: none.\n- deliverable: evidence/workmate/rename-delete-requirements/<ts>/contract.md (process artifact, English).\n\n**T2 [work] assignee: Explorer** — \"Project traversal dossier: where workmate sits + every coupled surface\"\n- The user asked to traverse the project. Deliverable: a grounded map: all 24 packages, which surfaces reference workmate (docs, presets, bundle patch, agent-teams binding, QA scripts, tests), gates, evidence conventions, install/pack flow.\n- independent, ready at start. deliverables: evidence/workmate/project-traversal/<ts>/dossier.md.\n- depends: none.\n- Explorer is read-only: it writes... hmm! Explorer is read-only (mechanically denied write tools). But a deliverable file requires writing. Read-only members \"never edit files\". So an Explorer task cannot produce a file — it must return the dossier as task output text. But then evidence-on-disk? The captain can persist it. Hmm, but a work task with a deliverable path it can't write = broken task.\n\nCareful: in this harness, \"read-only\" is enforced only via the roles plugin's toolFilter (mpd_role_spawn) — and in the team, members are spawned by the agent-teams plugin via `registerContinuableSetup`/`spawnAgent` with memberPersona; does the team spawn apply a read-only filter? The profile description says \"Read-only members (Architect, Researcher, Planner, Explorer, Plan Reviewer, Vision Analyst) take requirements/review/analysis tasks only and never edit files\" — that's a policy instruction, possibly not mechanically enforced. Let me check members.js for readonly enforcement. Earlier I saw `toolFilter` usage? Let me grep quickly... I should check to avoid designing an impossible task.\n\nAlso note: since the roles plugin's READONLY_DENY is BROKEN (unknown tool names → harness rejects), maybe the team's member spawn doesn't apply a filter at all. Let me check `lib/members.js` for toolFilter/restrict.\n\nLet me check that before finalizing. Also, for read-only members producing files: I'll make the read-only tasks' deliverables be *task output* (text), and have the captain or a worker persist them into evidence. Or use read-only tasks that only analyze and report (fine — the Web plan panel shows task output). Simplest: read-only tasks report in their output; the Lead (worker) can persist the synthesis into evidence during integration. Good.\n\n**T3 [implementation] assignee: Senior Engineer** — core plugin: `mpd_workmate_rename` + `mpd_workmate_delete` tools, service methods, web routes, liveness gate, archive layout, index consistency, root-safety guards, unit tests, dist rebuild.\n- inScope: packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/** (+ its tests)\n- verify: bun test packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/test/workmate.test.ts; bun run typecheck; bun build → dist\n- depends: T1\n**T4 [implementation] assignee: Junior Engineer** — GUI: workmate tab rename/delete controls + zh/en dictionaries + rebuild client.js + client test updates.\n- inScope: packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/src/web-client.js, packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/client.js, packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/test/**, scripts/build-mpd-client.mjs (if needed)\n- verify: node scripts/build-mpd-client.mjs; bun test packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/test/sidebar-tab.test.mjs\n- depends: T1\n\n**T5 [implementation/repair?] assignee: Deep Worker** — readonly deny-list fix (both plugins) + real-harness regression. This is an implementation task.\n- inScope: packages/mpd-roles-plugin/src/index.ts, packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/src/index.ts (READONLY_DENY), tests.\n  - CONFLICT: T3 also edits packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/src/index.ts! Both would edit the same file → parallel edit conflict. Options: (a) make T5 depend on T3 (serialize on the same file), or (b) put the READONLY_DENY fix inside T3's scope for the workmate side and make T5 only the roles side.\n  Decision: T5 = roles-plugin deny fix + the cross-harness regression test that proves restrict() is accepted (real harness, isolated); T3 owns the workmate-side `READONLY_DENY` change. To avoid overlap I'll state in T5's inScope: packages/mpd-roles-plugin/** only, and explicitly outOfScope: packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/** (T3 owns that line). T5 depends on nothing (roles plugin is independent) → can run in parallel with T3. \n  Hmm, but the regression test proving the real harness accepts the filter — where does it live? A QA case under skills/dsh-qa/scripts/ (a real-boot case). T5 inScope: packages/mpd-roles-plugin/**, skills/dsh-qa/**. But T6 (verification) may also touch QA scripts... Let me give the QA-case creation to T5 (it's part of the fix's evidence) and keep T6 as runner/verifier (no edits needed, or minor). Hmm, verification tasks that need to write evidence files — evidence dir is a distinct path. inScope for T6: evidence/**.\n  \n**T6 [verification] assignee: ???** — independent verification of T3+T4+T5: run all gates, real sandbox-HOME lifecycle (rename/delete incl. refusal cases), real boot checks, run the T5 QA case, capture evidence.\n- depends T3, T4, T5\n- assignee: a worker who isn't the implementer. Available workers: Senior Engineer (T3), Junior Engineer (T4), Deep Worker (T5), Lead (integration), Reviewer (review). If Reviewer does reviews and Lead does integration... For verification, use **Reviewer**? No, Reviewer should review T3. Hmm.\n  Members: 11 total. Workers: Senior Engineer, Junior Engineer, Deep Worker, Lead, Reviewer. Read-only: Architect, Researcher, Planner, Explorer, Plan Reviewer, Vision Analyst.\n  I need: V1 verification (independent) + RV1 review(core) + RV2 review(gui) + integration.\n  Assign: V1 → Deep Worker? But Deep Worker owns T5 (impl). A member can't own two unfinished tasks — T5 must complete before T6 can be claimed by the same member (dependencies allow: T5 → T6). Since T6 depends on T5 anyway, sequencing is fine: Deep Worker does T5, then T6. That's acceptable but the verifier verifying its own fix is not independent for the T5 part. Reviewer could verify T5... \n  Cleaner assignment:\n  - V1 [verification] → **Reviewer**? Reviewer is for review. \n  Let me map: \n  - T6 verification: assignee **Junior Engineer**? It owns T4. Hmm, same problem.\n  \n  Alternative: use more of the roster:\n  - T3 impl core → Senior Engineer (deepseek-v4-pro) ✓\n  - T4 impl GUI → Junior Engineer ✓\n  - T5 readonly fix + QA case → Deep Worker ✓\n  - T6 verification → assign to **Lead**? Lead is integration; let me instead give verification to the **Reviewer**... no.\n  \n  Options: I (captain) can take one task. Actually a clean mapping:\n  - T6 [verification] → **Deep Worker** (after T5) — but self-verification of T5. Mitigation: T6's scope is the cross-cutting acceptance (rename/delete end-to-end + full gates), while T5's own evidence is the restrict-acceptance probe; the Reviewer reviews the whole implementation set anyway.\n  - T7 [review] → **Reviewer** on T3 (core; includes the T5 fix? reviewedTaskId is single...).\n  \n  Hmm, three implementation tasks and one reviewedTaskId per review. Reviews needed: T3, T4, T5. Three review tasks