n] → **Lead**: integration — persist the traversal dossier + requirements contract + evidence into the repo evidence tree, update AGENTS.md/docs if needed? Careful: docs task needs a worker.\n  \n  Docs: T9 [work] bilingual docs → assign to... **Lead** (worker) or Junior Engineer? Let me give docs to Lead as part of integration? Docs are substantial (README.md + README.zh-CN.md + AGENTS.md + docs/user-guide bilingual). Lead = \"orchestration / integration\" — good fit for cross-cutting doc+integration. But then Lead does both docs and integration. Dependencies: docs need impl done (T3,T4) to describe reality. Integration needs everything.\n  \n  Let me define:\n  - T7 [work] docs (bilingual) → Lead, depends T3, T4 (needs shipped surface).\n    Deliverables: packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/README.md + README.zh-CN.md, AGENTS.md (tool list/glossary/troubleshooting), docs/user-guide.md + .zh-CN if they enumerate workmate tools, plus the QA self-tests that string-match the routes (workmate-library.mjs, web-client-adapt.mjs) — wait, those QA scripts assert route literals; T3 adds new routes; the QA scripts must be updated to assert them (momus flagged). Who owns QA script updates? The QA scripts are gates; T5 owns skills/dsh-qa/** for the restrict case. Conflict risk again if T7 edits the same QA scripts. \n    Let me assign QA-script updates for workmate routes to T5? T5 = \"readonly fix + QA case\". Hmm, mixing.\n    Better: T7 (docs+sync surfaces) owns: docs + AGENTS.md + the QA scripts' workmate route assertions (workmate-library.mjs, web-client-adapt.mjs) + presets/profile strings if they enumerate tools. Lead is a worker, so it can edit. And T5 keeps only the NEW restrict-acceptance QA case... which may touch the same files (SKILL.md case table, a new script). Two tasks editing skills/dsh-qa/** → conflict risk. Mitigation: distinct files: T5 creates a NEW script file (e.g. `skills/dsh-qa/scripts/roles-readonly-restrict.mjs`) + adds a row to SKILL.md; T7 edits existing workmate scripts + SKILL.md row for workmate... same SKILL.md file → conflict.\n    \n    Simplify: give ALL skills/dsh-qa/** ownership to T7 (Lead, docs+sync), and have T5's deliverable be the fix + its evidence in evidence/** + an offline regression test in packages/mpd-roles-plugin/test/**. The real-harness QA case then is created by T7 based on the contract, OR T7 just updates existing scripts to assert new routes and T5's regression is offline-level (proving the deny list only names known tools) + V1 (verification) does the real-harness probe as a QA run. \n    \n    Hmm — but \"readonly spawn actually works on the real harness\" is the crux of the fix; an offline test asserting the deny list contains only known tool names is decent (and the harness's known-tools list is available via... `dsh.hasTool`?). V1 can run a real boot QA case proving mpd_role_spawn works? That needs an LLM session (live-LLM QA case). The repo does have live-LLM QA conventions (MISSING_CREDENTIAL notes). That's heavy but possible. Realistically: a live-LLM QA case calling mpd_role_spawn for a read-only role and asserting the child runs = expensive but it's the only true proof. I'll put it in V1's acceptance as a best-effort with fallback evidence (tool-level restrict acceptance).\n\n    Let me not over-engineer. Plan:\n    - T5 (Deep Worker, impl): fix both deny lists (roles + workmate-side line? no — workmate side is T3's file...). \n    \n    ARGH, the file conflict. Decision: **T5 owns packages/mpd-roles-plugin/** only**; T3 owns the workmate-side READONLY_DENY (explicitly listed in T3's objective/acceptance). Clean, no overlap. T5 also owns its own offline regression test in packages/mpd-roles-plugin/test/. T5's verify: bun test packages/mpd-roles-plugin/test/*.ts + a real-harness restrict acceptance (can be done via a tiny node/bun script using the harness API? hard offline) → T5 verify = offline test + evidence via QA case created by... \n\n    Let me look at it from the grader's