evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:1003:{"type":"assistant/chunk","seq":25144,"time":1789027063680,"data":{"turn":1,"step":12,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":0,"block":{"type":"reasoning","text":"All decisions are in. Now let me design and stage the DAG in the existing staged team \"MPD Default\".\n\nDecisions (agreed requirements):\n- D1: Delete = archive by default (move to `~/.mpd/workmate/.archive/<name>-<ISO>/`), `purge: true` for real removal; GUI two-step confirm.\n- D2: Liveness gate: refuse rename/delete while the workmate is in use (in-flight spawn in this process; live/non-archived team member bound by name). When allowed, cascade ALL library-owned name references (dir, meta.name, index key, note self-reference, `renamedFrom` continuity). Archived teams are historical records — not rewritten (stated explicitly).\n- D3: readonly deny-list defect is IN scope: fix both plugins + real-harness regression.\n- D4: Surface = agent tools + web routes + Workmates sidebar GUI (zh/en).\n- D5: ASCII naming only for now; reject invalid/empty before any fs op.\n\nNow the tasks. Let me write them carefully with quality contracts.\n\nT1 requirements — Planner:\nsubject: \"Workmate rename/delete requirements contract + pinned wire protocol\"\nobjective: ...\nacceptance: list\ndeliverables: evidence/workmate/rename-delete/<ts>/requirements.md (hmm — Planner is read-only, cannot write). Read-only! So deliverables must be text output only. Let me set deliverables: [\"task output: requirements contract (text)\", \"edge-case matrix\"]. Better to just describe in description that the contract is delivered as task output text; the captain/Lead persists it in evidence during integration. I'll note that in T8 (integration) deliverable: persist the contract + dossier to evidence/.\n\nCareful about \"deliverables\" being workspace paths — I'll use names not paths for read-only tasks.\n\nT2 work — Explorer: project traversal dossier (read-only, output text).\n\nT3 implementation — Senior Engineer: core plugin.\ninScope: packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/\nverify: \n - \"bun test packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/test/workmate.test.ts\"\n - \"bun run typecheck\"\n - \"bun build src/index.ts --target node --format esm --outfile dist/index.js\" (run in package dir) — need the exact rebuild command from AGENTS.md §6. Use: \"cd packages/mpd-workmate-plugin && bun build src/index.ts --target node --format esm --outfile dist/index.js\". Hmm, `verify` commands run from workspace root presumably. Let me phrase with explicit cd.\n\nT4 implementation — Junior Engineer: GUI.\ninScope: packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/src/web-client.js, packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/test/, packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/client.js\nverify: \"node scripts/build-mpd-client.mjs\", \"bun test packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/test/sidebar-tab.test.mjs\"\n\nT5 implementation — Deep Worker: readonly deny fix.\ninScope: packages/mpd-roles-plugin/, skills/dsh-qa/scripts/ (new case), packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/src/index.ts? NO — that's T3's. But the workmate READONLY_DENY must be fixed too and T3 owns it. I'll state it in T3's objective/acceptance, and T5's outOfScope excludes packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/**.\nverify: \"bun test packages/mpd-roles-plugin/test/*.ts\", \"node skills/dsh-qa/scripts/<new case>.mjs --self-test\"\n\nHmm, does packages/mpd-roles-plugin have tests? Let me check quickly... I should verify test dirs exist so verify commands aren't fake. Let me check for test dirs across these packages. I'll run a quick ls.\n\nT6 verification — assignee? Let me pick Deep Worker? It owns T5. Alternatives: Reviewer/Lead are needed elsewhere. Hmm — what about **Architect** (read-only) doing verification? Read-only can RUN commands (bash) — verification doesn't require editing source, only writing evidence files. Writing evidence files under evidence/** requires write tools → read-only members can't. Hmm, but is read-only mechanically enforced for team members? I need to check. If team members have no filter, a read-only member could technically write. But policy says they never edit files. Evidence writing is \"editing files\" — risky.\n\nLet me check members.js for toolFilter enforcement to decide the assignee mapping correctly. Let me grep.\n\nActually, a practical approach for verification: the verifier runs commands via bash and reports command results in the task's `commandsRun` field (structured evidence) + writes evidence files. If read-only members can't write, the evidence goes into the task output and the Lead persists it (T8's job: persist evidence). That works: T6 assignee = a worker with write access: options left are **Reviewer** (does reviews) and **Lead** (docs+integration) and the three implementers.\n\nMapping constraint: each member owns ≤1 unfinished task; dependencies serialize.\n\nProposed final mapping:\n- T1 requirements → Planner (RO)\n- T2 dossier → Explorer (RO)\n- T3 core impl → Senior Engineer\n- T4 GUI impl → Junior Engineer\n- T5 readonly fix → Deep Worker\n- T6 verification → **Lead**? Then Lead also does T7 docs + T8 integration → three tasks for Lead, serialized. Hmm, that makes the critical path long and Lead overloaded.\n- Alternative T6 → **Reviewer** (verification is adjacent to review; Reviewer is a worker with write access). Then RV1 (review of T3) → also Reviewer? A member can own only one unfinished task, so RV1 waits for T6 to finish — that serializes review behind verification, which is fine ordering-wise (review sees verification evidence). But then RV2 (T4) → Plan Reviewer, RV3 (T5) → Architect. And T8 integration → Lead. T7 docs → Lead as well.\n\nSo:\n- T6 verification → Reviewer (runs all gates + real sandbox lifecycle + real boot; writes evidence)\n- RV1 review of T3 → Architect (deep correctness/safety review of the core library code) ✓ (Architect = architecture review / deep debugging / self-review)\n- RV2 review of T4 → Plan Reviewer (UI-UX critique + plan/contract conformance — perfect: \"work-plan QA review / UI-UX critique\") ✓\n- RV3 review of T5 → ??? left: Researcher, Vision Analyst, Reviewer (busy), Senior/Junior/Deep Worker (implementers: can a reviewer be an implementer? \"Do not approve your own implementation\" — RV3 must not be the T5 implementer (Deep Worker). Senior Engineer (T3) or Junior Engineer (T4) could review T5 — they didn't implement it. That's allowed. Assign RV3 → Senior Engineer (deepseek-v4-pro, strongest). But Senior Engineer owns T3; RV3 depends on T5; if T3 completes before T5, Senior can claim RV3. Ordering is uncertain but the scheduler will assign when ready. Risk: if T3 is still running when T5 completes, RV3 waits — fine, or the system might leave it pending. Acceptable.\n  Hmm, but it's cleaner to use Researcher? Researcher is read-only (\"code / open-source evidence search\") — reviewing a defect fix is evidence analysis; but the verdict requires rigor. I'd rather use Senior Engineer.\n  Wait — is there a risk that RV3 never gets claimed because Senior Engineer is stuck? Dependency-wise RV3 requires T5 completed. Senior Engineer finishes T3, then claims RV3 when T5 is done. Fine.\n\nHmm, actually: do I even need RV3? The user's directive put the readonly fix in scope; a third review adds a quality loop. The momus/plan reviewer in me says: an independent review of a fix to a *broken* mechanism is exactly where value is. Keep RV3.\n\n- T7 docs/sync → Lead (depends T3, T4, T5)\n- T8 integration → Lead (depends T6, RV1, RV2, RV3, T7, T2)\n\nHmm, T8 (integration) is a quality kind requiring objective+acceptance. Also \"integration\" is a quality kind per §8 quality kinds list (requirements, implementation, verification, review, repair, integration). Yes it's in the quality-kind list: \"Quality kinds (requirements, implementation, verification, review, repair, integration) need a contract\". So T8 needs objective + acceptance. Good.\n\nNote the protocol: \"A staged integration task may depend on review round 1.\" ✓.\n\nAlso, per §9 full-quality-mode: requirements → implementation → verification → review → integration. We have that.\n\nDo I need \"T2 dossier\" as kind=work? Yes, fine.\n\nLet me also consider: T3 and T5 both might touch `skills/dsh-qa`? T3's QA route assertions are in workmate-library.mjs / web-client-adapt.mjs — I assigned those to T7. So T3's inScope: packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/** + evidence/workmate/rename-delete/**. Wait, T3 must produce evidence too (AGENTS.md: evidence without evidence is incomplete; each gate needs on-disk evidence). Let me give each impl task an evidence path in its inScope: evidence/workmate/<slug>/**.\n\nCareful: multiple tasks writing into the same evidence dir → conflicts. Use distinct slugs:\n- T3: evidence/workmate/rename-delete-core/<ts>/\n- T4: evidence/workmate/rename-delete-gui/<ts>/\n- T5: evidence/workmate/roles-readonly/<ts>/\n- T6: evidence/workmate/rename-delete-verify/<ts>/\n- T7: evidence/workmate/rename-delete-docs/<ts>/\n- T8: evidence/workmate/rename-delete-integration/<ts>/\n\nGood.\n\nNow let me verify a few facts before writing tasks: do packages/mpd-roles-plugin and others have test dirs (for verify commands)? Let me check quickly. Also check the build command convention (package.json scripts).\n\nLet me run one command to check test dirs + package scripts.\n\nThen create the tasks (multiple agent_teams_create_task calls — can be parallel within one block? They're independent calls; the tool says tasks can depend on ids... I need ids from earlier calls for dependencies. So: first create T1, T2, T3, T4, T5 (independent of each other mostly: T3/T4/T5 depend on T1 → T3/T4 need T1's id). So sequence: create T1 & T2 in parallel → then T3,T4 (dep T1) & T5 (no dep) in parallel → then T6 (dep T3,T4,T5), RV1 (dep T3), RV2 (dep T4), RV3 (dep T5) → then T7 (dep T3,T4,T5) → T8 (dep T6,RV1,RV2,RV3,T7,T2).\n\nThat's 4 rounds of calls. Fine.\n\nLet me check facts first."}}}}
