evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:1247:{"type":"assistant/chunk","seq":32686,"time":1789027101509,"data":{"turn":1,"step":13,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":0,"block":{"type":"reasoning","text":"All gates exist. Now let me stage the DAG.\n\nRound 1: create T1 (requirements, Planner) and T2 (work dossier, Explorer).\n\nLet me write substantial, precise descriptions.\n\nT1:\nsubject: \"Requirements contract + pinned wire protocol for workmate rename/delete\"\nkind: requirements, round 1\nassignee: Planner\nobjective: \"Turn the user-approved decisions into a binding, implementation-ready contract for workmate rename + delete: exact tool surface, exact HTTP wire protocol, refusal semantics, cascade list, archive layout, and error taxonomy — covering every edge case surfaced by the adversarial interrogation.\"\nacceptance:\n- \"Contract fixes the exact names/signatures of the new agent tools: mpd_workmate_rename and mpd_workmate_delete, their required/optional args (incl. the delete archive-vs-purge switch and the confirmation arg), return fields, and error messages.\"\n- \"Contract fixes the exact HTTP wire protocol: path + method + request JSON + success/error body + status codes (405 wrong verb, 400 invalid JSON, 404 unknown name, 409/400 collision, cache-control: no-store) for every new route, byte-matching what the GUI task will call.\"\n- \"Contract pins the usage/liveness gate semantics: mutation is REFUSED while the workmate is in use (an in-flight spawn in this process, or a member of a live/non-archived team bound by name), naming the exact detection mechanism the implementation must use in this harness and its documented fallback.\"\n- \"Contract enumerates the complete cascade for rename: directory key, meta.name, index.json key, note.md self-reference, continuity metadata (renamedFrom/previousNames), updatedAt handling, and explicitly states which references are NOT rewritten (archived team records are historical and stay untouched).\"\n- \"Contract pins delete semantics: default archive to ~/.mpd/workmate/.archive/<name>-<ISO>/ (hidden from list/match, restorable), purge for real removal, and the exact refusal when the instance is in use.\"\n- \"Contract pins name-safety invariants: ASCII-only key scheme ([a-z0-9_-]); any name whose sanitized form is empty/invalid is rejected BEFORE any filesystem call; no operation may ever resolve to, move, or recursively remove the library root; collisions (dir, file, or live workmate) are refused.\"\n- \"Contract carries the edge-case matrix with a decided outcome for each item (empty/invalid name, unknown name, double delete, half-created instance without meta.json, case-only rename, sanitization collision, symlinks, partial-failure atomicity, spawn/reflect race, service immediacy, readonly instances, headless profile without web routes).\"\n- \"Verdict=pass with the contract text in the task output.\"\ndeliverables: [\"requirements contract (task output text)\", \"edge-case matrix with decided outcomes (task output text)\"]\nnonGoals: [\"Designing the implementation\", \"Editing any file (read-only role)\"]\ncoverageOf: [\"1. 可以给workmate改名\", \"2. 可以删除workmate\"]\n\nAlso description with the user decisions verbatim.\n\nT2:\nsubject: \"Project traversal dossier: workmate system in the full bundle context\"\nkind: work\nassignee: Explorer\ndescription: the whole-repo traversal; deliverables: dossier text (task output).\nNo contract needed for kind=work, but let me still include deliverables/acceptance? For kind=work only subject required. I'll add description + deliverables.\n\nLet me write these now. Keep descriptions detailed but not bloated.\n\nRound 2: T3 (Senior Engineer, implementation, dep T1), T4 (Junior Engineer, implementation, dep T1), T5 (Deep Worker, implementation, no dep).\n\nT3 details:\nsubject: \"Implement workmate rename + delete in mpd-workmate-plugin (tools, service, routes, safety, tests)\"\nkind: implementation, round 1\nassignee: Senior Engineer\ndependencies: [T1]\nobjective: \"Implement the rename and delete operations in packages/mpd-workmate-plugin exactly as the T1 contract pins them: new mpd_workmate_rename / mpd_workmate_delete tools, matching mpdWorkmate service methods, the new web routes, the usage/liveness refusal gate, the archive-first delete, full rename cascade, root-safety guards, and the workmate-side readonly deny-list correction — with unit tests and a rebuilt dist.\"\ninScope: [\"packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/\", \"evidence/workmate/rename-delete-core/\"]\noutOfScope: [\"packages/mpd-roles-plugin/\", \"packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/\", \"packages/mpd-agent-teams-plugin/\", \"AGENTS.md\", \"docs/\"]\nacceptance: (list ~7)\n - \"mpd_workmate_rename and mpd_workmate_delete are registered tools with the exact contract args/returns; the mpdWorkmate service exposes the matching methods and list/get/read reflect the mutation on the very next call (no caching).\"\n - \"After rename: old dir and old index key are gone; new dir basename === sanitizeName(meta.name) === the new index key; persona.md/memory.md/note.md content and caps, uses, lastTask and createdAt are preserved; note self-reference and continuity metadata updated; no re-instantiation and no uses bump.\"\n - \"After delete (default archive): the instance is moved under ~/.mpd/workmate/.archive/<name>-<ISO>/ and disappears from list/match/service; with purge it is removed; the index key is dropped in both paths; no other workmate's bytes change.\"\n - \"Usage/liveness gate: rename and delete are refused with a clear, actionable error while the workmate is in use (in-flight spawn in this process; a member bound by name in a live/non-archived team) using the mechanism T1 pinned; the refusal has zero side effects.\"\n - \"Name safety: any invalid/empty-sanitized name is rejected before any filesystem call; a rename collision (existing dir/file/workmate) is refused; a rename to the same sanitized key is a defined no-op error; no operation can ever resolve to, move, or remove ~/.mpd/workmate itself (root-safety test present).\"\n - \"Web routes are registered per the T1 wire contract with the pinned verbs/status codes/cache-control, and the plugin still works tool-only in a headless profile with no web server service.\"\n - \"The workmate-side READONLY_DENY no longer names tools this harness lacks (fix the readonly workmate spawn failure), pinned by a test.\"\n - \"bun test packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/test/workmate.test.ts and bun run typecheck pass; dist/index.js is rebuilt; evidence written under evidence/workmate/rename-delete-core/<timestamp>/ with command output.\"\nverify: \n - \"bun test packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/test/workmate.test.ts\"\n - \"bun run typecheck\"\n - \"cd packages/mpd-workmate-plugin && bun build src/index.ts --target node --format esm --outfile dist/index.js\"\nnonGoals: [\"GUI/client changes (T4)\", \"roles-plugin deny list (T5)\", \"docs (T7)\"]\n\nHmm: \"The workmate-side READONLY_DENY no longer names tools this harness lacks\" — good, and T5 does roles side.\n\nWait — is it safe/correct to just remove names? What ARE the correct tool names in this harness? Known: write, edit, mpd_hashline_edit, apply_patch? The harness has `write`, `edit`, `mpd_hashline_edit`. So deny should be [\"write\",\"edit\",\"mpd_hashline_edit\"] (+ maybe \"mpd_comment_check\"? no). The fix: drop the two unknown names. And also assert against the harness's known set — there's `dsh.hasTool(name)` at runtime; the plugin could filter the list dynamically by hasTool! That's a robust fix: `READONLY_DENY.filter(t => dsh.hasTool(t))`. Nice — I'll suggest that in the task as the preferred approach (filter at execute time so the list never goes stale). Good engineering. I'll mention it as a recommendation but let the implementer decide with the harness reality.\n\nT4: GUI\nsubject: \"Workmates sidebar tab: rename + delete controls (zh/en) and rebuilt client.js\"\nkind: implementation\nassignee: Junior Engineer\ndependencies: [T1]\nobjective: \"Give the Workmates DSH-better-sidebar tab the human-facing half of the feature: rename and delete controls wired to the T1 wire contract, bilingual (zh/en) dictionary keys, safe confirmation for the destructive path, stale-selection handling, and a rebuilt packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/client.js.\"\ninScope: [\"packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/src/web-client.js\", \"packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/test/\", \"packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/client.js\", \"evidence/workmate/rename-delete-gui/\"]\noutOfScope: [\"packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/\", \"packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/src/team-page.js\", \"AGENTS.md\", \"docs/\"]\nacceptance:\n- \"The workmate tab exposes rename (prefilled input) and delete (two-step arm-then-confirm, matching the archive-first semantics) for the selected workmate; both call ONLY the T1-pinned routes/methods/payloads.\"\n- \"Every new user-visible string exists in BOTH the zh and en dictionaries with identical key sets (zh remains the key-set source of truth).\"\n- \"After a successful rename the view follows to the new name (or returns to the refreshed list) and after delete it returns to the list; no stale detail polling, no 404 loop, no second tab.\"\n- \"An in-use refusal from the host (liveness gate) surfaces as a readable message instead of an unhandled failure.\"\n- \"The tab remains sidebar-only (no floating fallback / footer toggle reintroduced) and the sidebar seam policy (ctx.inject(['betterSidebar'])) is unchanged.\"\n- \"node scripts/build-mpd-client.mjs succeeds and the committed client.js contains the new routes/markers; bun test packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/test/sidebar-tab.test.mjs (and the workmate tab assertions) pass; evidence under evidence/workmate/rename-delete-gui/<timestamp>/.\"\nverify: [\"node scripts/build-mpd-client.mjs\", \"bun test packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/test/sidebar-tab.test.mjs\", \"bun run typecheck\"]\n\nHmm — `bun run typecheck` covers packages/*/src/**/*.ts only (client .js excluded). Fine, harmless but let me keep only relevant ones. Also client-harness.mjs test may need updating. Let me include \"bun test packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/test\".\n\nT5: readonly deny fix\nsubject: \"Fix the read-only deny lists so readonly spawn works on the real harness\"\nkind: implementation\nassignee: Deep Worker\ndependencies: [] (independent)\nobjective: \"Fix the live defect that read-only spawns fail on this harness: the deny lists name global tools that do not exist (str_replace_editor, apply_patch) and the harness rejects the whole restriction call, so mpd_role_spawn for read-only roster roles (and readonly workmate spawn on the workmate side, owned by T3) never applies. Prove the fix against the REAL dsh tool registry, not a stub.\"\ninScope: [\"packages/mpd-roles-plugin/\", \"skills/dsh-qa/scripts/\", \"evidence/workmate/roles-readonly/\"]\noutOfScope: [\"packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/\", \"packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/\", \"AGENTS.md\", \"docs/\"]\nacceptance:\n- \"packages/mpd-roles-plugin READONLY_DENY no longer contains any global tool name this harness does not register; the list is either corrected or filtered against the live tool registry so it can never go stale again.\"\n- \"A regression test proves the restriction list names only tools that exist in the real harness (not a stub), and that a read-only role spawn no longer fails with the 'names unknown global tools' error.\"\n- \"A dsh-qa case (script + --self-test + SKILL.md case-table row) boots the real dsh in an isolated DSH_HOME/HOME and demonstrates the read-only spawn path succeeds; it never touches the real ~/.dsh or real HOME.\"\n- \"The QA self-test sweep (bun run test:qa) still passes.\"\n- \"Evidence under evidence/workmate/roles-readonly/<timestamp>/ with the failing-before / passing-after command output.\"\nverify: [\"bun test packages/mpd-roles-plugin/test\", \"bun run test:qa\", \"bun run typecheck\"]\nnonGoals: [\"The workmate-side deny line (T3)\", \"Changing the roster, its personas or its model routes\", \"Widening what read-only roles may write\"]\n\nHmm: is verifying \"a read-only role spawn no longer fails\" possible without an LLM? mpd_role_spawn spawns a subagent → needs a live session/LLM. The QA case with real dsh boot + LLM is the live path (credentials copied into sandbox). The repo's QA skill supports live cases. It might be expensive but it's the honest proof. Alternatively, prove at the API level: the harness's `tools.restrict` acceptance. Could a live case call the tool directly without an LLM? The mpd-qa-roles-probe package does \"mpd preset resolve + mpdRoles roster\" — there's a QA probe package! `packages/mpd-qa-roles-probe`. Maybe the QA path can boot a headless session with a scripted prompt... Let me not over-specify: acceptance says \"boots the real dsh in an isolated DSH_HOME/HOME and demonstrates the read-only spawn path succeeds\" — the implementer will find the cheapest real proof (maybe calling restrict via the harness API in a probe session). Good.\n\nRound 3:\nT6 verification — assignee Reviewer? Hmm, wait: I assigned RV1 (review of T3) to Architect, RV2 (T4) to Plan Reviewer, RV3 (T5) to Senior Engineer, T6 verification to Reviewer, T7 docs to Lead, T8 integration to Lead.\n\nT6:\nsubject: \"Independent verification of rename/delete end-to-end and the full gate sweep\"\nkind: verification\nassignee: Reviewer\ndependencies: [T3, T4, T5]\nobjective: \"Independently verify the rename/delete feature and the readonly fix against the real harness and the real gates — not against the implementers' summaries: run every gate, exercise the lifecycle in a sandbox HOME (rename preserves identity, delete archives, purge removes, in-use refusal fires, invalid/empty names are rejected with zero side effects and never touch the library root), boot the bundle in an isolated DSH_HOME, and capture evidence on disk.\"\nacceptance:\n- \"Every gate command from AGENTS.md §4 is run and its real output recorded: bun test packages, bun run typecheck, bun run test:qa, node scripts/verify-vendor.mjs, and a dsh --profile headless --dump-config boot in an isolated DSH_HOME.\"\n- \"A real sandbox-HOME exercise demonstrates: rename keeps persona/memory/note/uses/createdAt byte-identical and frees the old key; delete archives by default (restorable under .archive/) and purges on demand; list/match/service views agree with the filesystem immediately after each mutation.\"\n- \"Negative cases demonstrably pass: empty/CJK/punctuation-only name rejected before any filesystem call (library root untouched), unknown name errors with zero side effects, double delete errors, rename collision refused, rename/delete refused while the workmate is in use.\"\n- \"The web routes answer per the T1 contract (real boot: correct status codes for success/invalid/unknown/wrong-verb), and the readonly spawn fix is demonstrated against the real harness.\"\n- \"Every command result is recorded in commandsRun with exit codes and evidence; results.json/output.log written under evidence/workmate/rename-delete-verify/<timestamp>/; the real HOME and real ~/.dsh are never touched (sandbox asserted).\"\nverify: [\"bun test packages\", \"bun run typecheck\", \"bun run test:qa\", \"node scripts/verify-vendor.mjs\"]\ninScope: [\"evidence/workmate/rename-delete-verify/\"]\nHmm — a verification task's inScope: only evidence. Fine.\noutOfScope: [\"packages/** (verification must not fix; failures go back as findings)\"]\nnonGoals: [\"Fixing anything yourself\", \"Approving the implementation\"]\n\nRV1 review of T3 → Architect:\nsubject: \"Review: core rename/delete implementation (safety, identity, atomicity, races)\"\nkind: review, round 1, reviewedTaskId T3\nassignee: Architect\nobjective: \"Adversarially review the T3 implementation against the T1 contract on the points where a bug is expensive: root-safety, name sanitization, identity preservation, atomicity/partial failure, index consistency, spawn/reflect races, service immediacy, and the readonly deny correction — judging the LATEST implementation state, not the implementer's summary.\"\nacceptance:\n- \"Each T1 acceptance criterion is checked against the code and evidence with pass/fail and a concrete citation (file/line or command output).\"\n- \"The review explicitly attempts to falsify the invariants: I1 (name↔dir↔index bijection + root safety), I2 (rename is identity-preserving, never a re-instantiation), I3 (delete complete and all-or-nothing), I4 (deterministic cross-module binding outcome) — reporting any counterexample found.\"\n- \"Race/failure analysis covers: reflect after rename (old-dir resurrection), reflect/delete ordering, partial failure between the directory move and the metadata write, and the in-flight spawn liveness gate.\"\n- \"Verdict is pass only with zero unresolved blocker/high findings; otherwise fail with structured findings (id/severity/problem/requiredFix) that name the exact file and the required fix.\"\ninScope: [] hmm, review tasks don't need inScope. Only implementation/repair require inScope. Right: \"implementation/repair also need inScope and verify\". So reviews need objective + acceptance only.\n\nRV2 review of T4 → Plan Reviewer:\nsubject: \"Review: Workmates tab rename/delete UI, locale keys and rebuilt client\"\nkind: review, round 1, reviewedTaskId T4\nobjective: \"Review the T4 GUI work as a UI/UX and contract-conformance reviewer: does the tab actually let a human rename and safely delete a workmate, does every string exist in both zh and en with identical key sets, do the calls match the T1 wire contract exactly, and are the destructive path, refusal messaging and stale-selection handling sound?\"\nacceptance:\n- \"Wire-contract conformance is verified call-by-call (path/method/payload/response handling) against T1 — any drift is a finding.\"\n- \"Every new visible string is present in both dictionaries with identical key sets; a missing zh or en key is at least a high finding.\"\n- \"The destructive flow is human-safe: explicit confirmation, archive-first wording that matches the host semantics, no single-click irreversible action, and a readable message on an in-use refusal.\"\n- \"The sidebar design constraints hold: sidebar-only tab (no floating fallback / footer toggle), single:true, no stale detail polling or 404 loop after a mutation, and client.js is rebuilt so the shipped bundle contains the new UI.\"\n- \"Verdict is pass only with zero unresolved blocker/high findings; otherwise fail with structured findings naming the file and the required fix.\"\n\nRV3 review of T5 → Senior Engineer:\nsubject: \"Review: read-only deny-list defect fix and its real-harness proof\"\nkind: review, round 1, reviewedTaskId T5\nobjective: \"Review the T5 fix on correctness of the diagnosis and strength of the proof: does the deny list now name only tools this harness registers, is the mechanism robust rather than a one-off literal patch, does the regression actually exercise the real harness (not a stub), and did the fix avoid widening read-only authority?\"\nacceptance:\n- \"The root cause is confirmed against the harness's real tool registry and the fix is shown to remove it (before/after evidence cited).\"\n- \"The regression/QA case is evaluated for real-harness fidelity: it must fail on the pre-fix code or on the original deny list, and pass after — a stub-only assertion is a finding.\"\n- \"Read-only authority is verified not to have widened: the roles remain unable to write (no write/edit/apply_patch-style tool becomes available).\"\n- \"Verdict is pass only with zero unresolved blocker/high findings; otherwise fail with structured findings naming the file and the required fix.\"\n\nRound 4:\nT7 docs/sync → Lead, kind implementation, deps [T3, T4, T5]:\nsubject: \"Bilingual docs + QA surface sync for workmate rename/delete\"\nobjective: \"Sync every human-facing and agent-facing surface with the shipped feature: the pac