evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:1439:{"type":"assistant/chunk","seq":37662,"time":1789027128706,"data":{"turn":1,"step":16,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":0,"block":{"type":"reasoning","text":"Now round 3: T6 (verification, Reviewer, deps t3,t4,t5), RV1 (review of t3, Architect), RV2 (review of t4, Plan Reviewer), RV3 (review of t5, Senior Engineer).\n\nNote RV1 depends on t3, RV2 on t4, RV3 on t5.\n\nHmm — one consideration about RV3 assigned to Senior Engineer: the instruction says \"Do not approve your own implementation.\" Senior Engineer implements T3, not T5. So it's fine.\n\nBut wait: is it better to assign RV3 to Researcher (read-only)? The review needs to run commands and reason. Senior Engineer (pro) is strongest. Keep it.\n\nLet me create these four."}}}}
evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:1440:{"type":"assistant/chunk","seq":37663,"time":1789027128707,"data":{"turn":1,"step":16,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":1,"block":{"type":"tool-call","id":"call_00_GZcGN8oYgLSWwBTaUDbU6578","name":"agent_teams_create_task","arguments":"{\"acceptance\": [\"Every AGENTS.md §4 gate is run on the integrated tree with real output recorded: bun test packages, bun run typecheck, bun run test:qa, node scripts/verify-vendor.mjs, and a `dsh --profile headless --dump-config` boot in an isolated DSH_HOME.\", \"A real sandbox-HOME lifecycle exercise demonstrates, from the outside (tools/service/routes, not by reading the implementer's code): rename preserves persona/memory/note/uses/createdAt and frees the old key; delete archives by default and the instance is restorable from .archive/; purge removes it; list/match/service views agree with the filesystem immediately after every mutation.\", \"Negative cases demonstrably behave: invalid/empty/CJK/punctuation-only names are rejected before any filesystem call with the library root untouched; unknown names error with zero side effects; a repeated delete errors; a rename collision is refused; a rename or delete is refused while the workmate is in use.\", \"The new web routes answer per the T1 contract on a real boot (correct status codes for success, invalid body, unknown name, wrong verb) and the read-only spawn path is demonstrated to work against the real harness.\", \"Every command result is recorded in commandsRun with exit codes and evidence, and results.json/output.log are written under evidence/workmate/rename-delete-verify/<timestamp>/.\", \"The real HOME and the real ~/.dsh are never touched (the sandbox path is asserted in the evidence).\"], \"assignee\": \"Reviewer\", \"dependencies\": [\"t3\", \"t4\", \"t5\"], \"description\": \"Verify the delivered feature independently. Do not trust the implementers' summaries: reproduce from the real artifacts (source, built dist, real dsh boot, sandboxed filesystem) and treat any mismatch between a claim and the observed behaviour as a finding you report — you do not fix code yourself.\\n\\nMethod that fits this repo: follow the dsh-qa discipline (skills/dsh-qa) — an isolated DSH_HOME and HOME per run, credentials copied once into the sandbox, sandbox paths asserted, evidence written under evidence/<domain>/<slug>/<timestamp>/{result.json,output.log}, and long-lived MCP children run with stdio to files rather than pipes. The workmate library lives under $HOME, so every lifecycle exercise MUST run with HOME pointing at a sandbox and the evidence must show it.\\n\\nPay particular attention to the cases where a naive implementation is quietly wrong: a name that sanitizes to an empty string (wmDir(\\\"\\\") is the library root itself), a rename that loses evolved memory or bumps uses, a delete that leaves the index stale or removes only part of the instance, a mutation racing an in-flight spawn, and a GUI-visible route that answers 200 on an unknown name.\\n\\nIf something fails, report it as a failed verification with the exact command, exit code and output — the scheduler turns that into a repair loop. Do not edit implementation files.\", \"inScope\": [\"evidence/workmate/rename-delete-verify/\"], \"nonGoals\": [\"Fixing anything yourself\", \"Approving the implementation\", \"Editing package sources, docs or QA scripts\"], \"objective\": \"Independently verify the shipped rename/delete feature and the read-only deny fix against the REAL harness and the REAL gates: run every gate, exercise the full lifecycle and its negative cases with HOME pointed at a sandbox, boot the bundle in an isolated DSH_HOME, confirm the web routes and the read-only spawn path behave as contracted, and leave reproducible evidence on disk.\", \"outOfScope\": [\"packages/\", \"AGENTS.md\", \"docs/\", \"skills/dsh-qa/scripts/\"], \"round\": 1, \"subject\": \"Independent verification: rename/delete end-to-end, negative cases, full gate sweep\", \"verify\": [\"bun test packages\", \"bun run typecheck\", \"bun run test:qa\", \"node scripts/verify-vendor.mjs\"]}"}}}}
evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:1441:{"type":"assistant/chunk","seq":37664,"time":1789027128707,"data":{"turn":1,"step":16,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":2,"block":{"type":"tool-call","id":"call_01_kDABfw1qBMlPEvv5WKb72164","name":"agent_teams_create_task","arguments":"{\"acceptance\": [\"Every T1 acceptance criterion is checked against the shipped code and the on-disk evidence, each with an explicit pass/fail and a concrete citation (file/line or command output).\", \"The review actively tries to falsify the invariants and reports any counterexample: name↔dir↔index bijection and root safety; rename is identity-preserving and never a re-instantiation; delete is complete and all-or-nothing; the cross-module binding outcome is deterministic with no state where directory, index.json, service view and team-member backing disagree.\", \"Race and failure analysis covers: a reflect landing after a rename (old-directory resurrection), reflect/delete ordering, partial failure between the directory move and the metadata write, and whether the in-use refusal gate actually closes the in-flight race.\", \"The implementation is judged against the LATEST state of the code, including any repair that already ran — not against the first submitted version.\", \"Verdict is pass only with zero unresolved blocker/high findings; otherwise the task fails with structured findings (id, severity, problem, requiredFix) naming the exact file and the required correction.\"], \"assignee\": \"Architect\", \"dependencies\": [\"t3\"], \"description\": \"Adversarially review the core implementation (task t3) on the axes where a mistake is expensive and hard to see. Read the T1 contract first, then the actual code in packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/src/index.ts, the tests and the evidence — and treat the implementer's summary as a claim to be checked, not as fact.\\n\\nSpecific things to attack:\\n- sanitizeName as the single key algorithm: can any input reach a filesystem call before validation? What exactly happens for an empty sanitized name, a CJK name, a punctuation-only name, a name differing only by case, and a name colliding after sanitization?\\n- rename identity: is the evolved instance really moved rather than recreated? Are persona/memory/note content, the caps, uses, lastTask and createdAt preserved? Is the note self-reference and the continuity metadata consistent with the new name?\\n- delete: archive-first behaviour, hidden-from-list/match, restorability, purge, index-key removal, and all-or-nothing failure behaviour.\\n- the in-use gate: does it really prevent the spawn/reflect race, or does it only shift the window? Is the chosen detection mechanism sound in this harness, and does it avoid writing to another plugin's state?\\n- index.json: it has no reader today — judge whether the rename/delete handling leaves it consistent and whether the contract's treatment of it is defensible.\\n- the workmate-side READONLY_DENY correction (part of t3): does it actually fix readonly spawn, and does it keep read-only authority intact?\\n\\nYou are a read-only role: report findings, do not edit files. A 'pass' verdict with an unverified criterion is worse than a 'needs_revision' with a real finding.\", \"nonGoals\": [\"Editing any file\", \"Reviewing the GUI (t4) or the roles-plugin fix (t5)\", \"Re-litigating decisions the user already made\"], \"objective\": \"Adversarially review the core rename/delete implementation against the T1 contract and the four invariants: root safety and name validation, identity-preserving rename, complete all-or-nothing delete, and deterministic behaviour under races and partial failure. Judge the latest code state and cite evidence for every claim.\", \"reviewedTaskId\": \"t3\", \"round\": 1, \"subject\": \"Review: core rename/delete implementation (safety, identity, atomicity, races)\"}"}}}}
evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:1444:{"type":"assistant/message","seq":37667,"time":1789027128709,"data":{"turn":1,"step":16,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"reasoning","text":"Now round 3: T6 (verification, Reviewer, deps t3,t4,t5), RV1 (review of t3, Architect), RV2 (review of t4, Plan Reviewer), RV3 (review of t5, Senior Engineer).\n\nNote RV1 depends on t3, RV2 on t4, RV3 on t5.\n\nHmm — one consideration about RV3 assigned to Senior Engineer: the instruction says \"Do not approve your own implementation.\" Senior Engineer implements T3, not T5. So it's fine.\n\nBut wait: is it better to assign RV3 to Researcher (read-only)? The review needs to run commands and reason. Senior Engineer (pro) is strongest. Keep it.\n\nLet me create these four."},{"type":"tool-call","id":"call_00_GZcGN8oYgLSWwBTaUDbU6578","name":"agent_teams_create_task","arguments":"{\"acceptance\": [\"Every AGENTS.md §4 gate is run on the integrated tree with real output recorded: bun test packages, bun run typecheck, bun run test:qa, node scripts/verify-vendor.mjs, and a `dsh --profile headless --dump-config` boot in an isolated DSH_HOME.\", \"A real sandbox-HOME lifecycle exercise demonstrates, from the outside (tools/service/routes, not by reading the implementer's code): rename preserves persona/memory/note/uses/createdAt and frees the old key; delete archives by default and the instance is restorable from .archive/; purge removes it; list/match/service views agree with the filesystem immediately after every mutation.\", \"Negative cases demonstrably behave: invalid/empty/CJK/punctuation-only names are rejected before any filesystem call with the library root untouched; unknown names error with zero side effects; a repeated delete errors; a rename collision is refused; a rename or delete is refused while the workmate is in use.\", \"The new web routes answer per the T1 contract on a real boot (correct status codes for success, invalid body, unknown name, wrong verb) and the read-only spawn path is demonstrated to work against the real harness.\", \"Every command result is recorded in commandsRun with exit codes and evidence, and results.json/output.log are written under evidence/workmate/rename-delete-verify/<timestamp>/.\", \"The real HOME and the real ~/.dsh are never touched (the sandbox path is asserted in the evidence).\"], \"assignee\": \"Reviewer\", \"dependencies\": [\"t3\", \"t4\", \"t5\"], \"description\": \"Verify the delivered feature independently. Do not trust the implementers' summaries: reproduce from the real artifacts (source, built dist, real dsh boot, sandboxed filesystem) and treat any mismatch between a claim and the observed behaviour as a finding you report — you do not fix code yourself.\\n\\nMethod that fits this repo: follow the dsh-qa discipline (skills/dsh-qa) — an isolated DSH_HOME and HOME per run, credentials copied once into the sandbox, sandbox paths asserted, evidence written under evidence/<domain>/<slug>/<timestamp>/{result.json,output.log}, and long-lived MCP children run with stdio to files rather than pipes. The workmate library lives under $HOME, so every lifecycle exercise MUST run with HOME pointing at a sandbox and the evidence must show it.\\n\\nPay particular attention to the cases where a naive implementation is quietly wrong: a name that sanitizes to an empty string (wmDir(\\\"\\\") is the library root itself), a rename that loses evolved memory or bumps uses, a delete that leaves the index stale or removes only part of the instance, a mutation racing an in-flight spawn, and a GUI-visible route that answers 200 on an unknown name.\\n\\nIf something fails, report it as a failed verification with the exact command, exit code and output — the scheduler turns that into a repair loop. Do not edit implementation files.\", \"inScope\": [\"evidence/workmate/rename-delete-verify/\"], \"nonGoals\": [\"Fixing anything yourself\", \"Approving the implementation\", \"Editing package sources, docs or QA scripts\"], \"objective\": \"Independently verify the shipped rename/delete feature and the read-only deny fix against the REAL harness and the REAL gates: run every gate, exercise the full lifecycle and its negative cases with HOME pointed at a sandbox, boot the bundle in an isolated DSH_HOME, confirm the web routes and the read-only spawn path behave as contracted, and leave reproducible evidence on disk.\", \"outOfScope\": [\"packages/\", \"AGENTS.md\", \"docs/\", \"skills/dsh-qa/scripts/\"], \"round\": 1, \"subject\": \"Independent verification: rename/delete end-to-end, negative cases, full gate sweep\", \"verify\": [\"bun test packages\", \"bun run typecheck\", \"bun run test:qa\", \"node scripts/verify-vendor.mjs\"]}"},{"type":"tool-call","id":"call_01_kDABfw1qBMlPEvv5WKb72164","name":"agent_teams_create_task","arguments":"{\"acceptance\": [\"Every T1 acceptance criterion is checked against the shipped code and the on-disk evidence, each with an explicit pass/fail and a concrete citation (file/line or command output).\", \"The review actively tries to falsify the invariants and reports any counterexample: name↔dir↔index bijection and root safety; rename is identity-preserving and never a re-instantiation; delete is complete and all-or-nothing; the cross-module binding outcome is deterministic with no state where directory, index.json, service view and team-member backing disagree.\", \"Race and failure analysis covers: a reflect landing after a rename (old-directory resurrection), reflect/delete ordering, partial failure between the directory move and the metadata write, and whether the in-use refusal gate actually closes the in-flight race.\", \"The implementation is judged against the LATEST state of the code, including any repair that already ran — not against the first submitted version.\", \"Verdict is pass only with zero unresolved blocker/high findings; otherwise the task fails with structured findings (id, severity, problem, requiredFix) naming the exact file and the required correction.\"], \"assignee\": \"Architect\", \"dependencies\": [\"t3\"], \"description\": \"Adversarially review the core implementation (task t3) on the axes where a mistake is expensive and hard to see. Read the T1 contract first, then the actual code in packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/src/index.ts, the tests and the evidence — and treat the implementer's summary as a claim to be checked, not as fact.\\n\\nSpecific things to attack:\\n- sanitizeName as the single key algorithm: can any input reach a filesystem call before validation? What exactly happens for an empty sanitized name, a CJK name, a punctuation-only name, a name differing only by case, and a name colliding after sanitization?\\n- rename identity: is the evolved instance really moved rather than recreated? Are persona/memory/note content, the caps, uses, lastTask and createdAt preserved? Is the note self-reference and the continuity metadata consistent with the new name?\\n- delete: archive-first behaviour, hidden-from-list/match, restorability, purge, index-key removal, and all-or-nothing failure behaviour.\\n- the in-use gate: does it really prevent the spawn/reflect race, or does it only shift the window? Is the chosen detection mechanism sound in this harness, and does it avoid writing to another plugin's state?\\n- index.json: it has no reader today — judge whether the rename/delete handling leaves it consistent and whether the contract's treatment of it is defensible.\\n- the workmate-side READONLY_DENY correction (part of t3): does it actually fix readonly spawn, and does it keep read-only authority intact?\\n\\nYou are a read-only role: report findings, do not edit files. A 'pass' verdict with an unverified criterion is worse than a 'needs_revision' with a real finding.\", \"nonGoals\": [\"Editing any file\", \"Reviewing the GUI (t4) or the roles-plugin fix (t5)\", \"Re-litigating decisions the user already made\"], \"objective\": \"Adversarially review the core rename/delete implementation against the T1 contract and the four invariants: root safety and name validation, identity-preserving rename, complete all-or-nothing delete, and deterministic behaviour under races and partial failure. Judge the latest code state and cite evidence for every claim.\", \"reviewedTaskId\": \"t3\", \"round\": 1, \"subject\": \"Review: core rename/delete implementation (safety, identity, atomicity, races)\"}"}],"source":{"kind":"model","provider":"deepseek-official","model":"deepseek-v4-flash"},"id":"0f045d55-06a9-4ced-8b01-870cf0484fbf"},"usage":{"inputTokens":245,"outputTokens":2031,"totalTokens":110308,"cacheReadTokens":108032,"reasoningTokens":155}},"sourceEventSeqs":[[35845,37666]],"surfaceOp":"append"}
