evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:1600:{"type":"assistant/chunk","seq":42007,"time":1789027151907,"data":{"turn":1,"step":18,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":0,"block":{"type":"reasoning","text":"Now the last two: t10 (docs/sync, Lead, deps t3, t4, t5) and t11 (integration, Lead, deps t6, t7, t8, t9, t10, t2).\n\nt10: docs — kind implementation with inScope+verify. Wait, docs is not really \"implementation\" but the kind requires inScope + verify; I have both. Alternatively kind=work with explicit acceptance in description. Hmm: if I use kind=implementation, the system might expect a review. Not required. But quality-wise, a doc task as \"implementation\" with verify commands is fine and gives the gate a contract.\n\nActually let me use kind=work for docs to keep semantics honest (docs aren't code implementation) but still list acceptance in the description. Hmm — but then there's no formal acceptance field. The DAG protocol says quality kinds need contracts; `work` needs only a subject. Given quality mode, I prefer the contract to exist. I'll use kind=implementation since the task does change shipped files (docs + QA scripts) and has real verify commands. That's defensible.\n\nt11 integration: kind=integration with objective+acceptance. deps: t6, t7, t8, t9, t10, t2.\n\nHmm — integration depends on t9 (review of t5, owned by Senior Engineer). Note t9's assignee Senior Engineer also owns t3. That's fine.\n\nWait, there's a scheduling nuance: RV3 (t9) depends on t5, and is assigned to Senior Engineer who owns t3. If t5 finishes while t3 is still running, t9 sits pending until t3 completes. Fine.\n\nAnother nuance: t6 (verification, Reviewer) depends on t3, t4, t5. And t10 (docs, Lead) depends on t3, t4, t5. Parallel — but both may touch different files: t6 only writes evidence; t10 writes docs + skills/dsh-qa/scripts/{workmate-library,web-client-adapt}.mjs + SKILL.md. T5 (Deep Worker) also touches skills/dsh-qa/scripts (new case) + SKILL.md. t10 depends on t5 so ordering is safe (t5 completes first). But note t6 runs `bun run test:qa` (the self-test sweep over skills/dsh-qa/scripts/*.mjs) while t10 edits those scripts — if t10 edits while t6 runs the sweep, the result could be inconsistent. Both run in parallel after t3/t4/t5.\n\nHmm. That's a real (if small) hazard. Options: make t6 depend on t10 too, or make t10 depend on t6. Better: t6 (verification) should verify the FINAL tree, including the docs/QA-script sync. But docs don't affect code behavior. However `test:qa` sweeps the QA scripts — which t10 modifies. So t6's sweep could fail on t10's in-flight edits.\n\nCleanest: t10 (docs/sync) depends on t5 (done) and runs in parallel; t6 depends on t10 as well → verification runs on the synced tree. But then verification waits for docs → serializes the critical path a lot (docs after all impl, then verification, then integration).\n\nAlternative: t10 (docs) depends on t6, t3, t4, t5 → docs after verification. Then t11 integration depends on t10 + reviews. That's a clean sequence: impl (parallel) → verify + reviews (parallel) → docs/sync → integration. And the \"verification of the final tree\" isn't strictly true for docs, but docs are checked by integration's consistency pass (t11's acceptance includes code↔docs consistency). And t10 itself runs `bun run test:qa` in its verify.\n\nHmm, but that makes verification not cover the QA script changes t10 makes. t10's own verify covers test:qa. Acceptable: t11 integration re-runs the gate sweep on the integrated tree (acceptance includes final gate sweep with real output). Good — the final tree IS gate-swept by t11.\n\nDecision: t10 deps = [t3, t4, t5, t6] — docs after the verified state (docs must describe what was verified). Then t11 deps = [t6, t7, t8, t9, t10, t2].\n\nLet me write them.\n\nFor t10's inScope, list docs paths + QA scripts. Also `docs/user-guide.md` etc.\n\nLet me also double check which docs mention workmate to scope precisely: docs/development.md, docs/architecture.md (+zh), docs/feature-audit.md (+zh), docs/user-guide.md (+zh), docs/index.md (+zh), README.md/README.zh-CN.md, packages/mpd-bundle/README(+zh), packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/README(+zh), packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/README(+zh), AGENTS.md. Some are process artifacts exempt from bilingual (feature-audit? it's in docs/ and has a zh version already). I'll scope inScope broadly: docs/, AGENTS.md, README.md, README.zh-CN.md, packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/README*.md, packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/README*.md, packages/mpd-bundle/README*.md, skills/dsh-qa/scripts/workmate-library.mjs, skills/dsh-qa/scripts/web-client-adapt.mjs, skills/dsh-qa/SKILL.md, evidence path.\n\nThat's a lot of inScope entries but fine. Let me trim: docs/, AGENTS.md, README.md, README.zh-CN.md, packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/README.md, packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/README.zh-CN.md, packages/mpd-bundle/README.md, packages/mpd-bundle/README.zh-CN.md, packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/README.md, packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/README.zh-CN.md, skills/dsh-qa/scripts/workmate-library.mjs, skills/dsh-qa/scripts/web-client-adapt.mjs, skills/dsh-qa/SKILL.md, evidence/workmate/rename-delete-docs/.\n\nAlso the preset/profile protocol string in packages/mpd-bundle/cordis.patch.yml + presets/mpd/agent.cordis.yml mention workmate tools — momus flagged them. Include presets/ and packages/mpd-bundle/cordis.patch.yml in inScope. Hmm, cordis.patch.yml is the bundle patch — editing it needs care and a boot check. Let me include presets/ + packages/mpd-bundle/ (patch) with a note: only the workmate tool enumerations if they exist; verify with a boot check. Add verify: `dsh --profile headless --dump-config` isolated? That requires a sandbox DSH_HOME — hard as a plain verify command. The QA skill has scripts for that. I'll add \"node skills/dsh-qa/scripts/bundle-lifecycle.mjs --self-test\" as a verify (it's a self-test) — but the real boot is t6's job. Fine.\n\nLet me write t10 and t11."}}}}
evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:1601:{"type":"assistant/chunk","seq":42008,"time":1789027151908,"data":{"turn":1,"step":18,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":1,"block":{"type":"tool-call","id":"call_00_jBb1oz0jH5g4ZyGxrd0S8930","name":"agent_teams_create_task","arguments":"{\"acceptance\": [\"packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/README.md and README.zh-CN.md are both updated to document the new tools, routes and semantics (archive-first delete, in-use refusal, ASCII-only naming, rename cascade) with the language switch link directly under each title; neither version is left stale.\", \"Every other human-facing doc that enumerates workmate capability is synced in BOTH languages in the same change (README pair at the root, docs/user-guide pair, docs/index pair, packages/mpd-bundle and packages/mpd-bundle-plugin README pairs) — the repo's binding bilingual rule means a change to one language version without the other is an unfinished change.\", \"AGENTS.md carries the new tool names in the workmate tool surface, an updated workmate glossary entry, and troubleshooting rows for the new failure modes (refused because the workmate is in use, restoring an archived instance, rejected invalid/CJK name), all in English.\", \"Any preset or bundle-patch text that enumerates the workmate tools is synced, and the QA scripts that pin the workmate route literals (skills/dsh-qa/scripts/workmate-library.mjs, skills/dsh-qa/scripts/web-client-adapt.mjs) assert the new routes, with SKILL.md's case table matching reality and all self-tests passing.\", \"Docs claim nothing the shipped code does not do: every documented tool name, argument, route, status code and refusal semantic is checked against the shipped sources and the verification evidence rather than copied from the contract draft.\"], \"assignee\": \"Lead\", \"dependencies\": [\"t3\", \"t4\", \"t5\", \"t6\"], \"description\": \"Sync every documentation and QA-assertion surface