evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:1643:{"type":"assistant/chunk","seq":42992,"time":1789027159252,"data":{"turn":1,"step":19,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":0,"block":{"type":"reasoning","text":"Oops — I accidentally wrote \"packages/mpd-social-plugin/\" in outOfScope (a hallucinated package name). Harmless (it just lists a non-existent path as out of scope), but let me fix it with agent_teams_edit_plan? Editing a staged plan is allowed via agent_teams_edit_plan (update_task). Since the team is staged, I can update the task. Let me do it as part of a final edit_plan batch, or just leave it? It's a cosmetic error but sloppy; an outOfScope entry naming a non-existent package is confusing in the plan the user reads. Let me fix it in one edit_plan operation... Actually for a staged team, `agent_teams_create_task` created real tasks; edit_plan ops include update_task with task_id. Does update_task support replacing outOfScope? The operation fields: action, task_id, subject, description, assignee, dependencies... There's no outOfScope field in the edit_plan schema! Fields: action, assignee, dependencies, description, member_name, provider, model, reasoning_effort, role, subject, task_id, execution_prompt. So no outOfScope replacement. I could remove and recreate the task, but that's churn and risks id changes.\n\nAlternatively leave it — it's harmless. Actually, cleanest: it's a minor cosmetic inconsistency in a field the user may read. I'll leave it and mention nothing? Hmm, better to keep the plan clean. I could recreate t10 with the corrected outOfScope: remove_task t10 then add_task (new id t12) — but dependencies of t11 (which I haven't created yet) point to t10's id... I haven't created t11 yet! So I can: remove t10, add t10-corrected as a new task, then create t11 with the new id.\n\nHmm, is that worth it? The plan quality matters. `outOfScope` listing a non-existent package could confuse an implementer (\"is there a social plugin?\"). Small confusion risk. Let me just do the clean thing: use edit_plan with remove_task + add_task (new id) and then create t11 referencing the new id.\n\nActually wait — simpler alternative: keep t10 and simply not worry; when creating t11 I'll reference t10. The outOfScope entry is harmless noise but the plan is what the user reviews; a bogus path is a credibility ding. I'll fix it.\n\nPlan: agent_teams_edit_plan([{action:\"remove_task\", task_id:\"t10\"}, {action:\"add_task\", subject:...}]) — but add_task in edit_plan: does it support kind/acceptance/deliverables/inScope/verify/objective/round? The schema for edit_plan operations is limited: action, subject, description, assignee, dependencies, ... no kind/objective/inScope. So an add_task via edit_plan can't carry a contract → can't create a quality task there. It would create a legacy `work` task without the contract. Not good.\n\nSo: remove t10 via edit_plan, then recreate it properly via agent_teams_create_task (full contract). Let me do that: two calls — edit_plan(remove t10) then create_task. But edit_plan on a staged plan is allowed now (it's the sanctioned revision path). Hmm, the captain protocol says edit_plan is used when the user returns to chat to request changes. Using it now for a self-correction is fine mechanically.\n\nHonestly, the simplest robust option: leave t10 as is and move on — the cost/benefit favors momentum. But a bogus package path in the approved plan is exactly the kind of sloppiness a reviewer would flag...\n\nLet me weigh once more: is outOfScope with a non-existent path harmful during execution? No: it just means \"don't touch this\", and nobody will. It's cosmetic. But it's also visible in the Web plan UI.\n\nDecision: fix it via remove+recreate (cheap, 2 calls, no dependencies broken since t11 doesn't exist yet). Actually — careful: does remove_task work on a staged plan whose task was just created? Should be fine.\n\nHmm, one risk: the id may be reused (t10) or incremented (t11). If the new one gets a different id, I must use it in t11's deps. Fine.\n\nLet me do it."}}}}
