evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:1822:{"type":"reasoning-chunks","seq0":47476,"time0":1789027188319,"data":{"turn":1,"step":23,"index":0,"dt":[1,0,0,46,1,0,0,11,0,1,13,0,1,19,0,25,1,32,1,0,0,0,1,0,0,36,11],"texts":[":"," I","'ll"," rebuild"," t","4","-t","12","."," If"," not",","," the"," display"," is"," mapping"," quality"," kinds"," to"," \"","work","\","," and"," I","'ll"," verify"," differently"," ("]}}
evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:1831:{"type":"assistant/chunk","seq":47607,"time":1789027189062,"data":{"turn":1,"step":23,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":0,"block":{"type":"reasoning","text":"One concern: the task `kind` did not persist as I specified! All tasks show as generic:\n- t1 [pending] requirements r1 ✓ (kind requirements shown)\n- t3 shows \"[pending] work r1\" — I specified kind=implementation but it shows \"work\". Hmm!\n\nLook: \"t3 [pending] work r1 attempt 0 Implement workmate rename + delete core...\" — kind is `work`, not `implementation`. Same for t4, t5, t6 (verification → shows \"work\"), t7/t8/t9 (review → \"work\"), t11, t12.\n\nBut t1 shows \"requirements r1\". So kind=implementation/verification/review/integration got degraded to `work`?? Or the status display collapses kinds? Let me check: t1 displays \"requirements r1\" so the display does show kinds. So my kinds were NOT applied except for t1.\n\nWait — did I pass kind correctly? For t3 I passed kind: \"implementation\"... Let me re-check my call: yes I passed `\"kind\": \"implementation\"` along with round 1. Hmm, but the response only echoed the subject. And for t6 I passed kind \"verification\", t7/t8/t9 kind \"review\" with reviewedTaskId.\n\nHmm, the display might map unknown/unsupported kinds to \"work\". This matters a lot: quality gates (verdict=pass requirements, review verdicts, repair loops, integration contracts) only apply to quality kinds. If they're all `work`, then reviews complete without verdict gates, and the whole quality-mode plan collapses.\n\nThis is a real problem I must fix. Options:\n1. Check whether the plugin actually stored the kind — maybe the status display is lossy. Let me inspect the team state? The protocol forbids inspecting .agent-teams state files *to revise a plan*. Hmm: \"Never inspect or edit .agent-teams state files or plugin source code to revise a plan.\" That's a binding rule. So I can't peek at the state file.\n\n2. Use agent_teams_edit_plan with update_task — but its operation schema has no `kind` field either. So I can't fix kinds via edit_plan.\n\n3. Remove and recreate the affected tasks via create_task (which took kind fine for t1). That's the reliable path: remove t3..t12 and recreate with kinds. That's 10 remove ops + 10 create calls. Ugh, but correctness of the quality gates matters.\n\nWait — let me first double-check that create_task actually accepted the kinds. Maybe the tool result for t1 displayed differently because the STATUS formatter labels requirements/review kinds... Let me test cheaply: create a throwaway task with kind=verification? That pollutes the plan.\n\nAlternative cheaper test: the displayed task line format is \"[pending] <kind> r<round> attempt N <subject>\". t1 shows \"requirements r1\". For t3 I gave kind=implementation round=1 → shows \"work r1\". So implementation → displayed as \"work\"? That suggests a mapping where only some kinds are recognized... but the enum explicitly includes implementation, verification, review, integration, repair, work, requirements.\n\nHmm, maybe the display maps quality kinds to their \"family\"? E.g., maybe the plugin normalizes implementation→work for display? Unlikely.\n\nLet me look at the plugin's task model code — is reading the plugin source allowed? The rule forbids inspecting plugin source \"to revise a plan\". Reading it to understand the kind semantics is... the rule's intent is to prevent hacking the plan through state/source. Hmm. It says \"Never inspect or edit .agent-teams state files or plugin source code to revise a plan.\" I'd be inspecting to verify whether kinds persisted. That's borderline but arguably within the spirit of a different purpose (understanding the tool contract, not revising the plan through backdoors).\n\nSafer alternative: use the tool's own reporting. Let me re-create ONE task with kind=implementation and see how status displays it. If it also shows \"work\", then the mapping is display-only, and the original tasks are fine... but that's a weak conclusion.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider: maybe the kind WAS stored, and the status line prints kind only for quality kinds that are in a specific set — and 'requirements' prints because it's a quality kind. Both are quality kinds though...\n\nLet me test empirically with a probe task that I then remove: create kind=verification with subject \"PROBE — kind display\" and no assignee, check status, then remove. Two extra calls + 1 status. That's clean and doesn't rely on source inspection. But it also creates/removes noise... acceptable and quick.\n\nActually — better idea: re-create t3 (implementation) properly right away (remove + create), and use the status output of the recreated t3 as the probe. If the recreated t3 shows \"implementation r1\", then the first attempt failed to pass kind (maybe because I combined kind with `verify`/`inScope`? no...) and I must rebuild all tasks. If it shows \"work r1\" again, then the display is lossy/mapping and I should think differently.\n\nHmm, but why would the first t3 call have dropped the kind? Possibly the tool validates kind and silently falls back to \"work\" when a required contract field is missing... For implementation, required: objective, acceptance, inScope, verify — I provided all four for t3. Hmm, but maybe the plugin requires `objective` AND `acceptance` as non-empty arrays... they were non-empty.\n\nOR: maybe the plugin requires `deliverables` for some kinds? Not stated.\n\nOR: maybe the JSON array I passed for `acceptance` was fine, but `kind` must be paired with `round`? I passed round 1.\n\nHonestly, empirical probing is the way. Let me remove t3 and recreate it identically, then check status.\n\nActually — wait. Let me look at the create response for t1 vs t3: t1: 'Task \"...\" created as t1 (status pending, assigned to Planner).' t3: 'Task \"...\" created as t3 (status pending, assigned to Senior Engineer).' No kind echo. So no info.\n\nLet me probe: remove t3, recreate with the same contract, then status. If it lands as \"implementation\", great: I'll rebuild t4-t12. If not, the display is mapping quality kinds to \"work\", and I'll verify differently (e.g. by checking whether a review task's completion demands a verdict — that only happens at runtime).\n\nLet me proceed with the probe."}}}}
