evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:1926:{"type":"tool-call-chunks","seq0":49742,"time0":1789027205294,"data":{"turn":1,"step":26,"index":2,"dt":[0,0,1,3,0,0,0,0,16,4,0,1,0,1,0,0,11,1,1,1,0,3,7,0,0,0,6,0,1,0,0,0,1,0,0,0],"id":"call_00_dVwfm3uAkP8WLnDvQO5G9590","name":"agent_teams_create_task","args":["les","-plugin","/","\"]",", ","\"","round","\"",": ","1",", ","\"","subject","\"",": ","\"","PRO","BE"," ","2"," kind"," persistence"," without"," assign","ee"," (","capt","ain"," diagnostic",")","\"",", ","\"","verify","\"",": ","[\""]}}
evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:1928:{"type":"assistant/chunk","seq":49786,"time":1789027205356,"data":{"turn":1,"step":26,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":0,"block":{"type":"reasoning","text":"The probe shows \"verification r1\" — so the kind IS preserved when passed. Which means my earlier calls for t3..t12 did NOT get their kinds applied. \n\nWait, what's different? For t13 I passed only: acceptance, description, kind, objective, round, subject. For t3 I passed: acceptance, assignee, dependencies, description, inScope, nonGoals, objective, outOfScope, round, subject, verify, kind... \n\nHmm! Maybe the presence of `assignee` + `dependencies`?? No... More likely: the kind was dropped because of a validation fallback — e.g. when `deliverables`/`nonGoals`/`inScope`/`verify` are present but something is malformed? \n\nLook at differences:\n- t1 (requirements, PRESERVED): I passed acceptance, coverageOf, deliverables, description, nonGoals, objective, round, subject. NO assignee/dependencies/inScope/verify/outOfScope. → preserved.\n- t3 (implementation, DEGRADED): passed acceptance, assignee, dependencies, description, inScope, nonGoals, objective, outOfScope, round, subject, verify. → degraded.\n- t5 (implementation, DEGRADED): acceptance, assignee, description, inScope, nonGoals, objective, outOfScope, round, subject, verify. → degraded.\n- t6 (verification, DEGRADED): acceptance, assignee, dependencies, description, inScope, nonGoals, objective, outOfScope, round, subject, verify. → degraded.\n- t7 (review, DEGRADED): acceptance, assignee, dependencies, description, nonGoals, objective, reviewedTaskId, round, subject. → degraded.\n- t13 (verification, PRESERVED): acceptance, description, kind, objective, round, subject. → preserved.\n\nCommon factor for degraded: `assignee` present! t1 has no assignee (assigned via... wait, t1 shows \"→ Planner\", so t1 HAS an assignee — I passed assignee:\"Planner\"? Let me re-check my t1 call... I passed `\"assignee\": \"Planner\"`? Looking at my t1 call: I included `\"assignee\": \"Planner\"`? Hmm — in my t1 create call I did NOT include assignee! Let me re-read: parameters were subject, kind, round, coverageOf, objective, description, acceptance, deliverables, nonGoals. No assignee! But the status shows t1 → Planner. Interesting — so the plugin AUTO-ASSIGNED t1 to Planner (because kind=requirements and profile has a requirements role mapping?). Hmm, that's a nice touch: quality kinds map to roles.\n\nYet t3 shows \"→ Senior Engineer\" which I did pass explicitly.\n\nSo the difference: t1 and t13 lack `assignee`; t3..t12 have `assignee`. Hmm, that seems like a weird bug. Unless... it's `deliverables`+`nonGoals`? t1 had both, t13 had neither → both preserved.\n\nLet me find the actual common factor differently. Degraded set (t3,t4,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9,t11,t12) all have: assignee, description, nonGoals, objective, round, subject, acceptance. Preserved set (t1,t13) both lack `assignee`.\n\nt2 (kind=work explicitly) — shown as \"work attempt 0\" (no r1) — I passed kind work, no round... hmm, \"work attempt 0\" without r1.\n\nSo it looks like quality kinds + explicit assignee → degraded to work? That seems arbitrary. More plausible: the plugin treats an explicitly assigned quality task as... no.\n\nOR maybe: the degrade happens when BOTH `assignee` and quality `kind` are given, because the plugin validates that the assignee is an *active* member (they're all unspawned/staged) and falls back? But t1 got auto-assigned to Planner successfully...\n\nHonestly, the mechanism doesn't matter as much as the fix: I need kinds to stick. Empirical approach: recreate one task WITHOUT assignee but with kind=implementation + contract, see if it stays \"implementation\". If yes, the fix is to omit `assignee` and let the plugin auto-assign (or assign via edit_plan update_task assignee, which supports assignee!).\n\nThat's a clean workaround: create quality tasks without assignee (kind preserved), then use agent_teams_edit_plan update_task {task_id, assignee} to assign.\n\nBut first, let me confirm the hypothesis with one probe: create kind=implementation + contract + no assignee → status.\n\nLet me do that now. I'll create a probe that I'd actually want anyway: recreate t3 as a proper implementation task? But t3's id is taken (still exists, degraded). I need to remove the degraded ones first (dependency-ordered edits: update dependents' dependency lists, then remove).\n\nPlan for the rebuild:\n1. Probe: create a new implementation-kind task without assignee → check status.\n2. If preserved: rebuild the whole DAG:\n   a. edit_plan: for tasks t3..t12: update dependencies to remove references? Actually the simplest order: remove tasks in reverse dependency order (dependents first):\n      - remove t12 (no dependents)\n      - remove t11 (no dependents now)\n      - remove t6, t7, t8, t9 (depend on t3/t4/t5)\n      - remove t3, t4, t5\n      - remove t2? t2 is kind=work and correct as-is (no assignee? I did pass assignee:\"Explorer\" for t2 and it shows \"work attempt 0\" — kind work is correct, and the display shows no round. Fine, keep t2. But wait: does t2 as kind=work need its assignee? It has one. OK.)\n      - t1 stays (requirements, correct, auto-assigned to Planner).\n   b. recreate t3..t12 with kinds, without assignee, then assign via edit_plan update_task.\n   c. new ids will be t14... etc. That's fine.\n3. Remove the probe t13.\n\nHmm, this is a lot of churn (10 recreations). Is it worth it? YES — quality gates are the core of the value here: without kind=review, a review can \"complete\" without a verdict; without kind=implementation, no contract enforcement; without integration/review gates, the plan is just a to-do list. The whole protocol depends on it.\n\nBut before the big rebuild, let me confirm the hypothesis precisely with ONE probe, because if the cause is something else (e.g. `nonGoals` or `inScope`), removing `assignee` won't help.\n\nProbe design: create kind=implementation with ALL the fields I used for t3 EXCEPT assignee and dependencies (deps are ids that will change anyway). Then check.\n\nLet me do it."}}}}
