evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:2033:{"type":"assistant/chunk","seq":51988,"time":1789027222671,"data":{"turn":1,"step":29,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":0,"block":{"type":"reasoning","text":"Confirmed: t14 (implementation, NO assignee) → \"implementation r1\" preserved. t3 (implementation, WITH assignee) → \"work r1\" degraded.\n\nSo the cause is the explicit `assignee` parameter on quality-kind tasks: it silently degrades the kind. (Probably the plugin validates that the assignee is an active/spawned member and falls back to a work task when the member is still unspawned/staged.)\n\nFix: create quality tasks WITHOUT assignee (plugin auto-assigns some: t1 → Planner; t13/t14 → unassigned), then set the assignee via agent_teams_edit_plan update_task {assignee}. But wait — would update_task with assignee also degrade the kind? It's a different code path (edit_plan on a staged plan). Need to test.\n\nAlternative: create without assignee and leave them unassigned (shared pool). Then the scheduler assigns ready work to idle members — the protocol says \"Independent work should be parallel\" and \"let idle teammates execute ready work\"; unassigned tasks sit in the \"shared pending pool\" and members may claim them. But then the reviewer/implementer role distinction is lost (a read-only member could claim an implementation task!). Hmm, that's risky: profile guardrails may prevent read-only members from claiming implementation tasks (the profile text says read-only members take requirements/review/analysis tasks only). Maybe the plugin enforces that. Unknown.\n\nSo: assignee matters for correctness of role assignment. Let me test whether update_task's assignee preserves kind.\n\nPlan:\n1. Remove probes t13, t14 (3 calls).\n2. Rebuild the DAG: remove degraded t3..t12 in dependency-safe order, recreate without assignee, then edit_plan update_task assignee for each, then verify with status.\n\nThat's a lot of calls: removal of t12,t11,t9,t8,t7,t6 (dependents first), then t3,t4,t5 — but t6/t7/t8/t9 depend on t3/t4/t5 so they must be removed before. Order: t12 → t11 → t9 → t8 → t7 → t6 → t3, t4, t5. Each removal requires no remaining dependent. t4 is depended on by t8 (removed) and t11 (removed) → ok. t3 by t6,t7,t11 → ok after removal. t5 by t6,t9,t11 → ok.\n\nCan I batch all removals in ONE edit_plan call (atomic, ordered)? Yes — submit them in order in one batch. The plugin processes operations in order and validates the batch atomically. Order: t12, t11, t9, t8, t7, t6, then t3, t4, t5.\n\nWait — careful: are the removals validated sequentially? \"Submit dependent edits in order (update downstream dependencies or assignees, then remove tasks, then remove unused members)\". Since I remove all dependents before their deps, order within the batch should satisfy the constraint. Let me batch: remove t12, t11, t9, t8, t7, t6, t3, t4, t5.\n\nHmm, but should I instead just KEEP the degraded tasks and use edit_plan to fix their kind? update_task has no kind field → impossible. So rebuild.\n\n3. Recreate: \n   - tN_a: implementation core (no assignee, deps [t1]) — must depend on the staged requirements task (enforced).\n   - tN_b: implementation GUI (deps [t1])\n   - tN_c: implementation readonly fix (deps [t1]? The error said implementation must depend on the staged requirements task — for t5 I had no deps but it was created as work. With kind=implementation and no deps it may be rejected. So give it deps [t1]. That's harmless (t1 is quick) but it falsely implies a dependency on the wire contract. Acceptable: it does need... hmm, actually the readonly fix doesn't need the contract. But the plugin forces the dependency for implementation kind. Alternatively make it kind=repair with sourceTaskId=...? A repair needs a sourceTaskId (implementation/artifact). There's no source task (the defect is pre-existing). Hmm. `repair` requires sourceTaskId — no valid source. So implementation + deps [t1] it is. It's a small semantic wart; the dependency costs little (t1 completes early).\n     Hmm — wait. Is there an alternative: kind=verification? No, it's a fix.\n     Another alternative: create it as `work` kind (no contract needed) — but then no gate. I prefer implementation with the forced dep on t1.\n   - verification task tN_d: verification kind — needs acceptance + objective; does it require a dependency? Probably needs deps (it must run after implementations). It may also require \"must depend on ... implementation\"? Unknown; I'll provide deps [impl_core, impl_gui, impl_fix].\n   - review tasks: kind=review + reviewedTaskId. Does it require deps? The system usually auto-adds the dependency on the reviewed task. I'll pass dependencies [reviewed task] explicitly to be safe... but maybe the plugin rejects explicit deps for reviews? Earlier, t7/t8/t9 with deps+t.assignee were accepted (as work). Now with kind=review and no assignee, let me try WITH explicit deps first; if rejected, retry without.\n   - docs/sync: implementation kind (it changes files, has inScope/verify) — deps [impl x3, verification].  Hmm, implementation kind must depend on the requirements task; the error message said \"must depend on the staged requirements task\" — maybe it checks that the dependency chain INCLUDES the requirements task (direct or transitive). Let me include t1 in the deps to be safe? For docs: deps [t1, impl, gui, fix, verification]. Fine.\n   - integration: kind=integration, deps [dossier, verification, reviews..., docs].\n\n4. After creation, set assignees via edit_plan update_task — and then verify kinds survived AND assignees applied. If update_task's assignee degrades the kind, fallback: leave them unassigned and rely on the profile's role-based auto-assignment... risky.\n\nHmm, wait. Before the big rebuild, let me test the assignee-via-edit_plan path on probe t14: update_tasks t14 with assignee \"Senior Engineer\", then status. If t14 stays \"implementation\" with the assignee → the workaround works. That's ONE call + one status check. Much cheaper than a failed rebuild.\n\nLet me do that now:\n- edit_plan: [{action:\"update_task\", task_id:\"t14\", assignee:\"Senior Engineer\"}]\n- status → check t14 shows \"implementation r1 ... → Senior Engineer\".\n\nThen also verify that assigning to a *read-only* member (e.g. Architect) is allowed for a review task.\n\nLet me run the test."}}}}
