evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:2655:{"type":"assistant/chunk","seq":67251,"time":1789027357249,"data":{"turn":2,"step":2,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":0,"block":{"type":"reasoning","text":"A wave of teammates started up and reported standing by — because the team loop is \"running\" and members turned, but they can't claim blocked work. The plan is staged and NOT approved; yet members spawned?! The status said \"idle/unspawned\" earlier; now several members joined (Senior Engineer \"joined ... as a member\"). Hmm — the team loop shows \"running\". It seems the members were spawned at some point (maybe the team was auto-approved? No...). Actually the messages show they \"joined\" and are idle, awaiting scheduler assignment. The plan is still staged/pending (tasks pending). So members are spawned but nothing is running — because the user hasn't approved.\n\nWait — but if the plan is staged and unapproved, why are members spawned? Possibly the live-delivery of my messages... no. Perhaps the session-start staged team has members pre-spawned in a limited \"standby\" mode. Their messages say \"no task started\", \"did not claim\". So no work is being done — good, no harm. But this is noise: they're each burning turns on standby.\n\nWhat should I do as captain now?\n1. Answer the Vision Analyst's offer (it asked for a task) — decide whether to create one.\n2. Quiet the standby loop: the members are turning repeatedly and reporting nothing. This will keep happening as long as the team loop is \"running\" but nothing is approved. I should not spam them; the best action is to wait for user approval. But the repeated standby turns are wasteful.\n\nHmm — should I have held the requirements conference differently? The protocol says members start only after approval. These turns are apparently triggered by the loop anyway.\n\nPractical captain actions now:\na. Decide on the Vision Analyst: I checked — no playwright/agent-browser on PATH, BUT `~/.cache/ms-playwright/chromium-1228` and `chromium_headless_shell-1228` exist! So Playwright browsers ARE installed (cache), though the playwright package isn't in the repo's node_modules. Maybe playwright is installed globally under nvm or in some other node_modules. Let me check more thoroughly: `npx playwright --version` (would try to install if absent — network, slow). Better: check global npm/bun installs and any playwright package dirs.\n\nIf Playwright is available (browsers cached + package somewhere), a visual QA task is genuinely executable: boot the QA web server in an isolated DSH_HOME (the repo's own QA script does this: web-client-adapt.mjs boots on a PORT and fetches routes), open the sidebar, screenshot, and hand the PNG to the Vision Analyst.\n\nBut careful about my own constraints: \"Do not start a replacement server unless the user asks; if one is needed, use a managed background job and verify its exact URL.\" Booting an isolated QA instance on a scratch port for a screenshot is a QA activity, not a replacement for the user's GUI. It's within the repo's QA discipline (dsh-qa boots isolated profiles). The user's GUI at 127.0.0.1:3080 remains untouched. That's acceptable — but it's also substantial extra scope.\n\nIs the juice worth the squeeze? The user's feature is a GUI change (\"可以给workmate改名/删除\" with the sidebar tab). Rendered verification would be genuinely valuable — the repo has a history of sidebar breakage (the troubleshooting table). And a Vision Analyst is on the roster; the visual-qa skill exists.\n\nHowever: the team is still staged awaiting user approval, and the GUI task (t16) hasn't run. A visual task must depend on t16. Adding it now = plan amendment; the plan hasn't been approved, so amending is fine and cheap (the user reviews the final plan).\n\nRisk: if the browser tooling turns out unusable, the task fails → repair loop noise. I can mitigate by scoping acceptance to be honest: \"verify the rendered UI; if the sandbox cannot run a real browser, report the exact blocker as a finding instead of fabricating pixel evidence, and fall back to a rendered-DOM/markup-level visual review with explicit confidence labels\". Hmm. Better: let me actually verify playwright availability NOW (cheap, decisive), then decide.\n\nLet me check:\n- global npm root / bun global for playwright\n- `~/.cache/ms-playwright/chromium-1228/chrome-linux/chrome` exists?\n- any `playwright-core` inside ~/.nvm/.../lib/node_modules or in the DSH checkout's node_modules (the harness may bundle playwright for web tests!)\n\nIf a usable chromium binary + a driver exist, the task is executable.\n\nb. Also: should I stop the standby churn? I can't \"pause\" the loop except by asking the user to approve/discard, or by sending a single guidance message to the standby members telling them to hold. Actually the messages say they finished their turns (idle). The loop \"running\" state apparently re-turns members periodically? That could keep spinning. One efficient action: send ONE message to the team? There's no broadcast; but I could message each standby member — that's 8 messages of noise and would actually START turns for them (send_message to idle starts a turn!) — the opposite of what I want.\n\nSo: do NOT message idle members (it would wake them). Their turns seem to be driven by the loop; they'll settle. The protocol says don't require reports from members with no assigned work.\n\nHmm, but they keep turning. Notice: the pattern seems to be that members spawn once and each took one turn (the loop \"running\" during staging appears to have spawned+run standby turns). Vision Analyst's subagent finished/failed. Maybe this settles now.\n\nThe right captain move: reply to the Vision Analyst's *request* by deciding — the message from Vision Analyst came via the mailbox, but replying with send_message would start a turn for it. If I create a task for it, the task is the answer (it will be assigned when the plan runs). Since the plan is staged, adding a task is a plan edit, not a spawn. Good: no wake-up.\n\nLet me check playwright availability, then decide on adding the visual task.\n\nLet me also reconsider: is adding the visual QA task scope creep that the user didn't ask for? The user asked to traverse the project and optimize the workmate system with rename + delete. Verifying the GUI visually is part of delivering a GUI feature well. Given the roster includes a Vision Analyst asking for work and the repo has `visual-qa` skill + playwright browser cache, it's a reasonable, bounded addition. But plan bloat is a real cost: the user must review it.\n\nMiddle ground: add ONE visual-verification task (kind=verification, Vision Analyst, deps t16 + t22?) — placed after t16 so the UI exists, and it can run in parallel with t18's gate sweep. Then t23 integration depends on it too.\n\nHmm, but if t23 depends on it and it fails for environmental reasons, integration blocks. I could make it NOT a dependency of t23 (integration can proceed; the visual verdict is reported separately)... but then a failing visual task wouldn't gate delivery, which is odd for a quality gate. Standard practice: any quality task in the plan participates in the gate. The system may auto-handle failures with repair loops.\n\nLet me first check playwright. Decide after."}}}}
evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:2656:{"type":"assistant/chunk","seq":67252,"time":1789027357250,"data":{"turn":2,"step":2,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":1,"block":{"type":"text","text":"A wave of teammates checked in — all correctly idle, none claiming blocked work. The Vision Analyst's offer is legitimate though: a GUI change deserves rendered verification. Let me check whether this sandbox can actually drive a browser before amending the plan."}}}}
