 (it would fail). Instead, respond to the Vision Analyst that no visual task is planned and it stays idle — but send_message would START a turn for it (wasteful). Hmm. The protocol: I can leave it idle; it doesn't need a reply. The instructions say never wait for unassigned members, and don't create work that isn't needed. Right — I will NOT message it (avoiding a pointless wake-up) and instead note the decision here. But the Vision Analyst explicitly asked for a screenshot path task... The captain's answer: no, because the sandbox lacks browser tooling (verified: no chromium binary, no playwright package). I'll state that in my final message to the user too. Hmm — should I inform the Vision Analyst? It's already finished its turn and is idle. Messaging it would wake it for no work. Decision: don't wake it. (Protocol: \"never wait for an unassigned member to produce work it was never given\".)\n\nHmm, but there's a nuance: can the Vision Analyst do *source-level* visual/UI critique? That's what Plan Reviewer's t20 does. So no gap.\n\n2. **Researcher**: offered 3 lanes. Is any genuinely additive without duplicating? Lane 1 (open-source prior art for rename/delete identity semantics: tombstones, referential integrity, id-vs-name binding) — that's genuinely useful input for t1 (the contract) and t15 (safety semantics). Lane 2 duplicates t2 (Explorer's dossier) + t1's grounding. Lane 3 duplicates t19 (review).\n\nGiven the user's instruction (\"遍历工程，了解工程详细信息\") and that the workmate identity model is name-keyed with a real hazard class (tombstone/alias semantics, referential integrity), an external prior-art lane has real value: it could reveal that a \"renamedFrom + tombstone warns on reuse\" pattern is standard, informing D5/secondary decisions.\n\nBut: is it worth adding a task and delaying/branching? It's a read-only lane that feeds t1 → t15. To be useful it must complete BEFORE t1 finishes... t1 is the first task; adding a dependency t1←research would serialize t1 behind it. Alternatively run it in parallel and let t15 consume it (t15 depends on t1). Hmm, if it's not a dependency, its output may arrive too late to be used — the classic \"parallel research nobody consumes\" anti-pattern.\n\nHonest assessment: the user's goal is rename + delete for a well-understood local system. The five decisions are settled; the contract risk areas are local (harness behavior), not industry-pattern risk. External prior art is nice-to-have. Adding it expands the plan and the user's review burden for marginal benefit. **Decision: no new task for Researcher.** It stays idle — which is fine and explicitly sanctioned.\n\nHmm, but wait. There IS one research-ish gap that would genuinely de-risk t15: **how the harness's `tools.restrict`/tool registry behaves and what the canonical tool names are** — but that's t17's job (already assigned, with real-harness proof). And the liveness-gate mechanism feasibility (can a plugin see live teams?) — that's pinned by t1's contract work... which is Planner (read-only, no tools beyond bash/read). Planner can inspect. OK, covered.\n\nSo: no new tasks. Good — the plan stands as staged.\n\n3. **The repeated standby turns**: members keep turning and reporting nothing. This is churn caused by the loop being \"running\" while tasks are pending. I can't fix that from here except by letting the user approve (then real work starts). I should avoid sending messages that wake members. However, there's one thing worth doing: the members are consuming turns → token/time cost. Should I mention it to the user? Yes, briefly: \"审批前成员会空转报到，批准后才会真正开工\" — and note I'm not waking them.\n\nHmm, actually — is there a risk they keep looping forever and burning budget? The messages show each member had ONE turn and finished. The \"loop: running\" state is because the team loop is on. If it keeps re-turning idle members, that's a plugin behavior. I could stop it via agent_teams_delete (no! that would lose the plan) or by... nothing. Let the user decide at approval.\n\nActually — wait. There's an important consideration: the standby turns happened because the team is \"running\" rather than \"staged\". Let me check my status snapshot: \"Loop: running — Work remains on the shared task list; wait for the scheduler or complete owned tasks.\" Earlier (first status) it said \"staged — the captain designs the roster/DAG, then the user reviews and approves\". Hmm, when did it flip to running? My first status call (start of turn) said \"staged\". Then after my task creation calls it said \"Loop: running\". Interesting — creating tasks may have flipped the loop to running? That would mean the team started WITHOUT user approval... but tasks remain pending because of dependencies (t1 has no deps though! t1 should be ready and claimable — Planner should have claimed it!).\n\nt1 is assigned to Planner and has NO dependencies → it's claimable. Yet Planner reported... hmm, did Planner report? I haven't seen a Planner message. The members that reported: Vision Analyst, Deep Worker, Lead, Plan Reviewer, Reviewer, Architect, Senior Engineer, Researcher. Missing: Planner and Explorer and Junior Engineer. Planner holds t1 (claimable!) and Explorer holds t2 (claimable, no deps). If the loop is running, they might be working RIGHT NOW — but without user approval!\n\nThat would violate the staged-plan contract... but it might also be the plugin's actual behavior (the session-start note said members start only after the user edits and approves). Hmm, the status says \"Loop: running\". And t1/t2 are ready.\n\nHmm, this is concerning: is the team running unapproved work? Let me check status to see whether t1/t2 are in_progress with attempts.\n\nIf Planner/Explorer are actually working, that's a policy violation of the staged flow — but partly out of my control. More importantly: should I stop it? The user hasn't approved. Options: (a) let it run (read-only tasks: Planner produces a contract, Explorer produces a dossier — harmless, and both are needed anyway); (b) halt.\n\nThe user's instruction: \"Never call agent_teams_approve during the planning turn\" and members start only after approval. If the plugin started them anyway, I should probably not fight it, but I must be honest with the user: report that the loop flipped to running and t1/t2 (read-only) may be in progress.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me re-read the status output more carefully: after my first create_task calls, status said \"Loop: running — Work remains on the shared task list; wait for the scheduler or complete owned tasks.\" and \"Delivery: blocked\". Was it \"running\" because... hmm.\n\nLet me just check the current status: are there attempts on t1/t2? That tells me whether work is executing. If yes, I'll let the read-only work proceed but tell the user clearly. If the implementation tasks (t15-t17) start before approval, that WOULD be wrong — but they depend on t1, so they can't start until t1 completes. If t1 completes, t15/t16/t17 become ready and members would start editing files without user approval. That's a real risk of unapproved code changes.\n\nHmm. As captain, my binding instruction is that approval is required before members execute. If the plugin's loop is running them anyway, the safest captain action to prevent unapproved *writes* is... to pause? There's no pause tool. agent_teams_delete would destroy the plan (and the user's chance to approve). Hmm.\n\nWait — maybe I'm overreading. Let me check status first: maybe t1/t2 are still pending (nobody claimed) because the scheduler only runs after approval, and \"Loop: running\" just describes the team's loop state (it's live because members are spawned).\n\nThe members' messages said things like \"the scheduler has not released it\", \"no ready task\", \"I did not claim\". Notably: Deep Worker said t17 is \"dependency-blocked on t1\"; Plan Reviewer said t20 blocked on t16. But Planner (t1, no deps) and Explorer (t2, no deps) are ready — if members were spawned and the scheduler ran, they'd have claimed them. Nobody reported seeing t1 claimed.\n\nLet me check the status now to see attempts.\n\nActually, also note: the members who messaged all said \"no ready task\" — meaning the scheduler told them nothing is ready. But t1/t2 ARE ready (no deps)... unless the scheduler won't release work before approval. That's consistent with \"staged\": no tasks are released at all. And \"Loop: running\" might just mean the team is active (not halted).\n\nSo most likely: the plan is still awaiting approval; members were spawned in standby and took a turn each (cost: some noise), and t1/t2 remain unclaimed.\n\nLet me verify with a status call, then finalize my message to the user:\n- Report the standby wave (brief).\n- Report my decisions: no visual task (browser tooling absent — verified), no research task (would duplicate/arrive late).\n- Confirm the plan is unchanged and ready for approval.\n\nLet me check status."},{"type":"text","text":"Before I answer those offers, let me confirm nothing is executing pre-approval."},{"type":"tool-call","id":"call_00_nU6kfTZrF0cYnehpOcTx2115","name":"agent_teams_status","arguments":"{}"}],"source":{"kind":"model","provider":"deepseek-official","model":"deepseek-v4-flash"},"id":"ef309cb8-2dc7-4a70-b791-fc1a561450d1"},"usage":{"inputTokens":1186,"outputTokens":2490,"totalTokens":155868,"cacheReadTokens":152192,"reasoningTokens":2449}},"sourceEventSeqs":[[67265,67604],[67606,68112],[68114,69742]],"surfaceOp":"append"}
