evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:2990:{"type":"assistant/chunk","seq":75885,"time":1789027415033,"data":{"turn":2,"step":5,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":0,"block":{"type":"reasoning","text":"Noise from an unrelated subagent failure/recall warning. Ignore it.\n\nNow, key facts confirmed:\n- Working tree: 33 dirty entries (21 modified + untracked), including exactly the GUI files. The previous session's work is uncommitted.\n- client.js (15:36) is NEWER than src/web-client.js (15:27) and contains the markers → the artifact is fresh relative to sources. Good: not stale. Junior Engineer's worry about \"899-line diff\" is just the uncommitted diff vs HEAD, not staleness.\n\nDecision on baseline: the essential mitigation is evidence attribution: tasks must capture `git status --porcelain` + HEAD before/after and must not revert pre-existing changes. I'll add a BASELINE clause to t15, t16, t18, t23.\n\nActually — since client.js is fresh (rebuilt at 15:36 AFTER sources at 15:27), and the uncommitted work is a coherent finished state, the pragmatic baseline note is: \"HEAD f697088 + 33 dirty entries (previous session's uncommitted sidebar-migration work) form the baseline; capture git status/HEAD before and after; never revert or stash pre-existing changes; report your own diff separately.\"\n\nLet me apply via edit_plan update_task with description replacement for t15, t16, t18, t23. I need to resend full descriptions. Let me do it — but to keep it economical I'll append a compact clause and keep the rest of the text identical.\n\nHmm, that's 4 long description rewrites ≈ heavy. Let me reduce to the three that matter most: t16 (artifact rebuild — the flagged one), t18 (verification baseline) and t23 (integration report). t15 also rebuilds dist... but t15's scope is a package whose dist is committed; same attribution issue. \n\nLet me just do t15, t16, t18, t23 — four. Actually, hmm: cost/benefit. The clause's purpose: (1) don't revert others' uncommitted work; (2) attribute changes. Both are also covered by my instruction to the Lead in t23 and by the Reviewer... no, reviewers read code not git.\n\nI'll do t15, t16, t18, t23. Let me write them compactly (keeping full original text + a BASELINE paragraph).\n\nWait — before spending that, consider: is the user's approval confirmed? t1/t2 running suggests approval happened. If the plan is approved (not staged), can edit_plan still work? The tool says \"Revise the current staged AgentTeams plan\" — if the team is running, edit_plan may reject. Let me try; if it fails, I'll fall back to putting the baseline note in the members' mailboxes... but that wakes them (they're idle now — waking a member with guidance is actually appropriate and cheaper than 4 description rewrites? Sending guidance to 4 members = 4 wake-ups × full turns = expensive).\n\nHmm! Wait. Better idea: the baseline note matters most to members when they START their task. The scheduler wakes them anyway. A single message to each when they start... can't time it.\n\nAlternative: since t23 (Lead, integration) and t18 (Reviewer, verification) are the evidence-owners, and t15/t16 are the writers, the cheapest effective action: edit_plan descriptions for t16 + t18 + t23, and rely on t1's contract... no, t15's dist rebuild is analogous to t16's.\n\nLet me try edit_plan with all four; if it errors (not staged), I'll reconsider.\n\nActually — hold on. Since the team appears APPROVED and RUNNING, the \"staged plan\" editing window may be closed. Let me test with a single small edit first (t16 description) to see if edit_plan still works... that's the same call cost as doing all four at once; if the batch fails, nothing is applied (atomic). So just send the batch of 4 and see.\n\nLet me compose carefully, preserving each task's original description text and appending the BASELINE clause.\n\nt15 original description (I have it). t16, t18, t23 likewise. Let me write them with the added paragraph:\n\n\"BASELINE (captain note): the working tree is deliberately dirty — HEAD f697088 plus 33 uncommitted entries from the previous session's sidebar-migration work (including the files in your scope). Treat that state as the baseline: never revert, stash or 'clean up' pre-existing modifications, record HEAD and `git status --porcelain` before and after your work, and report your OWN changes separately from the pre-existing ones so downstream review can attribute them.\"\n\nFor t16, add: \"the pre-existing client.js is already newer than src/web-client.js (rebuilt 15:36 vs 15:27) and contains the current markers — a rebuild is still required after your edit; diff client.js against the pre-existing version, not against HEAD.\"\n\nFor t18: \"record the baseline (HEAD + dirty-file list) in your evidence so the verified delta is attributable.\"\n\nFor t23: \"state the baseline (HEAD f697088 + pre-existing uncommitted work) and the change set attributable to this team in the delivery report.\"\n\nOK let me write the 4 update_task operations. This is long but let me do it.\n\nActually, let me reconsider ONE more time whether to do this at all. Simplification: the Lead's t23 report + t18's evidence note are the two places where baseline attribution truly lands. t15/t16 writers just need \"don't revert pre-existing changes\" — which is a natural instinct anyway and they're competent. Given the heavy token cost of 4 full description rewrites, I'll do 2 (t16 — the artifact rebuilder where the Junior Engineer explicitly asked for a baseline decision; and t18 — the verification evidence owner). And I'll note the baseline in t23? t23's report should state it... \n\nHmm, the flag came from Junior Engineer about t16/t18/t20/t23. Let me cover t16 + t18 + t23 = 3. And for t15, its dist rebuild is analogous to t16 — I'll skip t15 to save tokens? Inconsistent...\n\nYou know what, let me be decisive and practical: do all four (t15, t16, t18, t23) in one atomic batch. Accuracy over token thrift. It's the last plan edit.\n\nHmm, but wait: t19/t20 reviewers also diff. Their acceptance already says \"judge the latest code state\". Fine.\n\nLet me write it."}}}}
evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:2991:{"type":"assistant/chunk","seq":75886,"time":1789027415034,"data":{"turn":2,"step":5,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":1,"block":{"type":"text","text":"Baseline hazard verified independently — and one correction to the teammate's read: `client.js` (15:36) is **newer** than its source (15:27) and already carries the current markers, so it is not stale; the \"899-line diff\" is just the uncommitted diff vs HEAD. Recording the baseline decision in the plan so evidence stays attributable:"}}}}
evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:2992:{"type":"assistant/chunk","seq":75887,"time":1789027415034,"data":{"turn":2,"step":5,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":2,"block":{"type":"tool-call","id":"call_00_nY2RZG8YcYsiJWNPxfGj8049","name":"agent_teams_edit_plan","arguments":"{\"operations\": [{\"action\": \"update_task\", \"description\": \"Implement exactly what the T1 contract pins — read its output first and treat it as binding; if you find a contract gap or a genuine impossibility in this harness, report it in your task output instead of silently improvising a different design.\\n\\nThe work is centred on packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/src/index.ts: the 5 existing tools (list/init/spawn/reflect/match), the mpdWorkmate service, the index.json writer (no reader today), and the 4 lazily registered web routes. Correctness constraints that matter most: sanitizeName() is the single key algorithm and wmDir(\\\"\\\") resolves to the library root itself, so every name must be validated before any fs call; the rename must preserve the evolved identity (persona/memory/note/uses/createdAt) and must never be a re-instantiation; delete must be all-or-nothing; and a mutation racing an in-flight spawn/reflect must have a defined outcome (the liveness gate from D2 is how you close that race).\\n\\nKeep the house rules: all harness seams go through the mpd-dsh-adapter (packages/mpd-dsh-adapter-plugin) — never call ctx.tools/ctx.subagents directly; keep the state under ~/.mpd/workmate (the one sanctioned HOME exception); English-only code comments and docstrings; minimal diff, no speculative refactors. Rebuild the package dist when done (bun build src/index.ts --target node --format esm --outfile dist/index.js) — a checkout install reads dist directly.\\n\\nBASELINE (captain note, verified): the working tree is DELIBERATELY dirty — HEAD f697088 plus 33 uncommitted entries from the previous session's sidebar-migration work, and some of them lie in this repo area. Treat that state as your baseline: never revert, stash or \\\"clean up\\\" pre-existing modifications, record HEAD + `git status --porcelain` before and after your work, and report YOUR OWN changes separately from the pre-existing ones so downstream review can attribute them.\\n\\nScope boundary: the roles-plugin deny list is the other fix task's, the GUI is the GUI task's, all documentation is the docs task's. Do not touch them.\", \"task_id\": \"t15\"}, {\"action\": \"update_task\", \"description\": \"Deliver the human-facing half of the feature in the existing Workmates DSH-better-sidebar tab. Read the T1 wire contract first and treat it as binding — call exactly those routes and payloads; if the contract is missing something the UI needs, report it in your task output rather than inventing a route.\\n\\nSource of truth is packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/src/web-client.js: the WorkmateLibraryView component, its list/detail/init form, the request() helper, the WORKMATE_LOCALE_NAMESPACE zh+en dictionaries, SIDEBAR_TAB_ID=\\\"mpd-workmate\\\" and registerWorkmateSidebarTab. The tab is sidebar-only BY DESIGN (the old overlay floater and footer toggle were deliberately removed and the build fails if they come back), it is single: true, and it must never reintroduce an unguarded service declaration: optional seams are probed through ctx.get behind a helper that never throws (see the AGENTS.md troubleshooting rows and packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/test/sidebar-tab.test.mjs, which pins this seam policy).\\n\\nAfter editing the source you MUST rebuild the shipped client with `node scripts/build-mpd-client.mjs`, otherwise the bundle shows nothing new.\\n\\nBASELINE (captain decision on the hazard you raised): the dirty tree is the BASELINE, not an accident — HEAD f697088 plus 33 uncommitted entries from the previous session's sidebar-migration work. Do NOT commit, stash, revert or \\\"tidy\\\" any of it. Record HEAD + `git status --porcelain` before and after, and report your own delta separately. Note also, verified by the captain: the pre-existing packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/client.js is FRESHER than its source (rebuilt 15:36 vs source 15:27) and already contains the current markers, so it is not stale — after your edit, diff client.js against the pre-existing version rather than against HEAD, and state in your evidence that the rebuild regenerated it from on-disk sources.\\n\\nScope boundary: the host tools and web routes belong to the core implementation task; documentation and QA scripts belong to the docs task. Do not touch them.\", \"task_id\": \"t16\"}, {\"action\": \"update_task\", \"description\": \"Verify the delivered feature independently. Do not trust the implementers' summaries: reproduce from the real artifacts (source, built dist, real dsh boot, sandboxed filesystem) and treat any mismatch between a claim and the observed behaviour as a finding you report — you do not fix code yourself.\\n\\nMethod that fits this repo: follow the dsh-qa discipline (skills/dsh-qa) — an isolated DSH_HOME and HOME per run, credentials copied once into the sandbox, sandbox paths asserted, evidence written under evidence/<domain>/<slug>/<timestamp>/{result.json,output.log}, and long-lived MCP children run with stdio to files rather than pipes. The workmate library lives under $HOME, so every lifecycle exercise MUST run with HOME pointing at a sandbox and the evidence must show it.\\n\\nPay particular attention to the cases where a naive implementation is quietly wrong: a name that sanitizes to an empty string (wmDir(\\\"\\\") is the library root itself), a rename that loses evolved memory or bumps uses, a delete that leaves the index stale or removes only part of the instance, a mutation racing an in-flight spawn, and a GUI-visible route that answers 200 on an unknown name.\\n\\nBASELINE (captain note): the tree starts dirty by design — HEAD f697088 plus 33 uncommitted entries from the previous session, some inside the touched packages. Record HEAD + `git status --porcelain` in your evidence at the start and at the end, so the delta you verified is attributable to this team rather than to pre-existing work; never revert pre-existing changes to \\\"clean\\\" a run.\\n\\nIf something fails, report it as a failed verification with the exact command, exit code and output — the scheduler turns that into a repair loop. Do not edit implementation files.\", \"task_id\": \"t18\"}, {\"action\": \"update_task\", \"description\": \"Integrate the verified, reviewed work into one coherent change on disk and report it honestly. You own the last mile: nothing may be described as done that the evidence does not support.\\n\\nDo this concretely:\\n1. Persist the read-only deliverables that only exist as task text (the t1 contract, the t2 dossier) into the evidence tree for this change, together with a summary of t18's verification results.\\n2. Re-run the gate sweep on the FINAL integrated tree (after t22's doc/QA-script sync) and record the real output — an earlier green run on a pre-sync tree does not count.\\n3. Check code↔docs consistency in both directions and fix drift inside your inScope (documentation paths) only; report any code-side drift as an open finding instead of editing implementation files.\\n4. Produce the delivery report as your task output: changed files, the decisions implemented, the explicit non-goals and non-rewrites (archived team records are historical and untouched), evidence paths, residual risk, and anything the user should decide next (for example whether Unicode/CJK workmate names should be supported in a follow-up).\\n5. Confirm the release path is coherent: rebuilt package dists and the rebuilt web client correspond to the sources, so a later `npm run pack` would ship what was verified.\\n\\nBASELINE (captain note): the tree was already dirty before this team started — HEAD f697088 plus 33 uncommitted entries from the previous session's work, several inside the packages this team touched. In the delivery report, state that baseline explicitly and separate the change set attributable to THIS team from the pre-existing uncommitted work; do not commit, stash or revert anyone else's changes.\\n\\nIf a required review or verification is still failing, do not paper over it: report the integration as failed with the concrete blocker.\", \"task_id\": \"t23\"}]}"}}}}
evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:2995:{"type":"assistant/message","seq":75890,"time":1789027415040,"data":{"turn":2,"step":5,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"reasoning","text":"Noise from an unrelated subagent failure/recall warning. Ignore it.\n\nNow, key facts confirmed:\n- Working tree: 33 dirty entries (21 modified + untracked), including exactly the GUI files. The previous session's work is uncommitted.\n- client.js (15:36) is NEWER than src/web-client.js (15:27) and contains the markers → the artifact is fresh relative to sources. Good: not stale. Junior Engineer's worry about \"899-line diff\" is just the uncommitted diff vs HEAD, not staleness.\n\nDecision on baseline: the essential mitigation is evidence attribution: tasks must capture `git status --porcelain` + HEAD before/after and must not revert pre-existing changes. I'll add a BASELINE clause to t15, t16, t18, t23.\n\nActually — since client.js is fresh (rebuilt at 15:36 AFTER sources at 15:27), and the uncommitted work is a coherent finished state, the pragmatic baseline note is: \"HEAD f697088 + 33 dirty entries (previous session's uncommitted sidebar-migration work) form the baseline; capture git status/HEAD before and after; never revert or stash pre-existing changes; report your own diff separately.\"\n\nLet me apply via edit_plan update_task with description replacement for t15, t16, t18, t23. I need to resend full descriptions. Let me do it — but to keep it economical I'll append a compact clause and keep the rest of the text identical.\n\nHmm, that's 4 long description rewrites ≈ heavy. Let me reduce to the three that matter most: t16 (artifact rebuild — the flagged one), t18 (verification baseline) and t23 (integration report). t15 also rebuilds dist... but t15's scope is a package whose dist is committed; same attribution issue. \n\nLet me just do t15, t16, t18, t23 — four. Actually, hmm: cost/benefit. The clause's purpose: (1) don't revert others' uncommitted work; (2) attribute changes. Both are also covered by my instruction to the Lead in t23 and by the Reviewer... no, reviewers read code not git.\n\nI'll do t15, t16, t18, t23. Let me write them compactly (keeping full original text + a BASELINE paragraph).\n\nWait — before spending that, consider: is the user's approval confirmed? t1/t2 running suggests approval happened. If the plan is approved (not staged), can edit_plan still work? The tool says \"Revise the current staged AgentTeams plan\" — if the team is running, edit_plan may reject. Let me try; if it fails, I'll fall back to putting the baseline note in the members' mailboxes... but that wakes them (they're idle now — waking a member with guidance is actually appropriate and cheaper than 4 description rewrites? Sending guidance to 4 members = 4 wake-ups × full turns = expensive).\n\nHmm! Wait. Better idea: the baseline note matters most to members when they START their task. The scheduler wakes them anyway. A single message to each when they start... can't time it.\n\nAlternative: since t23 (Lead, integration) and t18 (Reviewer, verification) are the evidence-owners, and t15/t16 are the writers, the cheapest effective action: edit_plan descriptions for t16 + t18 + t23, and rely on t1's contract... no, t15's dist rebuild is analogous to t16's.\n\nLet me try edit_plan with all four; if it errors (not staged), I'll reconsider.\n\nActually — hold on. Since the team appears APPROVED and RUNNING, the \"staged plan\" editing window may be closed. Let me test with a single small edit first (t16 description) to see if edit_plan still works... that's the same call cost as doing all four at once; if the batch fails, nothing is applied (atomic). So just send the batch of 4 and see.\n\nLet me compose carefully, preserving each task's original description text and appending the BASELINE clause.\n\nt15 original description (I have it). t16, t18, t23 likewise. Let me write them with the added paragraph:\n\n\"BASELINE (captain note): the working tree is deliberately dirty — HEAD f697088 plus 33 uncommitted entries from the previous session's sidebar-migration work (including the files in your scope). Treat that state as the baseline: never revert, stash or 'clean up' pre-existing modifications, record HEAD and `git status --porcelain` before and after your work, and report your OWN changes separately from the pre-existing ones so downstream review can attribute them.\"\n\nFor t16, add: \"the pre-existing client.js is already newer than src/web-client.js (rebuilt 15:36 vs 15:27) and contains the current markers — a rebuild is still required after your edit; diff client.js against the pre-existing version, not against HEAD.\"\n\nFor t18: \"record the baseline (HEAD + dirty-file list) in your evidence so the verified delta is attributable.\"\n\nFor t23: \"state the baseline (HEAD f697088 + pre-existing uncommitted work) and the change set attributable to this team in the delivery report.\"\n\nOK let me write the 4 update_task operations. This is long but let me do it.\n\nActually, let me reconsider ONE more time whether to do this at all. Simplification: the Lead's t23 report + t18's evidence note are the two places where baseline attribution truly lands. t15/t16 writers just need \"don't revert pre-existing changes\" — which is a natural instinct anyway and they're competent. Given the heavy token cost of 4 full description rewrites, I'll do 2 (t16 — the artifact rebuilder where the Junior Engineer explicitly asked for a baseline decision; and t18 — the verification evidence owner). And I'll note the baseline in t23? t23's report should state it... \n\nHmm, the flag came from Junior Engineer about t16/t18/t20/t23. Let me cover t16 + t18 + t23 = 3. And for t15, its dist rebuild is analogous to t16 — I'll skip t15 to save tokens? Inconsistent...\n\nYou know what, let me be decisive and practical: do all four (t15, t16, t18, t23) in one atomic batch. Accuracy over token thrift. It's the last plan edit.\n\nHmm, but wait: t19/t20 reviewers also diff. Their acceptance already says \"judge the latest code state\". Fine.\n\nLet me write it."},{"type":"text","text":"Baseline hazard verified independently — and one correction to the teammate's read: `client.js` (15:36) is **newer** than its source (15:27) and already carries the current markers, so it is not stale; the \"899-line diff\" is just the uncommitted diff vs HEAD. Recording the baseline decision in the plan so evidence stays attributable:"},{"type":"tool-call","id":"call_00_nY2RZG8YcYsiJWNPxfGj8049","name":"agent_teams_edit_plan","arguments":"{\"operations\": [{\"action\": \"update_task\", \"description\": \"Implement exactly what the T1 contract pins — read its output first and treat it as binding; if you find a contract gap or a genuine impossibility in this harness, report it in your task output instead of silently improvising a different design.\\n\\nThe work is centred on packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/src/index.ts: the 5 existing tools (list/init/spawn/reflect/match), the mpdWorkmate service, the index.json writer (no reader today), and the 4 lazily registered web routes. Correctness constraints that matter most: sanitizeName() is the single key algorithm and wmDir(\\\"\\\") resolves to the library root itself, so every name must be validated before any fs call; the rename must preserve the evolved identity (persona/memory/note/uses/createdAt) and must never be a re-instantiation; delete must be all-or-nothing; and a mutation racing an in-flight spawn/reflect must have a defined outcome (the liveness gate from D2 is how you close that race).\\n\\nKeep the house rules: all harness seams go through the mpd-dsh-adapter (packages/mpd-dsh-adapter-plugin) — never call ctx.tools/ctx.subagents directly; keep the state under ~/.mpd/workmate (the one sanctioned HOME exception); English-only code comments and docstrings; minimal diff, no speculative refactors. Rebuild the package dist when done (bun build src/index.ts --target node --format esm --outfile dist/index.js) — a checkout install reads dist directly.\\n\\nBASELINE (captain note, verified): the working tree is DELIBERATELY dirty — HEAD f697088 plus 33 uncommitted entries from the previous session's sidebar-migration work, and some of them lie in this repo area. Treat that state as your baseline: never revert, stash or \\\"clean up\\\" pre-existing modifications, record HEAD + `git status --porcelain` before and after your work, and report YOUR OWN changes separately from the pre-existing ones so downstream review can attribute them.\\n\\nScope boundary: the roles-plugin deny list is the other fix task's, the GUI is the GUI task's, all documentation is the docs task's. Do not touch them.\", \"task_id\": \"t15\"}, {\"action\": \"update_task\", \"description\": \"Deliver the human-facing half of the feature in the existing Workmates DSH-better-sidebar tab. Read the T1 wire contract first and treat it as binding — call exactly those routes and payloads; if the contract is missing something the UI needs, report it in your task output rather than inventing a route.\\n\\nSource of truth is packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/src/web-client.js: the WorkmateLibraryView component, its list/detail/init form, the request() helper, the WORKMATE_LOCALE_NAMESPACE zh+en dictionaries, SIDEBAR_TAB_ID=\\\"mpd-workmate\\\" and registerWorkmateSidebarTab. The tab is sidebar-only BY DESIGN (the old overlay floater and footer toggle were deliberately removed and the build fails if they come back), it is single: true, and it must never reintroduce an unguarded service declaration: optional seams are probed through ctx.get behind a helper that never throws (see the AGENTS.md troubleshooting rows and packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/test/sidebar-tab.test.mjs, which pins this seam policy).\\n\\nAfter editing the source you MUST rebuild the shipped client with `node scripts/build-mpd-client.mjs`, otherwise the bundle shows nothing new.\\n\\nBASELINE (captain decision on the hazard you raised): the dirty tree is the BASELINE, not an accident — HEAD f697088 plus 33 uncommitted entries from the previous session's sidebar-migration work. Do NOT commit, stash, revert or \\\"tidy\\\" any of it. Record HEAD + `git status --porcelain` before and after, and report your own delta separately. Note also, verified by the captain: the pre-existing packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/client.js is FRESHER than its source (rebuilt 15:36 vs source 15:27) and already contains the current markers, so it is not stale — after your edit, diff client.js against the pre-existing version rather than against HEAD, and state in your evidence that the rebuild regenerated it from on-disk sources.\\n\\nScope boundary: the host tools and web routes belong to the core implementation task; documentation and QA scripts belong to the docs task. Do not touch them.\", \"task_id\": \"t16\"}, {\"action\": \"update_task\", \"description\": \"Verify the delivered feature independently. Do not trust the implementers' summaries: reproduce from the real artifacts (source, built dist, real dsh boot, sandboxed filesystem) and treat any mismatch between a claim and the observed behaviour as a finding you report — you do not fix code yourself.\\n\\nMethod that fits this repo: follow the dsh-qa discipline (skills/dsh-qa) — an isolated DSH_HOME and HOME per run, credentials copied once into the sandbox, sandbox paths asserted, evidence written under evidence/<domain>/<slug>/<timestamp>/{result.json,output.log}, and long-lived MCP children run with stdio to files rather than pipes. The workmate library lives under $HOME, so every lifecycle exercise MUST run with HOME pointing at a sandbox and the evidence must show it.\\n\\nPay particular attention to the cases where a naive implementation is quietly wrong: a name that sanitizes to an empty string (wmDir(\\\"\\\") is the library root itself), a rename that loses evolved memory or bumps uses, a delete that leaves the index stale or removes only part of the instance, a mutation racing an in-flight spawn, and a GUI-visible route that answers 200 on an unknown name.\\n\\nBASELINE (captain note): the tree starts dirty by design — HEAD f697088 plus 33 uncommitted entries from the previous session, some inside the touched packages. Record HEAD + `git status --porcelain` in your evidence at the start and at the end, so the delta you verified is attributable to this team rather than to pre-existing work; never revert pre-existing changes to \\\"clean\\\" a run.\\n\\nIf something fails, report it as a failed verification with the exact command, exit code and output — the scheduler turns that into a repair loop. Do not edit implementation files.\", \"task_id\": \"t18\"}, {\"action\": \"update_task\", \"description\": \"Integrate the verified, reviewed work into one coherent change on disk and report it honestly. You own the last mile: nothing may be described as done that the evidence does not support.\\n\\nDo this concretely:\\n1. Persist the read-only deliverables that only exist as task text (the t1 contract, the t2 dossier) into the evidence tree for this change, together with a summary of t18's verification results.\\n2. Re-run the gate sweep on the FINAL integrated tree (after t22's doc/QA-script sync) and record the real output — an earlier green run on a pre-sync tree does not count.\\n3. Check code↔docs consistency in both directions and fix drift inside your inScope (documentation paths) only; report any code-side drift as an open finding instead of editing implementation files.\\n4. Produce the delivery report as your task output: changed files, the decisions implemented, the explicit non-goals and non-rewrites (archived team records are historical and untouched), evidence paths, residual risk, and anything the user should decide next (for example whether Unicode/CJK workmate names should be supported in a follow-up).\\n5. Confirm the release path is coherent: rebuilt package dists and the rebuilt web client correspond to the sources, so a later `npm run pack` would ship what was verified.\\n\\nBASELINE (captain note): the tree was already dirty before this team started — HEAD f697088 plus 33 uncommitted entries from the previous session's work, several inside the packages this team touched. In the delivery report, state that baseline explicitly and separate the change set attributable to THIS team from the pre-existing uncommitted work; do not commit, stash or revert anyone else's changes.\\n\\nIf a required review or verification is still failing, do not paper over it: report the integration as failed with the concrete blocker.\", \"task_id\": \"t23\"}]}"}],"source":{"kind":"model","provider":"deepseek-official","model":"deepseek-v4-flash"},"id":"700a7bc5-7d40-409f-b826-51d665600d0d"},"usage":{"inputTokens":2907,"outputTokens":3339,"totalTokens":167526,"cacheReadTokens":161280,"reasoningTokens":1419}},"sourceEventSeqs":[[72579,75889]],"surfaceOp":"append"}
