evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:3098:{"type":"assistant/chunk","seq":78543,"time":1789027433152,"data":{"turn":2,"step":6,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":0,"block":{"type":"reasoning","text":"Confirmed: the team is RUNNING — meaning the plan was approved (the user must have clicked Approve & Run in the Web panel, since I never called approve). So the plan is locked and work has begun. I cannot edit task descriptions anymore.\n\nSo I must convey the baseline decision through the other allowed channel: agent_teams_send_message. But messaging idle members starts a turn (wasteful). However, the protocol explicitly supports \"send guidance with agent_teams_send_message\" for a running team. The relevant recipients: Senior Engineer (t15, will start after t1), Junior Engineer (t16, after t1), Reviewer (t18), Lead (t23). All are idle now, awaiting the scheduler.\n\nHmm: t1/t2 are in progress. When t1 completes, the scheduler will wake t15/t16/t17. If I message them NOW, they'll each take a turn immediately (burning tokens) just to receive guidance, then be woken again to work. That's wasteful but ensures the baseline rule reaches them.\n\nIs the baseline rule important enough? Its content: don't revert pre-existing dirty work; attribute your own delta. Honestly, competent members will not revert anything. The genuinely useful bits:\n- client.js is not stale (corrects Junior Engineer's concern) — it will be rebuilt anyway.\n- Evidence should record HEAD + git status before/after for attribution.\n\nGiven the cost/benefit, I'll message only the two members where attribution actually lands and where the misconception exists: **Junior Engineer** (t16 — raised it, and holds the artifact rebuild) and **Reviewer** (t18 — owns verification evidence). And maybe Lead (t23 — final report)... \n\nHmm, minimizing: the Lead's t23 report baseline note would be nice but the Lead is 100% downstream; I can also state the baseline myself in my final report to the user (I'm the captain and I verify at the end). So: message Junior Engineer + Reviewer only.\n\nWait — actually, since all four are now idle and each message costs a full turn... Let me reconsider: is a message even needed for the Reviewer? Its acceptance (which I wrote) already says: \"The real HOME and the real ~/.dsh are never touched (the sandbox path is asserted in the evidence).\" Nothing about the baseline. The risk of NOT messaging: the Reviewer might see 33 dirty files and report a scary \"uncommitted changes everywhere\" finding, or worse, try to \"clean up\". Hmm, that's a real risk of a false-positive finding + reviewer confusion.\n\nOK: message the two (Junior Engineer, Reviewer). Actually — also the Lead would produce the final report and might misattribute changes. Three messages. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, one message each to the 5 members who will eventually touch evidence... No. Let me cap at three: Junior Engineer (t16), Reviewer (t18), Lead (t23). Senior Engineer (t15) gets it implicitly? It edits mpd-workmate-plugin only; the dirty files don't include that package (only agent-teams lib/client.js and bundle-plugin). So t15 is unaffected. Good — skip Senior Engineer.\n\nWait, one more: t17 (Deep Worker, roles plugin) — is packages/mpd-roles-plugin dirty? Not in the list. Unaffected. Skip.\n\nSo: messages to Junior Engineer, Reviewer, Lead. Each will take a turn to acknowledge (cost) — acceptable for plan-critical info.\n\nHmm, actually, hold on. Let me reconsider messaging the Junior Engineer: it already knows (it raised the hazard and did the analysis). Its message asked me to \"pin the baseline\". A reply IS the answer to a direct request — appropriate. Yes.\n\nReviewer and Lead: they don't know. Send.\n\nNow — the other decisions to communicate:\n1. Vision Analyst: no task (browser tooling absent — verified: no chromium binary, no playwright package). Should I tell it? Messaging = a turn. It's idle and holds no work; the protocol says don't create work for unassigned members and don't wait for them. Telling it \"you have no task, stay idle\" costs a turn for zero value. Skip — but the user should know (in my summary).\n   Hmm, but the Vision Analyst explicitly offered and asked. Leaving a direct offer unanswered is slightly impolite but the cost/benefit favors silence; a running team's members shouldn't be woken for \"no work\". Actually... there's a subtle consideration: on delivery, t23 might want the visual QA. Not applicable (no browser).\n   Decision: skip messaging Vision Analyst; report the rationale to the user.\n2. Researcher: offered 3 lanes; I decided none is needed (lane 2 duplicates t2, lane 3 duplicates t19, lane 1 is nice-to-have that would arrive too late to inform t1/t15). Skip messaging (same cost logic). Report to the user.\n   Hmm, is there a case for lane 1 as a genuinely additive independent task? Consider: the contract t1 will pin semantics for tombstones/aliases — external prior art COULD change the design (e.g. \"keep a tombstone so old names can't be silently reused\"). But the user already decided: archive-first delete, in-use refusal, full cascade. A tombstone-on-rename question remains open (momus raised it as Q5: old name freed immediately → potential identity swap). Planner's t1 contract should decide it. If I had a Researcher, it could... eh. The contract will decide; a later user decision can adjust. Keep it lean. Skip.\n\nSo: 3 messages now. Then monitor.\n\nActually, wait. Should I even message Lead/Reviewer now, or wait until they're actually woken by the scheduler? A message to an idle member starts a turn immediately — so they'd wake now, note it, and idle again. Then when their task becomes ready, the scheduler wakes them again to work. The guidance persists in their context? Members have their own session; the message lands in their mailbox and is delivered as a turn. When the scheduler later wakes them, do they still see the earlier mail? Their session is durable (\"durable continuable member\"), so yes, prior turns persist in their context. Good — guidance survives.\n\nCost: 3 extra turns now. Worth it for plan-critical baseline + artifact facts? For the Junior Engineer, yes (resolves its blocker). For Reviewer/Lead, moderate. I'll send all three — the alternative (a reviewer reporting 33 dirty files as a blocker, or the Lead writing a misleading report) costs more.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider the Lead one more time: t23 is the LAST task; by then the messages... will the Lead still have them? Yes (durable session). OK.\n\nLet me send the 3 messages in one block (independent calls).\n\nThen compose my summary to the user:\n- Plan approved & running (I never approved — the user must have clicked Approve & Run; if they did, good; if not, tell me).\n  Hmm, careful: I should ask/confirm. Actually the team went from \"staged\" to \"running\" and t1/t2 got attempts — the only legitimate trigger is the user's approval (or the plugin's auto-start). I'll state it as: 我观察到计划已进入 running（t1/t2 已开始执行）——这正是 Web 面板 Approve 之后的行为；如果你并没有点批准，请告诉我，我会立刻处理。\n  And note: since the plan is locked, further edits need... hmm, edit_plan is refused. So any change to task text is impossible now; I'd have to rely on messages/reassignments.\n- t1 (Planner) and t2 (Explorer) are running; 9 members idle/ready, correctly not claiming blocked work.\n- The dirty-tree baseline: verified myself (33 entries, HEAD f697088), client.js is fresh (correcting the teammate's read), and I've sent guidance to the three members whose evidence/report it affects.\n- Vision Analyst / Researcher: no task — with reasons (verified absence of browser tooling; duplication).\n- Next: I'll monitor; writers start when t1 completes.\n\nLet me send the messages and then write this up. Keep the final message tight — the user's hands are on the approve button, not on a wall of text.\n\nActually, I should also set up monitoring: the loop will notify me on progress. I'll end the turn after messaging.\n\nLet me write the three messages concisely."}}}}
