evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:3294:{"type":"assistant/chunk","seq":83343,"time":1789027737911,"data":{"turn":3,"step":1,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":0,"block":{"type":"reasoning","text":"The Planner delivered a very strong contract. Key findings that need captain action:\n\n1. **The readonly defect root cause is a single dead name: `apply_patch`** (registered nowhere). `str_replace_editor` IS valid (host row dsh-base). Wait — my earlier evidence: when I ran `mpd_role_spawn`, the error was: `tools.restrict() names unknown global tools \"str_replace_editor\", \"apply_patch\"; known global tools: <the big list>`. The known list included... let me check: the list from the error did NOT include str_replace_editor (I scanned it: it had write, edit, mpd_hashline_edit, bash, read, glob, grep... no str_replace_editor, no apply_patch). Hmm! The Planner claims str_replace_editor is valid via \"host row dsh-base/cordis.patch.yml:424 → global layer\". But my observed error listed the known global tools and str_replace_editor was NOT among them...\n\nLet me re-read the error message from my earlier mpd_role_spawn call:\n\"Error: tools.restrict() names unknown global tools \"str_replace_editor\", \"apply_patch\"; known global tools: agent_teams_add_member, ..., mpd_hashline_edit, mpd_memory_save, ..., write, ralph, read, read_image, send_message, skill, ssh_*, subagent, subagent_fork, todo_write, update_goal, web_search, workflow\"\n\nI see \"read\", \"write\", \"edit\" (edit is in the list: \"...describe_image, edit, exit_plan_mode...\"), and mpd_hashline_edit — yes. But no str_replace_editor and no apply_patch.\n\nSo Planner's claim that str_replace_editor is valid contradicts my observed error listing (both names flagged as unknown in the SAME error). Hmm — unless the harness reports only the first unknown? No, it listed both.\n\nSo who's right? My observation is direct evidence from the live harness: BOTH names were reported unknown. The Planner's claim cites \"host row dsh-base/cordis.patch.yml:424\" — a config row, not the live registry. It's plausible that a row exists in a patch file but the row isn't composed in this profile, OR that the tool name in that row is different.\n\nThis is exactly the kind of claim the captain must verify before it propagates into t17 (which will \"fix\" the deny list). If t17 keeps `str_replace_editor` believing it's valid, the read-only spawns would STILL fail. That's a critical verification. My instruction: \"You are responsible for the final result: verify everything a subagent claims before presenting it as done.\" Also the Planner's warning not to use `dsh.hasTool` filtering — its reasoning: hasTool reads the global view only, so write/edit/bash (agent-plane) report false. That's a plausible and important insight, and it aligns with the Planner's claim that str_replace_editor lives on the \"global layer\" — but my error message was literally the list of \"known global tools\"! And it didn't include str_replace_editor.\n\nHmm, wait: maybe the error listed \"known global tools\" in a truncated form? The message I got was long and ended with \"...workflow\". Let me look again at the actual error text from the tool result:\n\n\"Error: tools.restrict() names unknown global tools \"str_replace_editor\", \"apply_patch\"; known global tools: agent_teams_add_member, agent_teams_approve, agent_teams_claim_task, agent_teams_create, agent_teams_create_task, agent_teams_delete, agent_teams_edit_plan, agent_teams_add_member, ... mpd_hashline_edit, ... write, ralph, read, read_image, send_message, skill, ssh_cluster, ... subagent, subagent_fork, todo_write, update_goal, web_search, workflow\"\n\nI need to check whether \"str_replace_editor\" appears in that list. From my reading of the tool output, the list included: ..., mcp__nowledge_mem__*, mpd_boulder_*, mpd_comment_check, mpd_config_*, mpd_hashline_*, mpd_memory_*, mpd_modelchain_resolve, mpd_role_persona, mpd_role_spawn, mpd_roles_list, mpd_ultrawork, mpd_ulw, mpd_verif_*, mpd_workmate_*..., ralph, read, read_image, send_message, skill, ssh_*, subagent, subagent_fork, todo_write, update_goal, web_search, workflow.\n\nNo str_replace_editor. So the Planner's claim #1 is WRONG about str_replace_editor (at least in this profile/session). And it explicitly told me \"Do not let anyone 'clean up' those\" — meaning t17 might keep a dead name and the fix would be incomplete.\n\nThis is a high-value captain intervention: I must verify empirically and correct the record before t17 executes. t17 is assigned to Deep Worker and is reportedly unblocked now (t1 complete). The risk window is short.\n\nHow to verify empirically? The best evidence: attempt a restrict() call with each name. Can I do that? I can call `mpd_role_spawn` for a read-only role — that's the failing path itself; the error message lists unknown names. If only apply_patch is unknown, the error would say so. That's a direct empirical test! Let me now re-run mpd_role_spawn with a read-only role (e.g. oracle/Architect, or librarian) and read the error. If the error now says only \"apply_patch\", the fix list differs.\n\nBut careful: mpd_role_spawn spawns a subagent — if the restriction SUCCEEDS (which it can't until t17 fixes it) it would start a live LLM child (cost). Given the deny list is still broken, it will fail with the unknown-name error — exactly the evidence I want. Low cost, high value.\n\nAlso I should check the host patch row the Planner cited: `/root/.nvm/.../@deepseek-ai/dsh/...` hmm, it said \"dsh-base/cordis.patch.yml:424\". Let me grep the dsh checkout for str_replace_editor to see if it's registered anywhere. That's cheap and decisive too: `grep -rn \"str_replace_editor\" <dsh checkout>`.\n\nLet me do both in parallel:\n1. bash: grep for str_replace_editor / apply_patch in the DSH checkout + the bundle patch + profile config.\n2. mpd_role_spawn readonly attempt → capture the exact