evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:3417:{"type":"assistant/chunk","seq":86569,"time":1789027760794,"data":{"turn":3,"step":2,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":0,"block":{"type":"reasoning","text":"Now I have decisive evidence:\n\n**The live harness error (twice observed, identical):**\n\"tools.restrict() names unknown global tools \"str_replace_editor\", \"apply_patch\"; known global tools: <list>\"\n\nAnd the list of \"known global tools\" definitively does NOT include `str_replace_editor` or `apply_patch`. The DSH checkout has a *package* named `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-str-replace-editor` (it exists in node_modules!) — so the tool EXISTS in the install, but it is NOT registered as a global tool in this profile/session. The Planner conflated \"a row exists in dsh-base/cordis.patch.yml\" with \"registered in the live registry of this session/profile\". My error evidence is authoritative for the actual spawn path.\n\nSo both names are wrong for THIS profile. The safest fix: the deny list must contain only names the live registry actually has — and both `str_replace_editor` and `apply_patch` fail the live check. Since `restrict()` throws on ANY unknown name, keeping `str_replace_editor` = read-only spawns STILL fail. This is critical and the Planner told implementers \"do not remove str_replace_editor\".\n\nHmm, but wait — one nuance: does the harness throw only for the FIRST unknown name or does it list all unknown names? The message lists both together, so it validates the whole list and reports all unknown ones. Both are unknown. Confirmed by the \"known global tools:\" enumeration which lacks both.\n\nAlso note the tool name in the checkout package is `dsh-tool-str-replace-editor` — that package presumably registers a tool named `str_replace_editor`, but it isn't loaded in this profile (the mpd preset declares native tool presentation with write/edit/etc.).\n\nSo: the correct fix for t17 (roles) and t15 (workmate side) is to remove BOTH `str_replace_editor` and `apply_patch` from the deny lists. And the Planner's \"hasTool is forbidden\" warning is a *good* caution but has a subtlety: my measured evidence says `hasTool` (global view) returns... for `write`: unknown. The Planner claims write/edit/bash are agent-plane, so `dsh.hasTool('write')` = false, and filtering would drop them → un-guarded children. Plausible — I can't fully verify without running, but I can partially: is there a way to check? The harness has `dsh.capabilities()`/`hasTool`. I could write a tiny probe... that's implementation work.\n\nA robust fix that satisfies both constraints: hardcode a curated list of names that are VALID in this harness (write, edit, mpd_hashline_edit — all three appear in the live known-tools list I observed; `str_replace_editor` and `apply_patch` do NOT) and drop the two dead names. Optionally add a defensive filter that only removes names known to be optional MCP tools.\n\nLet me verify from my observed list: `write` ✓ present, `edit` ✓ present, `mpd_hashline_edit` ✓ present. So deny = [\"write\",\"edit\",\"mpd_hashline_edit\"] is valid per the live registry. \n\nSo the correct deny list = the intersection of the current list with the live known-global-tools list = {write, edit, mpd_hashline_edit}. Both dead names removed.\n\nNow: how do I get this to t17 (Deep Worker) and t15 (Senior Engineer) before they implement? They're unblocked now (t1 passed). t15/t16/t17 likely started or will start within seconds/minutes. I must message them NOW with the correction. Messages to running members are delivered at their nearest step boundary — good, that's ideal (steering without disrupting).\n\nLet me send:\n1. To Deep Worker (t17): correction — BOTH names must go; evidence is the live error listing known global tools (enumerate that write/edit/mpd_hashline_edit are present, str_replace_editor/apply_patch absent); the Planner's dsh-base row claim refers to a package/row that is not