in members? The Planner says each has members named Planner/Researcher/…. Fine.\n\nLet me also consider: should I tell t15 to make the refusal message actionable (name blocking team + member)? The contract already requires that. Good.\n\nOne more important thing: my message to t15 must not conflict with the t1 contract's \"do not remove str_replace_editor\" instruction. My correction is authoritative (captain + live evidence). I'll be explicit: \"If the t1 contract says keep str_replace_editor, this correction overrides it — here is the live evidence.\"\n\nLet me also record a memory entry about this correction (self-iteration + durable lesson). Yes — it's a genuine lesson: \"row in a patch file ≠ registered in the live profile registry; verify against the live error's known-tools list\".\n\nLet me send the two messages + write the memory entry, then report to the user.\n\nNote: sending messages to running members is exactly the sanctioned steering path.\n\nLet me do it: 2 send_message calls + 1 memory write, in parallel (independent).\n\nWait — is Deep Worker running t17 now? Status earlier showed t17 pending; after t1 completed, t15/t16/t17 become ready. The scheduler may have started them. Either way, the message lands at the nearest step boundary or starts a turn. Ideal.\n\nLet me craft the messages precisely.\n\nMessage to Deep Worker (t17):\n- Correction to t1 finding #1 (verified live, twice).\n- Evidence: the restrict error enumerates \"known global tools\"; that list contains write, edit, mpd_hashline_edit, read, bash... and does NOT contain str_replace_editor or apply_patch. So BOTH names are dead in this profile.\n- Why the contract was wrong: the `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-str-replace-editor` package exists in the install and a dsh-base row references it, but it is not registered in this profile's live registry — the spawn path validates against the live registry, so a patch row is not proof.\n- Correct deny list: [\"write\",\"edit\",\"mpd_hashline_edit\"] (