evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:3604:{"type":"assistant/chunk","seq":90662,"time":1789029034999,"data":{"turn":4,"step":2,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":0,"block":{"type":"reasoning","text":"Current state:\n- t2 shows `in_progress` attempt 1, assigned to Explorer, but Explorer is `idle/ready`. So the Explorer's turn ended WITHOUT completing or failing the task — i.e., the member went idle while the attempt remains open (a stalled/abandoned attempt). The user observed \"t2 好像执行失败了\" — from their view (Web panel), t2 looks stuck/failed.\n\nSo the attempt is orphaned: the member is idle, the task is not terminal. The scheduler won't re-run it because the attempt is still held.\n\nCaptain action: `agent_teams_reassign_task` on t2. Options:\n- Reassign to the SAME member (Explorer) to retry: \"reason: attempt 1 ended without a terminal update (member idle, task still in_progress)...\"\n- Reassign to another member: Researcher (idle, offered exactly this lane).\n\nWhich is better? The Explorer may have crashed (context limit? model issue?). Retrying the same member is the protocol-preferred first option (\"Prefer another idle member or a retry with the same member\"). Actually the guidance says: \"If work must change owner, restart from scratch, or be taken over, call agent_teams_reassign_task first. Prefer another idle member or a retry with the same member.\"\n\nHmm. Since Explorer already burned an attempt and went idle without output, and Researcher explicitly offered the exact same content (\"Repo evidence pack for the current workmate identity model: exact file:line ... so t1's wire protocol and t15's safety contract are grounded\") — but the dossier (t2) is broader than that lane. Whoever takes it should deliver the full traversal dossier.\n\nI'll retry with the same member first? Or switch to Researcher? Consider: if Explorer failed due to a systemic issue (e.g. the dossier is too big for one turn), a retry with the same member may fail again. But reassigning to Researcher is also untested.\n\nThe user asked me to \"调度一下\" (dispatch/handle it). Best: reassign to **Researcher** — a fresh, idle member that volunteered for this exact work — and explicitly narrow the task description to what the user actually needs? I can't edit the task description (plan locked), but I can send guidance to the new owner: split the dossier into a focused, deliverable-in-one-turn form (structured, file:line-anchored, bounded length), and mention the risk of over-long output.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, the likely failure mode: Explorer tried to produce an enormous dossier and exceeded a limit, or the run crashed. A retry with clear \"bounded output\" guidance improves the odds.\n\nDecision: reassign t2 → Researcher, with a message giving: bounded scope (deliver in ≤ ~200 lines of structured text), the exact sections needed (the ones from the original description), explicit instruction to read the real sources and cite file:line, and NOT to duplicate t1's contract (which already covers the ground truth for the workmate internals) — instead focus on the whole-repo map + the workmate coupling/stale-surface map, which is what the user asked for (\"遍历工程，了解工程详细信息\").\n\nHmm, but hold on: should I keep Explorer? The protocol's reassign revokes the old attempt and interrupts the member... Explorer is already idle. Reassigning to Researcher is clean.\n\nAlso: after reassigning, the new owner must claim it (the reassign gives it to a member who then claims). Per the tool: \"Atomically retry, reassign, or let the captain take over one ready unfinished/failed task.\" So reassign_task(t2, \"Researcher\", reason).\n\nThen send guidance to Researcher with the bounded deliverable spec.\n\nAlso verify afterwards that t2 moves. And maybe the Explorer should be told why it lost the task? Not necessary; reassignment handles the attempt revocation.\n\nOne more consideration: is t2 (kind=work) still failing to proceed because the member is idle but the attempt open? Reassign should fix it. Let's do it.\n\nAlso — should I check whether the failure was a crash (e.g. no output at all)? The status shows no output for t2 (t1 had output text; t2 shows nothing). So the attempt produced nothing. Consistent with a crash.\n\nAdditionally, since the user asked me to handle it, I might also consider taking the dossier myself (captain takeover) — but the instruction says a captain takeover should only be for a task I'll drive to terminal in this same turn. The dossier is a big read-only research job; I already did substantial traversal myself and could write a solid dossier from my own findings + a few extra reads. Hmm — that's actually attractive: it guarantees a result, and I have most of the facts (I did the recon at session start). But it would consume my context and the turn. Also the protocol prefers delegation. Let me delegate to Researcher (fresh member) with tight guidance, and if it fails again, I'll take it over myself.\n\nLet me act:\n1. agent_teams_reassign_task(t2, \"Researcher\", reason=\"...\")\n2. agent_teams_send_message(to Researcher, guidance)\n\nBoth can go in one block? The message should be delivered to Researcher; sending right after reassign is fine (both independent calls — but ordering matters logically: assign then message). They're separate tools; if sent in the same block, order isn't guaranteed. Safer: reassign first, then message. Actually parallel calls in one block are executed... probably in order, but let me be safe and sequence: reassign first (it's the critical action), then message.\n\nLet me do the reassign now."}}}}
