evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:3947:{"type":"assistant/chunk","seq":98104,"time":1789029096564,"data":{"turn":4,"step":10,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":0,"block":{"type":"reasoning","text":"Messages are queued in the mailbox (Junior Engineer now has 2) but members stay idle and nothing runs. So the mailbox delivery is NOT starting turns right now. The team appears stalled: the loop says \"running\" but no member is being woken.\n\nThis looks like a systemic stall of the member runtime in this deployment. The user said \"t2 好像执行失败了，你调度一下\" — they want progress.\n\nOptions:\nA. Keep trying to poke the plugin (messages, reassigns) — evidence so far: ineffective.\nB. **Captain takes over the work directly.** I can do the work myself: I'm the main agent with full tools. I already own the requirements corrections. The user's request (rename + delete workmate, traversal dossier) can be executed by me directly, verified by me, with the team as a (currently stalled) parallel track.\n\nGiven \"You are responsible for the final result\" and the stalled team, taking over is the responsible path — but the protocol warns: \"Do not claim implementation or review yourself unless the user asked the captain to take over.\" Hmm! That's an explicit constraint. However, the user just said \"你调度一下\" (you dispatch/schedule it) — that's about dispatching the team, not about me writing the code.\n\nLet me think about what's the best action for the user:\n- The team is stalled at the runtime level (no member turns executing). My dispatch tools (reassign, message) don't wake them.\n- The user's actual goal: workmate rename + delete implemented and verified.\n\nPossible additional dispatch mechanisms I haven't tried:\n1. `agent_teams_claim_task` with assignee=<member> — \"Claim one ready task for a member (or yourself). A member cannot own a second unfinished task.\" This is the captain-side claim that returns an attempt_id. Maybe claiming starts the member's run? Hmm, claim_task for a member issues the attempt; the member then needs a turn. But maybe the plugin's live delivery triggers on claim? Unclear.\n2. `agent_teams_resume` — for halted teams. The status says loop \"running\", not halted. But maybe the team IS effectively halted (\"Delivery: blocked\")? The protocol: \"halted means the human stopped the team; call agent_teams_resume before creating more work.\" The status doesn't say halted. Hmm — but is there a \"halted\" indicator? The status header says \"Loop: running\". So not halted.\n3. `agent_teams_create_task({resume: true, resumeReason})` — that's for halted teams.\n4. Maybe the members need to be *spawned* — the status shows \"idle/ready\" which per list_agents semantics means \"exists only in storage — resumable, not terminal\". For agent_teams, \"idle/ready\" might mean the member's process isn't running. A message should start a turn (\"if it is idle, the message starts a turn\" — that's the subagent tool's semantics; for agent_teams members, \"member recipients get the message as their next turn\").\n\nSo messages SHOULD start their turns. They didn't (yet). Maybe there's latency — the delivery is asynchronous and the status snapshot is immediate. The messages I sent seconds ago may take effect shortly. I shouldn't conclude it's broken after one instant check.\n\nLet me wait a bit and re-check: use a bounded wait via a sleep in bash (e.g. 30-45s) then status. That's not busy-polling; it's a single delayed check.\n\nActually, better: send the kick messages to the remaining two (t15/t17 owners) now (they'd be needed anyway if the mechanism works), then wait ~60s and check the status once.\n\nHmm, but if the mechanism doesn't work, those messages are wasted (they'd sit in mailboxes). They're already needed for the corrections (already sent) — so no new messages needed except the kick. Let me send kicks to Senior Engineer and Deep Worker (t15/t17) and Researcher (t2) — one line each? That's 3 more messages... \n\nAlternatively use the strongest available lever: `agent_teams_claim_task` for each ready task assigned to its member. If the plugin treats a claim as the trigger to run the member... Let me test on ONE task (t16 → Junior Engineer) and see whether the member wakes. Combined with the message already sent.\n\nPlan:\n1. Send kick messages to Senior Engineer (t15), Deep Worker (t17), Researcher (t2) — short, action-forcing.\n2. Wait ~60s (sleep) and check status.\n3. If still idle: try claim_task for t16 and see; if that fails, tell the user plainly that the member runtime appears stuck and propose either (a) I do the work directly as captain (with their go-ahead), or (b) them restarting/interacting with the AgentTeams panel.\n\nActually, thinking about it more: the user's \"你调度一下\" plus the stalled runtime means the pragmatic answer may be: I do the work myself now (I have all context: the contract, the corrections), and the team remains as the review/verification layer if it comes back to life. But the protocol forbids captain implementation without the user asking... The user asking me to \"调度\" is a dispatch instruction, not a takeover instruction. Hmm. But they want the task done.\n\nBalanced approach: \n- Send the kicks (cheap, needed anyway).\n- Wait and verify.\n- If dead: tell the user the runtime is stalled, report exactly what I tried, and offer the takeover as an explicit option (one question). Meanwhile — to keep momentum without violating the \"don't implement yourself\" rule — I could do the T2 dossier myself (t2 is a read-only research task and I already hold most of the facts; and honestly the user asked me personally at session start to \"遍历工程\"). Hmm, but that's also captain takeover.\n\nLet me not over-rotate: first the kicks + a delayed check.\n\nLet me send the 3 kick messages now (they're genuinely needed to start those members if messages work), then sleep 60s, then status.\n\nMessage content: short, action-forcing, referencing the attempt/claim steps. Keep them brief to save tokens.\n\nActually — for Researcher (t2), its message is already detailed (I sent the bounded spec). A kick is redundant... but a second message might be what triggers the turn. Since message delivery seems to not be triggering turns, adding more messages may be pointless. Let me send ONLY to Deep Worker and Senior Engineer (short kicks, since their corrections were sent as guidance, not as \"start now\" orders) — and skip Researcher (already has an explicit \"claim it and deliver in ONE turn\" instruction).\n\nThen wait 60s and check."}}}}
