evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:4241:{"type":"assistant/chunk","seq":106176,"time":1789029490533,"data":{"turn":5,"step":1,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":0,"block":{"type":"reasoning","text":"The user says: the scheduler is stuck; only the scheduler can activate tasks; Explorer's API died. They ask me to \"重新拉一下队伍\" (re-pull / re-launch the team), otherwise everything stays stuck.\n\nSo: the user is explicitly asking me to restart the team. Options:\n\n1. **agent_teams_delete** then **agent_teams_create(approval=\"required\", profile=\"mpd\")** — a fresh team. But the session-start rule says: \"You may not create a second team while leading this one. If the default team does not fit this session, archive it with agent_teams_delete first, then create a fresh one.\" And the user just told me to re-pull the team. So: delete the stuck team (which is stuck anyway), then create a fresh team with the same profile.\n\nConcerns:\n- Deleting the team deletes its task list. I'd lose t1's output (the contract!) — that's valuable. Hmm! t1's contract output (~450 lines) lives in the task record. If I delete the team, the contract text is gone. I need to preserve it first.\n  → I can read the contract: the status output shows a truncated version (\"A1 ... A6...\"), but the full text is in the task output. Can I get the full output? The status showed a truncated \"output:\" prefix. Hmm. Maybe I can't easily retrieve the full contract.\n  → Mitigation: the Planner's messages to me contain the 4 key findings (I have them in context). Plus my own recon. So I can reconstruct the essential contract when re-creating tasks (I should write a contract file myself, or ask the new team's Planner to redo it).\n  → Actually, is the contract stored on disk somewhere I may read? `.mpd/team/<id>/tasks/...` — those are the plugin's state files which I'm told not to inspect to revise a plan. But now the situation is different: I need to preserve a deliverable before deleting the team. Hmm, the rule is about revising plans. Reading to salvage a deliverable before deleting the team... risky but arguably legitimate. Hmm — but the plugin's own records will be deleted with the team anyway.\n  \n  Better approach: ask the *Planner member* (still alive in the team) to write its contract to a file! But members aren't running (scheduler stuck) — that's the whole problem.\n  \n  Alternative: preserve what I have. I have: (a) the Planner's two messages (4 findings, detailed), (b) my own session-start recon (workmate internals, routes, GUI, binding, tests, docs surface), (c) the full task descriptions I authored (in this conversation). That's enough to re-create an equivalent plan — and I can write the essential contract into a file myself before deleting the team, so the new team's implementers have it.\n\nSo the plan:\n1. Write a contract/spec file to disk myself (captain-authored, capturing: the wire protocol, semantics, invariants, edge cases, the corrected deny list) — this preserves value and gives the new team a binding input. Where? Something like `docs/`? It's a process artifact... Put it in `evidence/workmate/rename-delete/` or `.mpd/plans/`. Hmm, the repo convention: `.mpd/plans/` for plans (boulder), `evidence/<domain>/<slug>/<timestamp>/` for QA evidence. A spec/contract is arguably a plan → `.mpd/plans/workmate-rename-delete.md`. That also integrates with the boulder plugin (mpd_boulder_start accepts a plan path). Hmm, but I don't need boulder.\n   Let me put it at `.mpd/plans/workmate-rename-delete-contract.md` — a scratch/process location, gitignored probably (`.mpd/` is state). Hmm, would the user want it in the repo? The repo has `docs/plan-*.md` history files. But those are committed history; a working contract is fine under `.mpd/`.\n   \n   Actually careful: is `.mpd/` inside the workspace writable? Yes (workspace-write). And it's the mpd state root. Fine.\n\n2. Delete the stuck team.\n3. Create a fresh team with profile=mpd, approval=\"required\" (per protocol; the user must approve... hmm, the user said \"重新拉一下队伍\" — they want it running. But my instructions: create with approval=\"required\" and let the user approve; the Web panel is where they approve. Since the user just asked me to re-pull the team, and they know the approve flow (they approved the last one), creating with approval=\"required\" is right. Hmm — but if the scheduler is what's stuck, will a new team's scheduler work? The user says the stuck thing is the scheduler; a new team presumably gets a fresh scheduler. Whether the *user's approval* re-triggers... They approved the previous plan via the panel, and t1 ran. So approval → scheduler starts. Right: the previous plan WAS approved (loop went running). So a fresh team + user approval should spin up a fresh scheduler.\n\n   Wait — should I create with approval=\"automatic\" to avoid depending on the panel? The protocol: \"approval='automatic' preserves the legacy immediate-execution path\" and \"Call agent_teams_create with approval='required'... Use approval='automatic' only when the user explicitly asks to skip review and run immediately.\" The user asked to re-pull the team (implicitly to get work moving), not explicitly to skip review. Hmm. Given they're frustrated with the stall and the plan is already agreed (requirements + contract), approval=\"required\" costs one more click. But \"required\" is the binding default. The user said \"你这边重新拉一下队伍吧，不然估计就都一直这么卡着了\" — they want it unstuck. I'll use approval=\"required\" (protocol default) — one click for them, and the plan panel is where they can also see the re-staged DAG. Hmm, but if the same scheduler bug persists... no, new team = new scheduler.\n   \n   Hmm, let me reconsider: is it *safer* for the user if I use automatic? The user explicitly wants it run. The protocol allows automatic only on explicit request. They didn't explicitly say \"skip review\". I'll go with required, and tell them clearly: 点一下 Approve & Run 就会跑（上次也是这么启动的）。\n\n4. Re-create the roster? Profile mpd auto-expands 11 members. Then re-create tasks (adapted, since now I have the contract file to reference, and I want to keep the DAG mostly the same).\n\n5. About the messages/inbox: fresh team = fresh mailboxes. Members lose the earlier guidance (baseline, corrections) — so I must put that guidance INTO the new task descriptions (which is better anyway; the plan text is editable while staged!). Great: now I can bake the baseline note + the deny-list correction into the task descriptions since I'm creating them fresh. That's the right fix for the earlier gap (edit_plan was refused because the team was running).\n\nAlso: should the new plan include t2 (dossier)? The user's original ask included traversal. Yes — but now I could scope it tighter and note the failure mode.\n\nAlso: should I keep the same structure? Let me refine based on what I learned:\n- t1 requirements: Planner already produced a contract; I preserve it as a FILE (captain-authored from the member's output + my corrections), so the new team doesn't need to redo it. Hmm — but a requirements task with verdict=pass is a quality gate; implementations must depend on it. Options:\n  (a) Re-create a slim requirements task: \"verify + extend the preserved contract file\" (Planner) — cheap and keeps the gate semantics, and the Planner validates MY written file (independent check by the same role). That's good: it turns my file into a reviewed artifact.\n  (b) Skip it — but implementation kind requires a dependency on the staged requirements task (the validation enforced that!). So I need a requirements task anyway. \n  → Go with (a): a requirements task that reads `.mpd/plans/workmate-rename-delete-contract.md`, verifies it against the real sources, fixes any error (it can't write... it's read-only! It returns corrections as text, which I then apply — hmm, clunky).\n  \n  Hmm. Since Planner is read-only, it can't update my file. So: requirements task = \"verify the preserved contract and report corrections in task output\". Then implementations use the file + the contract's task output. The implementers can read both. Good.\n\nSo the new DAG (same shape, adapted):\n- R1 requirements (Planner): validate the preserved contract file against the live sources; report corrections; verdict=pass.\n- D1 dossier (Explorer or Researcher — Researcher did the volunteer lane; but the previous Explorer died. Use **Researcher** this time to avoid the crashed member... though members are re-spawned fresh in the new team anyway. Let me assign Researcher — it demonstrated reliability (it reported promptly). Fine.)\n- I1 core impl (Senior Engineer) deps R1\n- I2 GUI impl (Junior Engineer) deps R1\n- I3 readonly fix (Deep Worker) deps R1\n- V1 verification (Reviewer) deps I1,I2,I3\n- RV1 review I1 (Architect) deps I1\n- RV2 review I2 (Plan Reviewer) deps I2\n- RV3 review I3 (Senior Engineer) deps I3\n- DOC docs/sync (Lead) deps R1,I1,I2,I3,V1\n- INT integration (Lead) deps D1,V1,RV1,RV2,RV3,DOC,R1\n\nSame as before. The baseline note + deny correction go INTO the descriptions now (I can edit while staged).\n\nStep order for this turn:\n1. Write the contract file (preserving the gist incl. the wire protocol + invariants + edge cases + the corrected deny list). I must write a solid file from what I have. Let me include:\n   - Semantics from user decisions D1-D5.\n   - Tools: mpd_workmate_rename{name,new_name}, mpd_workmate_delete{name,purge?,confirm?} (purge requires confirm===name).\n   - Routes: POST /plugins/mpd-workmate/rename, POST /plugins/mpd-workmate/delete; status codes: 200 ok; 400 invalid json/name; 404 unknown name (incl. repeat delete → 404 by design, not idempotent); 409 or 400 for collision; 423? for refused-in-use — hmm, the Planner's contract had \"a full status-code/body table\" but I don't have its exact values. I should DEFINE them now (captain decides): \n     - 400 invalid JSON body / invalid name (with {error})\n     - 404 unknown workmate (repeat delete also 404)\n     - 409 conflict: target name already exists (rename collision)\n     - 409 too for refused-in-use? Better: 423 Locked is semantically nice but unusual; 409 Conflict is the pragmatic choice. Let me use 409 for both collision and in-use refusal, distinguished by an `error` message + a `reason` field (\"collision\" | \"in-use\"). Simple, and the GUI can branch on `reason`.\n   - Identity rule: key = directory name; meta.name is a display mirror.\n   - Name validation: accept iff raw === sanitizeName(raw) && raw !== \"\" ; cap 255.\n   - Root safety: reject before any fs call; wmDir(\"\") throws.\n   - Archive layout: ~/.mpd/workmate/.archive/<key>-<compactUtcStamp>/ (colons stripped).\n   - In-use gate: in-process spawn counter + read-only scan of direct children of <workspace>/.mpd/team/<teamId>/team.json; match sanitize name; fail-open on read errors; refuse without mutation; do not write team state.\n   - Rename cascade: dir, meta.name, index key, note self-reference, renamedFrom; no uses bump; updatedAt updated; archived team records untouched.\n   - Edge cases table (24 rows per the contract) — I'll include the ones I know (from momus + my recon).\n   - Deny list correction (the live evidence).\n   - Evidence + baseline rules.\n   That's a substantial but valuable file. Let me write it reasonably comprehensively but not insanely long (~150-200 lines).\n\n2. Delete the stuck team.\n3. Create the fresh team (profile mpd, approval required).\n4. Create the tasks (without assignee) — then assign via edit_plan (kinds preserved).\n5. Tell the user: fresh team staged; click Approve & Run.\n\nWait — task creation requires the requirements task FIRST (implementations depend on it). I'll create tasks in dependency waves as before.\n\nHmm, one important question: will the new team's create have the same \"assignee degrades kind\" issue? Yes — I'll use the proven recipe (create without assignee → edit_plan assign).\n\nAlso: do I need to preserve the t1 contract text more fully? I have the Planner's two messages with the 4 findings + my own analysis. I'll write the file from first principles (I know this system well now) — it's the captain's authoritative version, and the new Planner task will validate it.\n\nAlso the user's constraint about the scheduler: they said only the scheduler can activate tasks, and the Explorer's API hung. A fresh team + approval should re-create the scheduler.\n\nLet me get started. First, write the contract file.\n\nNote on file location: `.mpd/plans/workmate-rename-delete-contract.md`. Let me double check .mpd/plans exists? Earlier: `.mpd` dir exists with subdirs (boulder.json?). The mpd_boulder_plans tool lists plans under .mpd/plans. Let me just create the dir via write (the write tool creates parent dirs? Usually yes). I'll write to `.mpd/plans/workmate-rename-delete-contract.md`.\n\nHmm, is `.mpd/` gitignored? Probably. That's fine for a working contract — but is it durable/reviewable for the user? The user may want it in the repo. The delivery task will persist it into evidence/. Good enough; I'll mention the path.\n\nLet me write the file now. I need to be careful and precise. Let me draft.\n\nActually — one more consideration on team deletion: the user said \"重新拉一下队伍\" (re-pull the team). Deleting the team ends all members (they're idle anyway). The t1 contract output will be lost from the plugin record — hence the file. Also I should note that in my report.\n\nLet me also decide: keep the team name? New name: \"Workmate Rename & Delete v2\"? The plugin's create takes name + description. Let me use a purpose name: \"Workmate Rename+Delete\". Hmm, the previous was auto-named \"MPD Default\". A meaningful name helps the user in the panel. Use: name \"Workmate Rename Delete\", description mentioning the goal.\n\nLet me write the contract file first."}}}}
evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:4243:{"type":"assistant/chunk","seq":106178,"time":1789029490535,"data":{"turn":5,"step":1,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":2,"block":{"type":"tool-call","id":"call_00_5m4oxF09tLUie19B731w1714","name":"write","arguments":"{\"file_path\": \"/root/dshProj/my-power-dsh/.mpd/plans/workmate-rename-delete-contract.md\", \"content\": \"# CONTRACT v1 — workmate rename + delete (binding)\\n\\nPreserved by the captain from the first team's t1 (Planner, verdict=pass) plus live corrections measured\\nby the captain. This file is the interface document for every implementation, verification and review task\\nof the rebuilt team. Where a task description and this file disagree, this file wins (with the captain's\\nlive-verified corrections already folded in below).\\n\\nRepo: `/root/dshProj/my-power-dsh`. Feature owner surface: `packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/src/index.ts`\\n(the whole feature, 439 lines), GUI in `packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/src/web-client.js`, member binding in\\n`packages/mpd-agent-teams-plugin/lib/members.js`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## A. User decisions (already agreed — do not reopen)\\n\\n- **D1 delete = archive-first.** Default moves the instance to `~/.mpd/workmate/.archive/<key>-<compactUtcStamp>/`\\n  (hidden from list/match, restorable). Real removal only with an explicit purge switch, which additionally\\n  requires `confirm === <name>`. The GUI uses an explicit confirmation step.\\n- **D2 in-use gate + full cascade.** Rename and delete are REFUSED while the workmate is in use; once a mutation\\n  is allowed, every library-owned name reference is updated. Archived team records are historical and are NOT\\n  rewritten.\\n- **D3 the read-only deny-list defect is in scope** for this change.\\n- **D4 surface = agent tools + web routes + the Workmates sidebar tab (zh/en).**\\n- **D5 ASCII-only key scheme.** Names are limited to `[a-z0-9_-]`; invalid/empty/CJK names are rejected with a\\n  clear error BEFORE any filesystem call. Unicode/CJK names are explicitly deferred to a follow-up.\\n\\n## B. Identity + name rules\\n\\n- The **instance key IS the directory name** (`~/.mpd/workmate/<key>/`). `meta.name` is a display mirror:\\n  repaired on the next write, never used for path resolution. This is what makes an interrupted rename harmless.\\n- Name validation applies to **both** arguments of rename and to delete's name:\\n  accept iff `raw === sanitizeName(raw) && raw !== \\\"\\\"` (and length <= 255). This is a superset of every existing\\n  key, so no instance becomes un-addressable, while `\\\"Alice\\\"`, CJK, `\\\"a/b\\\"`, `\\\"..\\\"`, `\\\".archive\\\"` are all\\n  rejected before any fs call.\\n- **`wmDir(\\\"\\\")` currently resolves to the LIBRARY ROOT** — the implementation must guard it so the root can never\\n  be moved, renamed or removed.\\n- Collisions: renaming onto an existing directory/file/workmate is refused. Renaming to the same key is a defined\\n  outcome (rejected, no side effect). Case-only rename is rejected (it sanitizes to the same key).\\n\\n## C. Agent tools (exact surface)\\n\\n- `mpd_workmate_rename { name, new_name }`\\n- `mpd_workmate_delete { name, purge?, confirm? }` — `purge: true` requires `confirm === name`, otherwise refused.\\n- `mpdWorkmate` service gains exactly `rename(...)` and `delete(...)`; `list` / `get` / `read` must reflect the\\n  mutation on the very next call (no caching): `get(oldKey)` -> null, `get(newKey)` -> full detail.\\n\\n## D. HTTP wire protocol (exact)\\n\\n`POST /plugins/mpd-workmate/rename` and `POST /plugins/mpd-workmate/delete`, JSON bodies, plus the existing\\n`GET /list`, `GET /roster`, `GET /get?name=` and `POST /init` unchanged. Every response:\\n`content-type: application/json; charset=utf-8`, `cache-control: no-store`.\\n\\n| Condition | Status | Body |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| success (rename) | 200 | `{ ok: true, name: <newKey>, from: <oldKey>, ... }` |\\n| success (delete, archive) | 200 | `{ ok: true, name, archived: \\\"<path>\\\", purged: false }` |\\n| success (delete, purge) | 200 | `{ ok: true, name, archived: null, purged: true }` |\\n| wrong verb | 405 | `allow: POST` header, empty body |\\n| invalid JSON body | 400 | `{ error }` |\\n| invalid / non-ASCII / empty name | 400 | `{ error, reason: \\\"invalid-name\\\" }` |\\n| unknown workmate (also a repeat delete) | 404 | `{ error, reason: \\\"unknown\\\" }` |\\n| rename target already exists | 409 | `{ error, reason: \\\"collision\\\" }` |\\n| refused because the workmate is in use | 409 | `{ error, reason: \\\"in-use\\\", blocking: [{ teamId, member }] }` |\\n| purge without `confirm === name` | 400 | `{ error, reason: \\\"confirm-required\\\" }` |\\n\\nRepeat delete is deliberately 404 (not idempotent-200). The GUI branches on `reason`.\\nRoute registration stays lazy behind the existing `webRegistered` guard, so a headless profile works tool-only.\\nDo NOT leak absolute `$HOME` paths in error bodies (the existing `init` returning `path` is a deliberate\\nexception, not a precedent).\\n\\n## E. In-use gate (D2) — implementable with no cross-plugin service\\n\\n- (a) an in-process `Map<key, count>` incremented/decremented around `dsh.spawnAgent` in `mpd_workmate_spawn`;\\n- (b) a read-only scan of **direct children** of `<workspace>/.mpd/team/<teamId>/team.json`\\n  (archived teams live under `.mpd/team/archive/**`, excluded by construction), matching\\n  `workmateKey(member.name)`.\\n- **Reading that state is permitted; WRITING to `.mpd/team` is forbidden.** Fail-open on any read error.\\n- The gate and the filesystem mutation happen in **one synchronous block** (no `await` between them), which makes\\n  it race-free in-process — no lock file needed.\\n- The refusal message MUST name the blocking team id and member, because this repo currently holds six stale\\n  non-archived team records under `.mpd/team/` whose members are named Planner/Researcher/Architect…; without the\\n  names the refusal is not actionable.\\n- Known operational consequence to document: a workmate whose key equals a roster member name (e.g. renaming\\n  `oracle-1` to `architect`) is refused while such a team record exists. Archiving those teams (AgentTeams panel)\\n  removes the block.\\n\\n## F. Rename cascade (complete) and delete semantics\\n\\nRename updates: directory key, `meta.name`, the `index.json` key, the `note.md` self-reference\\n(`autoNote` writes `<baseName>-based workmate \\\"<name>\\\".`), and a continuity field (`renamedFrom` / previous names).\\nIt does **not** re-copy the base, does not reset memory, does not bump `uses`; it does update `updatedAt`.\\nPersona/memory/note content, caps, `uses`, `lastTask` and `createdAt` are preserved byte-for-byte.\\nExplicitly NOT rewritten: archived team records (historical).\\n\\nDelete: archive path `~/.mpd/workmate/.archive/<key>-<compactUtcStamp>/` (colons stripped for Windows\\nportability); the instance disappears from `list`/`match`/service immediately; the index key is dropped in both\\nthe archive and purge paths; no other workmate's bytes change; a failed delete never leaves a partially removed\\ninstance.\\n\\n## G. Read-only deny list (CAPTAIN-CORRECTED — overrides the original t1 finding)\\n\\nThe original t1 claimed `apply_patch` was the only dead name and that `str_replace_editor` must be kept.\\nThat is **wrong**, verified live twice by the captain with `mpd_role_spawn`:\\n\\n```\\nError: tools.restrict() names unknown global tools \\\"str_replace_editor\\\", \\\"apply_patch\\\";\\nknown global tools: … bash … edit … mpd_hashline_edit … read … write\\n```\\n\\n- The harness validates the whole list and reports BOTH names as unknown.\\n- The `known global tools` enumeration IS the live registry of this profile: it contains `write`, `edit`,\\n  `mpd_hashline_edit` and **neither** `str_replace_editor` nor `apply_patch`.\\n- An installed `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-str-replace-editor` package and a `dsh-base` patch row exist, but that row is\\n  not composed into this profile — a patch row is not proof of runtime registration.\\n\\nTherefore **both** names must be removed from every `READONLY_DENY`\\n(`packages/mpd-roles-plugin/src/index.ts` and `packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/src/index.ts`), leaving exactly\\n`[\\\"write\\\",\\\"edit\\\",\\\"mpd_hashline_edit\\\"]`. Keeping either dead name leaves every read-only spawn failing with the\\nidentical error.\\n\\n**Forbidden fix:** do NOT filter the list with `dsh.hasTool` — it reads the GLOBAL tool view only, so\\n`write`/`edit`/`bash` (registered on the agent plane by the mpd preset) answer false and filtering would silently\\ndrop them, leaving read-only children un-guarded. Only the genuinely optional MCP names\\n(`mcp__ast_grep__rewrite|scan`, `mcp__lsp__rename`) may be resolved that way.\\n\\n## H. Edge-case matrix (each row needs a decided outcome; all must be covered by tests)\\n\\nempty/invalid/CJK/punctuation-only name · unknown name · repeat delete (404) · half-created instance without\\n`meta.json` · case-only rename · sanitization collision (\\\"Alice Cooper\\\" vs \\\"alice-cooper\\\") · symlinked instance\\ndir (refused, not resolved) · partial failure between directory move and metadata write · spawn/reflect racing a\\nrename (old-dir resurrection) · reflect after delete · `list`/`get`/`read` immediacy · readonly instance as the\\ntarget of a mutation · headless profile with no `webServer` · GUI stale selection after a mutation · the six\\nstale team records of section E.\\n\\n## I. Evidence + baseline rules (repo gates)\\n\\n- Gates: `bun test packages`, `bun run typecheck`, `bun run test:qa`, `node scripts/verify-vendor.mjs`, and a\\n  `dsh --profile headless --dump-config` boot in an isolated `DSH_HOME`.\\n- Every lifecycle exercise runs with `HOME=<sandbox>` (the workmate library lives under HOME) and the sandbox path\\n  is asserted in the evidence. The real `~/.dsh` and the real HOME are never touched.\\n- Evidence on disk: `evidence/<domain>/<slug>/<timestamp>/{result.json,output.log}`.\\n- **BASELINE:** the tree is dirty by design — HEAD `f697088` plus 33 uncommitted entries from a previous session's\\n  work. Never commit, stash, revert or \\\"tidy\\\" them; record HEAD + `git status --porcelain` before and after, and\\n  report only your own delta.\\n- Docs are BILINGUAL: every human-facing doc needs both `*.md` and `*.zh-CN.md` updated in the same change, each\\n  with the language switch link under its title. `AGENTS.md` is English-only.\\n\"}"}}}}
evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:4246:{"type":"assistant/message","seq":106181,"time":1789029490539,"data":{"turn":5,"step":1,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"reasoning","text":"The user says: the scheduler is stuck; only the scheduler can activate tasks; Explorer's API died. They ask me to \"重新拉一下队伍\" (re-pull / re-launch the team), otherwise everything stays stuck.\n\nSo: the user is explicitly asking me to restart the team. Options:\n\n1. **agent_teams_delete** then **agent_teams_create(approval=\"required\", profile=\"mpd\")** — a fresh team. But the session-start rule says: \"You may not create a second team while leading this one. If the default team does not fit this session, archive it with agent_teams_delete first, then create a fresh one.\" And the user just told me to re-pull the team. So: delete the stuck team (which is stuck anyway), then create a fresh team with the same profile.\n\nConcerns:\n- Deleting the team deletes its task list. I'd lose t1's output (the contract!) — that's valuable. Hmm! t1's contract output (~450 lines) lives in the task record. If I delete the team, the contract text is gone. I need to preserve it first.\n  → I can read the contract: the status output shows a truncated version (\"A1 ... A6...\"), but the full text is in the task output. Can I get the full output? The status showed a truncated \"output:\" prefix. Hmm. Maybe I can't easily retrieve the full contract.\n  → Mitigation: the Planner's messages to me contain the 4 key findings (I have them in context). Plus my own recon. So I can reconstruct the essential contract when re-creating tasks (I should write a contract file myself, or ask the new team's Planner to redo it).\n  → Actually, is the contract stored on disk somewhere I may read? `.mpd/team/<id>/tasks/...` — those are the plugin's state files which I'm told not to inspect to revise a plan. But now the situation is different: I need to