evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:5148:{"type":"assistant/chunk","seq":128589,"time":1789029694132,"data":{"turn":6,"step":4,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":0,"block":{"type":"reasoning","text":"Critical finding: **my contract §G wording is wrong for the roles list.** The actual roles READONLY_DENY is:\n\n```ts\nexport const READONLY_DENY = [\n  \"write\",\n  \"edit\",\n  \"str_replace_editor\",\n  \"apply_patch\",\n  \"mpd_hashline_edit\",\n  \"bash\",\n  \"mcp__ast_grep__rewrite\",\n  \"mcp__ast_grep__scan\",\n  \"mcp__lsp__rename\",\n]\n```\n\nAnd the Architect's message says: \"Note `bash` is correctly NOT in the deny list (read-only children still need read/bash) — keep it out.\"\n\nWAIT. That contradicts the file! The file DOES contain \"bash\" at line 29. And the roles test asserts bash ∈ writeTools ⊆ READONLY_DENY (\"no shell/AST/LSP write bypass\").\n\nThe Architect says \"keep it out\" — i.e., it believes bash is not in the list and should not be added. And Deep Worker said the list \"must leave exactly write, edit, mpd_hashline_edit, bash, mcp×3\" — i.e., KEEP bash (matching the file).\n\nSo we have a direct contradiction between two members:\n- Deep Worker (t5 owner): keep bash + MCP names → deny = [write, edit, mpd_hashline_edit, bash, mcp__ast_grep__rewrite, mcp__ast_grep__scan, mcp__lsp__rename] (7 names).\n- Architect (t7 reviewer, reviewing t3 though): \"bash is correctly NOT in the deny list — keep it out\" → deny = [write, edit, mpd_hashline_edit] (+MCP?).\n\nMy contract §G said: \"both names must be removed from every READONLY_DENY ... leaving exactly [\"write\",\"edit\",\"mpd_hashline_edit\"]\" — which would REMOVE bash and the MCP names from the roles list. That would be a **security regression**: read-only roles could then run `bash` (arbitrary writes via shell!) and the AST-grep rewrite / LSP rename MCP tools (which write files). The test's own name says exactly that: \"read-only deny list covers every write-capable tool (no shell/AST/LSP write bypass)\".\n\nSo my §G wording is dangerous and must be corrected NOW, before t5 (Deep Worker) executes. Deep Worker already read the file and pinned the correct 7-name list — good, it's on the right side. But §G (the binding doc) says otherwise, and t5's acceptance says \"contains exactly the live-registered subset (write, edit, mpd_hashline_edit)\". If Deep Worker follows the contract literally, it would delete bash + MCP names → security hole + the roles test would fail (since the test asserts they're present... well, the test must be rewritten anyway, but the intent is clear).\n\nActions (urgent, before t5 starts):\n1. **Fix the contract file §G** — precise per-file expected lists:\n   - roles: keep write, edit, mpd_hashline_edit, bash, mcp__ast_grep__rewrite, mcp__ast_grep__scan, mcp__lsp__rename (7); remove str_replace_editor, apply_patch.\n   - workmate: keep write, edit, mpd_hashline_edit (3); remove str_replace_editor, apply_patch.\n   Plus: rebuild both dists; fix test/roles.test.ts:106-107 to pin the new list (and add a guard that dead names are absent).\n2. **Correct Deep Worker (t5)** — confirm its reading is right (keep bash + MCP), and warn it NOT to follow my §G \"exactly write/edit/mpd_hashline_edit\" literally for the roles list; §G is being amended.\n3. **Correct the Architect's claim** — it asserted bash is not in the list and should stay out; the file shows bash IS in the list and the test treats bash as write-capable (shell bypass). Its other findings are excellent (dist rebuild, lstat/symlink, renameSync overwrite hazard, index old-key removal, finally-decrement for the in-use counter, `delete:` reserved-word property). I should relay the bash correction to it? The Architect is the t7 reviewer (of t3 — workmate side, where bash is NOT in the list). Its statement was about the roles list (t5). Since t9 reviews t5 (owned by Senior Engineer), the Architect's wrong claim could reach the reviewer... Actually, Architect said \"no action needed\" — it's guidance. To be safe, I'll correct it publicly to the t5 owner (Deep Worker) and the t9 reviewer (Senior Engineer) — those are the ones who will act on roles-list content. And note it in my report.\n\nHmm — wait, let me double check whether denying `bash` for read-only roles is actually intended. The roles test name: \"read-only deny list covers every write-capable tool (no shell/AST/LSP write bypass)\" and it includes \"bash\" in writeTools. So yes: the design intent is that read-only specialists cannot use bash (because bash can write). The Architect's claim that \"read-only children still need read/bash\" is a plausible-sounding but WRONG inference — read tools (read/glob/grep) are not denied; bash is denied deliberately because it can write. My live error message confirms bash is registered (valid name).\n\nSo the correct roles list = 7 names (drop the 2 dead ones).\n\n4. Also notify t9's owner (Senior Engineer) — it will review t5 against the contract; if §G says \"exactly 3 names\", it might flag the correct 7-name list as a deviation. Must correct.\n\nLet me do:\na) Edit the contract file §G (surgical edit).\nb) Send messages: Deep Worker (t5), Senior Engineer (t9 reviewer + t3 owner), Architect (courtesy correction + acknowledge its valuable findings). Hmm, messaging Architect wakes it (idle) — is it needed? Its claim could propagate if it later reviews... t7 reviews t3 (workmate side) where the workmate list IS 3 names. Its bash claim concerned the roles list, which it doesn't own. Risk is low, but its message is in MY context; other members don't see it. The ones who'd act wrongly are t5 (Deep Worker) and t9 (Senior Engineer). I'll message those two, and skip Architect (no waking). I'll mention the correction in my report.\n\nAlso for t3's owner (Senior Engineer): the workmate-side list is indeed just the 3 names; the Architect's other findings (lstat, renameSync overwrite, index old-key drop, finally-decrement, reserved word `delete:`) are valuable — Senior Engineer said it deliberately deferred reading src lines 114-397. A short message with those five implementation hazards would materially improve t3's quality. Since I'm already messaging Senior Engineer for the t9/§G correction, I can include the Architect's t3 hazards in the same message. Good — one message, two purposes.\n\nLet me also update the contract file with the Architect's t3-relevant hazards? They're implementation guidance, not contract semantics. The contract already covers symlink refusal and one-block gate. I'll add a short \"§J implementation hazards (pre-emptive, from reviewer recon)\" — hmm, contract should stay contract. But adding verified hazards helps. Let me add them compactly to §H area... Actually cleaner: add a new short section \"## J. Verified implementation hazards\" listing the 5 items with file:line. That's genuinely useful and it's my file.\n\nLet me do the edit + messages.\n\nEdit 1: §G paragraph. Current text:\n\n\"Therefore **both** names must be removed from every `READONLY_DENY`\n(`packages/mpd-roles-plugin/src/index.ts` and `packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/src/index.ts`), leaving exactly\n`[\"write\",\"edit\",\"mpd_hashline_edit\"]`. Keeping either dead name leaves every read-only spawn failing with the\nidentical error.\"\n\nReplace with a per-file table. Let me write it.\n\nThen append §J at the end of the file.\n\nLet me be precise about the current file contents to edit correctly. §G text I wrote:\n\n```\nTherefore **both** names must be removed from every `READONLY_DENY`\n(`packages/mpd-roles-plugin/src/index.ts` and `packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/src/index.ts`), leaving exactly\n`[\"write\",\"edit\",\"mpd_hashline_edit\"]`. Keeping either dead name leaves every read-only spawn failing with the\nidentical error.\n```\n\nLet me do the edit with the exact string (including newlines). I'll use the edit tool.\n\nNew text:\n\n```\nTherefore **both** names must be removed, and ONLY those two — each list keeps every other entry it already\nhas, because those entries are the read-only guarantee itself:\n\n| File | Ships as | Removed |\n|---|---|---|\n| `packages/mpd-roles-plugin/src/index.ts:23-33` | `write, edit, mpd_hashline_edit, bash, mcp__ast_grep__rewrite, mcp__ast_grep__scan, mcp__lsp__rename` | `str_replace_editor`, `apply_patch` |\n| `packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/src/index.ts:30` | `write, edit, mpd_hashline_edit` | `str_replace_editor`, `apply_patch` |\n\n**`bash` must stay denied in the roles list.** It is not a read tool: a shell can write files, and the existing\nregression `packages/mpd-roles-plugin/test/roles.test.ts:106` exists precisely to keep \"no shell/AST/LSP write\nbypass\". A claim that read-only children \"still need bash\" is wrong — `read`/`glob`/`grep` are not denied.\nLikewise `mcp__ast_grep__rewrite`, `mcp__ast_grep__scan` and `mcp__lsp__rename` are write-capable and stay denied\n(all three are live-registered, unlike the two dead names).\n\nBoth `dist/index.js` copies must be rebuilt in the same change; the test at\n`packages/mpd-roles-plugin/test/roles.test.ts:106-107` currently PINS the two dead names and must be re-pointed in\nthe same change (otherwise the suite goes red for the wrong reason), gaining a guard that the dead names cannot\ncome back. Keeping either dead name leaves every read-only spawn failing with the identical error.\n```\n\nThat's a solid amendment.\n\n§J: append at end:\n\n```\n## J. Verified implementation hazards (pre-emptive, file:line)\n\n- `fs.renameSync` on Linux silently OVERWRITES an existing empty target directory — an `existsSync(target)` check alone is not a sufficient collision guard; check with `lstatSync` and keep the check + rename in the SAME synchronous block as the in-use gate (§E).\n- Refuse a symlinked instance dir or symlinked target with `lstatSync` rather than resolving it (§H row).\n- `writeIndexEntry` (`packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/src/index.ts:104-108`) only ever SETS the new key: rename/delete must delete the OLD key and write the file once, or the stale key lingers in `index.json` forever.\n- Guard `wmDir(\"\")` (line 55) at the top of both tools: `raw === sanitizeName(raw) && raw !== \"\"` before any fs call.\n- `mpd_workmate_spawn` must decrement its in-process in-use counter in a `finally` (throw path included), or one failed spawn leaks a permanent in-use block.\n- `mpdWorkmate.delete` must be declared as an object-literal property `delete: (…) => …` — a bare `delete(...)` member is a parse error.\n```\n\nGood. Now messages.\n\nMessage to Deep Worker (t5 owner):\n- Your pinned 7-name list is CORRECT; my contract §G wording was too narrow and I have amended it.\n- Do NOT remove bash or the MCP names.\n- Must: rebuild dist, re-point roles.test.ts:106-107, add a guard against re-adding dead names.\n\nMessage to Senior Engineer (t3 + t9):\n- §G amended: roles list keeps bash + MCP names; the workmate list (your t3 line 30) is the 3-name list — so your t3 change is exactly that.\n- Warn: for your t9 review, judge t5 against the amended §G, not the original text.\n- Plus the Architect's 5 t3 hazards.\n\nLet me execute: edit file (2 edits: §G replace + §J append), then 2 messages. The edits are dependent? No, independent (different strings). I can do both edits in parallel — careful: both modify the same file. Parallel edits to the same file could conflict. Let me do them sequentially: first the §G replace, then the append. Actually the edit tool applies literal replacement; doing them in one block risks a race on the file. Safer sequential.\n\nLet me start with the §G edit."}}}}
