 the ROLES array it also deletes `bash`, `mcp__ast_grep__rewrite|scan`, `mcp__lsp__rename` — all live-known write bypasses — a policy regression, and it breaks `roles.test.ts:105-112`. Correct final arrays (decided):\n- `packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/src/index.ts:30` → `[\"write\", \"edit\", \"mpd_hashline_edit\"]` (drop only the 2 dead names)\n- `packages/mpd-roles-plugin/src/index.ts:23-33` → remove ONLY lines 26+27 → `[\"write\",\"edit\",\"mpd_hashline_edit\",\"bash\",\"mcp__ast_grep__rewrite\",\"mcp__ast_grep__scan\",\"mcp__lsp__rename\"]`\nTest sync required: `packages/mpd-roles-plugin/test/roles.test.ts:106-112` asserts the 9 names including both dead ones (it passes today); it MUST be rewritten to the 7-name list or the `bun test packages` gate goes red.\n\n**A2 — must-decide (recommend ALIGN).** The workmate array has no `bash` and no MCP write tools, so a readonly workmate could still write through bash. Today that is masked (every readonly spawn throws); this fix un-masks it. Recommend: both arrays carry the same 7 names + a test asserting the two arrays are equal (kills the drift class permanently). If declined, t10 must document the asymmetry.\n\n**A3 (t3) — fs paths must key on the DIRECTORY, never `meta.name`.** Today `listInstances()` (line 181) returns `meta.name` and reads the note via `meta.name`; `ensureInstance` returns only `{meta}` and reflect writes via `wmDir(meta.name)` (326-335) → after a rename (or an interrupted one) a later reflect can resurrect the OLD dir and `list` shows a name that no longer resolves. Decided: `ensureInstance` → `{meta, dir, key}` with `key = sanitizeName(name)`; every write uses `key`; `listInstances` returns `name: e.name`; get/read/list normalize `name` to the key; `writeIndexEntry` takes the key. Test: dir `alice` whose meta.json says \"stale\" → list shows alice, reflect writes alice/, index key alice.\n\n**A4 (t3) — `readMeta` (81-87) is a whitelist, so `renamedFrom` would be silently DROPPED on the next reflect.** Add optional `renamedFrom?: string[]` to `Meta` + readMeta (default `[]`), append the previous key on rename (dedupe, cap 10), and carry it through init/reflect/rename writes. Test: rename → reflect → `renamedFrom` survives and `get()` exposes it.\n\n**A5 (t3) — §F tension (\"note.md self-reference updated\" vs \"note content byte-for-byte\").** Decided rule: rewrite ONLY a leading `<baseName>-based workmate \"<oldKey>\".` prefix to the new key; if the note does not start with that prefix (custom note passed at init, line 242) leave the bytes untouched. This keeps `autoNote`'s `prev.startsWith(prefix)` dedupe (line 132) correct. Test both branches.\n\n**A6 (t6/t10) — the repo has 7 non-archived team records, not 6**, and every one carries the 11 roster member names. Blocked keys: architect, researcher, planner, explorer, reviewer, plan-reviewer, vision-analyst, lead, deep-worker, senior-engineer, junior-engineer. So (i) happy-path renames must avoid those keys (use `oracle-1` → `oracle-2`), and (ii) the 409 in-use path is free and deterministic HERE by renaming to `architect` — use that instead of fabricating team state. The scan design is unaffected: direct children only; `archive/` has no `archive/team.json` → excluded by construction; `retired-members.json` is a file → skipped by the isDirectory filter.\n\n**A7 (t4) — artifact + i18n gates.** `sidebar-tab.test.mjs` loads the COMMITTED `packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/client.js` (line 17) → t4 must run `node scripts/build-mpd-client.mjs` in the same change or the tests exercise stale bytes. Bundle rows point at `dist/index.js` (patch lines 149/174) → t3/t5 must rebuild `bun build src/index.ts --target node --format esm --outfile dist/index.js`. No zh/en key-parity test exists → t4 should add `Object.keys(zh).sort() === Object.keys(en).sort()`, since D4's zh/en requirement is currently unenforced. `request()` (205-217) throws `Error(body.error)` and drops `reason`/`blocking` → extend it, or §D's \"the GUI branches on reason\" is unimplementable.\n\n**ORDERING / OWNERSHIP CONSTRAINT.** t3 and t5 BOTH write `packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/src/index.ts` and rebuild the same `dist/` → running them in parallel risks a lost update. Recommended split that keeps t3/t4/t5 fully parallel with zero file overlap: t3 owns ALL of `packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/**` (including the line-30 array + its test + dist rebuild); t5 owns `packages/mpd-roles-plugin/**` + the shared governance tests + the live readonly-spawn proof; t4 owns `packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/**`. Alternative: keep t5 on both files but make t5 depend on t3.\n\nMinor confirmations (no action needed): `wmDir(\"\")` = library root is CONFIRMED (54-65) and the D5 rule (`raw === sanitizeName(raw) && raw !== \"\"`) already rejects `\"\"` and `\"   \"`, so the explicit root guard is belt-and-braces — keep it. §D's archive path in a SUCCESS body may be absolute (the no-absolute-path rule is scoped to error bodies); tests should assert `existsSync(body.archived)`. §E's sync-block rule needs `renameSync`/`rmSync`/`lstatSync` added to the line-13 `node:fs` import — no `node:fs/promises`, no `await` between gate and mutation, and `try/finally` around the spawn counter (an uncaught throw would otherwise block mutations forever). §H spawn/reflect-vs-rename: both bodies are synchronous inside an async fn → no in-process interleaving; the residual risk is a SECOND dsh process on the same HOME (document in t10, do not fix). §E(b) sees only teams in the CURRENT workspace while the library is cross-project → documented residual limitation.\n\nNothing else is waiting on me; t3/t4/t5 unlock now."}],"source":{"kind":"plugin","plugin":"dsh-agent-teams"},"role":"user","id":"ef2fb1cc-b52f-4994-af82-fc27bfdaa1ff"},"surfaceOp":"append"}
