ontract file → covered by the file amendment.\n\nThe contract file is the cheapest, most durable channel (all members read it). So: put A1–A7 there as \"§L Amendments from the t1 validation (binding)\". I already fixed §G (A1) and §J (some of A3-A5 partially). Now let me add §L with A2-A7 properly, and also fix the \"six stale teams\" → seven (A6) in §E.\n\nThen send 2-3 messages: Senior Engineer (t3: A2/A3/A4/A5/A6/A7 + ownership), Reviewer (t6: A6 test recipes), and maybe Deep Worker (t5: ownership boundary per the ordering constraint — t5 owns roles + governance tests; t3 owns the workmate line — already agreed, so just confirm the shared-test ownership).\n\nHmm, the ordering constraint: t3 and t5 both write mpd-workmate-plugin/src/index.ts and rebuild its dist. My task descriptions already carved out: t5's outOfScope includes packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/**, and t3 owns line 30. So the conflict is already prevented by my design — good (the Planner's \"alternative\" isn't needed). I'll confirm that in the message to both.\n\n\"the shared governance tests\" — Planner says t5 should own \"the shared governance tests\". Which tests? Perhaps a test asserting both arrays are equal (the A2 drift guard). Hmm: if the drift-guard test lives where? A test asserting equality of the two arrays must import both plugins — could live in either package. Since t3 owns the workmate plugin and t5 owns roles, and the guard spans both... Assign it to t5 (roles plugin test dir) or create it in... Let me decide: the equality guard belongs in `packages/mpd-roles-plugin/test/roles.test.ts` (t5's file, which already asserts the write-tool coverage) — it can import the workmate plugin's exported READONLY_DENY. Currently the workmate's READONLY_DENY is NOT exported (const, not exported) — t3 must export it. So: t3 exports it; t5 asserts equality. That's a cross-task interface, worth stating explicitly in messages to both.\n\nHmm, is that over-engineering? A2 alignment + one equality test. It's cheap and kills a real drift class. Let me do it:\n- t3: change workmate READONLY_DENY to the 7-name list and EXPORT it (`export const READONLY_DENY`).\n- t5: add to roles.test.ts an assertion that both exported arrays are equal.\n\nLet me now do:\n1. Contract edits: §L amendments (A2-A7) + fix \"six\" → \"seven\" in §E + note the ownership boundary.\n2. Messages: Senior Engineer (t3), Deep Worker (t5), Junior Engineer (t4 — small addition: parity test), Reviewer (t6 — A6 recipes).\n\nHmm, that's 4 messages again. Junior Engineer: I already sent the 5 checkpoints; the new bit is \"add zh/en key parity assertion\" (which I mentioned: \"assertions in the offline harness\") plus \"client.js is loaded by the test at line 17 → rebuild required\". I already said rebuild. The parity test is minor but explicit. I'll fold it into a short message? Or rely on the contract §L entry (A7) which includes it. The Junior Engineer re-reads the contract... Actually the contract is canonical and I'll add A7 there. To limit wake-ups, I'll message only t3 and t5 (the two whose *code content* changes beyond what I already sent: A2/A3/A4/A5 for t3; A2 ownership + export for t5) and the Reviewer (t6 test recipes, high value). Skip Junior Engineer (its guidance is already complete; §L A7 covers the rest, and it reads the contract).\n\nHmm, but does the Junior Engineer re-read the contract before starting? Its task description says \"Read first: .mpd/plans/...\". Yes it will.\n\nOK. Let me write the contract §L now. Then 3 messages (Senior Engineer, Deep Worker, Reviewer).\n\nLet me draft §L concisely but completely:\n\n## L. Amendments from the t1 validation (binding, replace any earlier wording)\n\nA1 (already folded into §G) — per-file deny arrays; remove ONLY the two dead names; test/roles.test.ts:106-112 must be rewritten in the same change; both dists rebuilt.\n\nA2 — DENY-LIST PARITY (captain decision: ALIGN). Both arrays ship the same seven names: write, edit, mpd_hashline_edit, bash, mcp__ast_grep__rewrite, mcp__ast_grep__scan, mcp__lsp__rename. Rationale: the workmate array previously lacked bash/MCP, so a readonly workmate could still write through a shell — masked today only because every readonly spawn throws; the fix un-masks it. t3 exports the workmate array; t5 adds a test asserting both exported arrays are equal (drift guard).\n\nA3 — FS PATHS KEY ON THE DIRECTORY, never meta.name. ensureInstance returns {meta, dir, key} with key = sanitizeName(name); every write uses key; listInstances returns the DIRECTORY name; get/read/list normalize to the key; writeIndexEntry takes the key. Test: dir alice with meta.json saying \"stale\" → list shows alice, reflect writes alice/, index key alice.\n\nA4 — renamedFrom must be in readMeta's whitelist or it is silently dropped on the next reflect: add `renamedFrom?: string[]` to Meta + readMeta (default []), append the previous key on rename (dedupe, cap 10), carry through init/reflect/rename writes. Test: rename → reflect → renamedFrom survives and get() exposes it.\n\nA5 — note self-reference rule: rewrite ONLY a LEADING `<baseName>-based workmate \"<oldKey>\".` prefix; if the note does not start with that prefix (custom note from init), leave the bytes untouched (keeps autoNote's prev.startsWith(prefix) dedupe correct). Test both branches.\n\nA6 — seven non-archived team records, not six, each carrying all 11 roster member names. Therefore blocked keys are: architect, researcher, planner, explorer, reviewer, plan-reviewer, vision-analyst, lead, deep-worker, senior-engineer, junior-engineer. Happy-path renames must avoid those keys (use oracle-1 → oracle-2); the 409 in-use path is deterministic in this workspace by renaming to `architect` — use that instead of fabricating team state. Archive exclusion holds by construction (archive/ has no team.json; retired-members.json is a file).\n\nA7 — artifacts and i18n gates: sidebar-tab.test.mjs loads the COMMITTED client.js (line 17), so t4 must rebuild before the suite means anything; bundle rows point at dist/index.js (patch 149/174) so t3/t5 must rebuild dist; no zh/en key-parity test exists → t4 adds `Object.keys(zh).sort() === Object.keys(en).sort()`; request() must carry reason/blocking.\n\nResiduals to DOCUMENT (t10/t11), not fix: §E(b) sees only teams of the CURRENT workspace while the library is cross-project; a second dsh process on the same HOME can still race a mutation (in-process gate cannot see it); `wmDir(\"\")` guard is belt-and-braces given D5.\n\nOwnership/serialization: t3 owns ALL of packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/** (incl. line 30 + its test + dist rebuild); t5 owns packages/mpd-roles-plugin/** (incl. roles.test.ts and the parity guard); t4 owns packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/**. Zero file overlap → t3/t4/t5 stay fully parallel. No additional dependency is needed.\n\nAlso fix §E's \"six stale\" → \"seven\".\n\nLet me implement: edit §E text (\"six stale non-archived team records\" → \"seven ... (see A6)\") and append §L.\n\nThen messages.\n\nLet me do the edits."},{"type":"text","text":"Planner's validation independently reproduced my §G correction and confirmed it against the harness source — plus it surfaced **one decision only I can make** (A2) and four implementation-shaping amendments. Amending the binding contract immediately:"},{"type":"tool-call","id":"call_00_DMfog3bW18uI9dgVKBuP4298","name":"edit","arguments":"{\"file_path\": \"/root/dshProj/my-power-dsh/.mpd/plans/workmate-rename-delete-contract.md\", \"new_string\": \"- The refusal message MUST name the blocking team id and member, because this repo currently holds **seven** stale\\n  non-archived team records under `.mpd/team/` (not six — corrected by the t1 validation), each carrying all 11\\n  roster member names; without the names the refusal is not actionable. See §L A6 for the exact blocked keys and for\\n  the deterministic test recipe.\", \"old_string\": \"- The refusal message MUST name the blocking team id and member, because this repo currently holds six stale\\n  non-archived team records under `.mpd/team/` whose members are named Planner/Researcher/Architect…; without the\\n  names the refusal is not actionable.\"}"}],"source":{"kind":"model","provider":"deepseek-official","model":"deepseek-v4-flash"},"id":"40149aa3-a35b-4510-9bfa-934508317fa7"},"usage":{"inputTokens":2075,"outputTokens":3002,"totalTokens":259413,"cacheReadTokens":254336,"reasoningTokens":2716}},"sourceEventSeqs":[[134341,137310]],"surfaceOp":"append"}
evidence/agent-teams/scheduler-wakeup-fix/2026-09-10T10-16-23Z/captain-session-f333e946.jsonl:5483:{"type":"user/message","seq":137317,"time":1789029890232,"data":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Agent 0939898a-8f09-4084-835a-0901f2f255b8 sent a message:"},{"type":"text","text":"Planner / t1 (requirements r1) — COMPLETE, verdict=pass. Self-contained result:\n\nSCOPE: validated `.mpd/plans/workmate-rename-delete-contract.md` against the real tree and the live harness. Read-only role respected: no file created, edited or deleted.\n\nBASELINE (pre-change, ALL FOUR offline gates GREEN): `bun test packages` → 218 tests/28 files, 215 pass, 3 skip, 0 fail; `bun run typecheck` exit 0; `node scripts/verify-vendor.mjs` PASS; `bun run test:qa` all self-tests passed. HEAD `f697088` + 33 porcelain entries identical before AND after my work. Real HOME library holds exactly one instance `oracle-1` (ideal rename subject: not a roster name, readonly base).\n\nLIVE REPRODUCTION (my own): `mpd_role_spawn {role:\"oracle\"}` →\n`Error: tools.restrict() names unknown global tools \"str_replace_editor\", \"apply_patch\"; known global tools: … bash … edit … mpd_hashline_edit … mcp__ast_grep__rewrite, mcp__ast_grep__scan, … mcp__lsp__rename … read … write …`\nHarness code `dsh-tools/lib/index.js:2802-2804` validates the WHOLE list, so one dead name aborts every read-only spawn (fail-fast, no child created). Decisive: the other 7 names of the roles list are all restrictable live; neither dead name is registered by any composed bundle/preset row.\n\nFINDINGS (7 amendments + 1 constraint; full text already sent to the captain):\n- A1 BLOCKING: §G's \"leaving exactly [write, edit, mpd_hashline_edit]\" is right only for the WORKMATE array. For the ROLES array the correct fix removes ONLY `str_replace_editor` + `apply_patch`, leaving the 7-name list (write, edit, mpd_hashline_edit, bash, mcp__ast_grep__rewrite, mcp__ast_grep__scan, mcp__lsp__rename). A literal reading deletes live write bypasses (security regression) and fails `roles.test.ts:105-112`, which asserts the 9 names and passes today.\n- A2 must-decide (recommend align): the workmate array lacks bash + the 3 MCP write tools; the fix un-masks that hole. Recommend both arrays carry the same 7 names + an equality test.\n- A3: fs paths must key on the DIRECTORY, not `meta.name` (`listInstances` line 181, reflect 326-335) — otherwise reflect can resurrect the old dir after a rename.\n- A4: `readMeta` is a whitelist → `renamedFrom` would be silently dropped on the next reflect.\n- A5: decided rule for §F's note.md tension — rewrite only the leading identity prefix, custom notes stay byte-identical.\n- A6: 7 non-archived team records (not 6); blocked keys are the 11 roster member names; the 409 in-use path is free here via renaming to `architect`.\n- A7: t4 must rebuild `packages/mpd-bundle-plugin/client.js` (the test loads the artifact) and add the missing zh/en key-parity test; `request()` drops `reason`/`blocking`, so §D's GUI branch is unimplementable without extending it.\n- ORDERING CONSTRAINT: t3 and t5 both write `packages/mpd-workmate-plugin/src/index.ts` + its dist → keep them parallel only if t3 owns all of that package and t5 owns `mpd-roles-plugin` + tests + the live proof (or make t5 depend on t3).\n\nNEXT: paste A1–A7 into the contract file / relay to t3/t4/t5 before they start, and apply the ownership split. Nothing else is waiting on me; t3/t4/t5 are now unlocked."}],"source":{"kind":"agent-message","form":"relay","senderSessionId":"0939898a-8f09-4084-835a-0901f2f255b8"},"role":"user","id":"1f68db88-ea50-40a6-9277-5c101c56cbd7"},"surfaceOp":"append"}
